window.BANK = [{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"both","q":"A homeowner hired a flooring company to supply oak planks for $6,500 and to lay them for $900. The planks were flawless, but the installer left gaps between the boards. The homeowner sued for breach of contract in a state that applies the predominant purpose test. Select the option that best describes which source of law governs the dispute:","o":["The common law, because the gravamen of the complaint is the faulty installation.","The UCC, because the contract as a whole is predominantly a sale of goods.","The UCC, because the flooring company is a merchant that deals in goods of the kind.","The common law, because planks fixed to a house are land and not goods."],"a":1,"w":"The predominant purpose test asks what the deal is mainly about, and here nearly all of the money is for the planks. So the UCC governs the whole contract, even the installation part. Pointing to the faulty installation is the gravamen of the complaint test, which this state does not use.","id":"d1-1"},{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"both","q":"Which of the following statements is least accurate about the sources of contract law?","o":["Article 2 of the UCC is a statute enacted in every state except Louisiana.","Where the UCC is silent on a point, the common law fills the gap.","The Restatement (Second) of Contracts is persuasive authority that courts often follow.","Article 2 of the UCC applies only when at least one party to the sale is a merchant."],"a":3,"w":"Article 2 covers every sale of goods, between anyone, even two neighbours selling a bike. Being a merchant only matters for a few special rules inside the UCC. The other three statements are accurate: the UCC is a binding statute, the common law fills its gaps, and the Restatement persuades but does not bind.","id":"d1-2"},{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"ucc","q":"Which of the following is NOT a \"good\" under UCC § 2-105?","o":["Shares of stock in a family corporation.","A printing press specially manufactured to the buyer's design.","A crop of soybeans still growing in the seller's field.","The unborn calf of a dairy cow."],"a":0,"w":"Goods are things that are movable when they are identified to the contract. Shares and other investment securities are left out of that definition. The custom press is the tempting choice, but specially manufactured goods are still goods, and so are growing crops and the unborn young of animals.","id":"d1-3"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following is the most accurate definition of consideration?","o":["The careful thought a party gives to a deal before deciding to enter it.","A detriment suffered by a promisee who reasonably relied on a promise.","A performance or return promise that is bargained for, sought by the promisor and given in exchange.","An exchange of roughly equal value that a court finds fair to both sides."],"a":2,"w":"Under Restatement § 71, consideration is a performance or a return promise that is bargained for. Each side's piece is the price of the other's. Detriment suffered in reliance describes promissory estoppel, which is a different doctrine, and courts do not ask whether the exchange was equal or fair.","id":"d1-4"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"A mechanic driving home saw a stranded motorist, a man of 30, and towed his car forty miles to town without being asked and without charge. A week later the motorist's mother, who had known nothing of the breakdown, wrote to the mechanic: \"For what you did for my son, I will pay you $300.\" She never paid. If the mechanic sues the mother:","o":["The mechanic will win, because the mother had a moral obligation to pay for her son's rescue.","The mechanic will win, because he relied on the mother's promise when he towed the car.","The mother will win, because the towing was finished before she promised and so was not bargained for.","The mother will win, because a promise to pay for a benefit to another person is void."],"a":2,"w":"The tow was already done when the mother made her promise, so it could not be the price of that promise. Past consideration is no consideration, which is the lesson of Mills and Wyman. A moral obligation alone does not make a contract, and the mechanic could not have relied on a promise that did not exist yet.","id":"d1-5"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"In which of the following is a court most likely to find consideration?","o":["A grandmother promises her grandson $2,000 if he stays off social media until graduation, and he does.","A manager promises a retiring clerk a $5,000 bonus \"in recognition of your twenty years of service.\"","A woman tells her neighbor, \"Come by my garage on Sunday and you can have my old bicycle.\"","A brother promises to drive his sister to the airport, and she then books an early flight."],"a":0,"w":"The grandson gave up something he had a legal right to do because his grandmother asked for it, and forbearance that is bargained for is consideration, as in Hamer and Sidway. The bonus for twenty years of service looks valuable, but that work was already done, so it is past consideration. The bicycle is a gift with a condition, and the airport ride is a gratuitous promise.","id":"d1-6"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"At a colleague's retirement party, Dale complained loudly about his vintage motorcycle and told a coworker, \"It's yours for $2,000.\" The coworker asked whether the helmet was included. Dale said yes, wrote \"I sell my motorcycle and helmet to Priya for $2,000\" on a card, signed it and handed it over. Priya said, \"Deal,\" and signed it too. The next morning Priya brought $2,000 in cash. Dale refused, explaining that he had been joking the whole time and never meant to sell. Nothing in his manner at the party suggested a joke. Which best describes the legal situation?","o":["There is no contract, because Dale did not subjectively intend to sell the motorcycle.","There is no contract, because a statement made at a social gathering is a gratuitous promise.","There is a contract, because Priya relied on the card by withdrawing the cash.","There is a contract, because under the objective theory of assent Dale's words and conduct showed a serious offer."],"a":3,"w":"The law looks at what a person shows, not at what he secretly thinks. Dale negotiated a term, wrote the deal down and signed it, so a reasonable person would take him as serious, just as in Lucy and Zehmer. His private joke does not count, and there is no need to reach for reliance, because there was an offer, an acceptance and a price.","id":"d1-7"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"A furniture store mailed a flyer reading: \"Spring clearance! Leather recliners from $199. Great selection, while supplies last.\" A customer arrived with $199 in cash and demanded a leather recliner. The cheapest recliner left in the store was priced at $450, and the store refused to sell it for less. If the customer sues:","o":["The customer will win, because the flyer was an offer that she accepted by tendering the price.","The store will win, because the flyer was an invitation to deal and not a clear, definite offer.","The store will win, because the offeror is master of the offer and may change the price at will.","The customer will win, because a merchant's advertisement is a firm offer that cannot be revoked."],"a":1,"w":"An advertisement is usually only an invitation to deal. It becomes an offer only when it is clear, definite and leaves nothing to negotiate, like the one fur stole for one dollar in Lefkowitz. This flyer names no particular recliner and no quantity, so there was no offer for the customer to accept. Master of the offer is the wrong reason, because that rule is about who may accept an offer and how.","id":"d1-8"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following is least likely to be treated as an offer?","o":["A poster promising \"$200 to whoever returns my lost terrier, Biscuit.\"","A signed letter of intent listing a price but stating it is \"subject to a final written agreement.\"","A signed note reading, \"I will sell you my canoe for $350. Tell me by Sunday.\"","An advertisement reading, \"Saturday 8 a.m.: one 55-inch television, $50, first person through the door.\""],"a":1,"w":"An offer shows willingness to deal so that the other person's yes will close the bargain. Words like \"subject to a final written agreement\" tell the reader that the parties are still negotiating, which was the point of Empro and Ball-Co. The television advertisement is tempting because advertisements are usually not offers, but this one is clear, definite and leaves nothing to negotiate.","id":"d1-9"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"On June 1 a landowner sent a prospective buyer a signed letter offering to sell a vacant parcel for $90,000. The letter added, \"This offer will stay open until June 30.\" The buyer paid nothing for that assurance. On June 20 the landowner telephoned the buyer and said, \"I've changed my mind. The offer is withdrawn.\" On June 25 the buyer hand-delivered a signed letter accepting the offer at $90,000. Which best describes the result?","o":["There is a contract, because the landowner promised to keep the offer open until June 30.","There is a contract, because the revocation of a written offer must itself be in writing.","There is no contract, because the offer had already lapsed after a reasonable time.","There is no contract, because the promise to hold the offer open was gratuitous and the offer was revoked first."],"a":3,"w":"A promise to keep an offer open binds only if the offeree gives consideration for it. Nothing was paid here, so the landowner was free to revoke at any time before acceptance, and the phone call on June 20 did that. That is the rule from Dickinson and Dodds. The offer did not lapse, because it named its own deadline, so lapse of time is the wrong reason.","id":"d1-10"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"Same facts as the previous question: the landowner's June 1 letter offered the parcel for $90,000 and said the offer would stay open until June 30, the landowner telephoned on June 20 to withdraw it, and the buyer delivered a signed acceptance on June 25. This time, however, the buyer had paid the landowner $200 on June 1 in exchange for the promise to keep the offer open. Which best describes the result?","o":["There is no contract, because the offeror is master of the offer and may revoke before acceptance.","There is no contract, because $200 is inadequate consideration for tying up a $90,000 parcel.","There is a contract, because the $200 created an option contract and the attempted revocation had no effect.","There is a contract, because the buyer relied on the deadline and reliance takes the place of acceptance."],"a":2,"w":"This time the buyer bought the time. Consideration given for a promise to hold an offer open creates an option contract, so the offer could not be revoked before June 30 and the June 25 acceptance formed a contract. The $200 is enough, because courts do not weigh the adequacy of consideration.","id":"d1-11"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"On March 1 a seller mailed a buyer an offer to sell a piano. The offer said nothing about how or when acceptance had to be made. On March 4 the seller mailed a revocation, which the buyer received on March 6. On March 5 the buyer mailed a properly addressed acceptance, which the seller received on March 8. Which analysis is most accurate?","o":["A contract was formed on March 5, because an acceptance is effective when sent and the revocation had not yet arrived.","No contract was formed, because the revocation was sent on March 4, before the acceptance was sent.","A contract was formed on March 8, because an acceptance is effective when the offeror receives it.","No contract was formed, because under the mirror image rule letters that cross in the mail cancel out."],"a":0,"w":"Under the mailbox rule in Restatement § 63, a properly addressed acceptance takes effect the moment it is sent. A revocation works only when it is received. The buyer's acceptance went out on March 5, a day before the revocation landed, so the contract was formed that day. Looking at the date the revocation was mailed is the classic mistake.","id":"d1-12"},{"t":"Promissory Estoppel","k":"cl","q":"An uncle, proud that his nephew had been admitted to culinary school, told him, \"I am giving you $15,000 toward tuition. No strings attached.\" The uncle knew the nephew could not attend without the money. Trusting the promise, the nephew quit his job and signed a nonrefundable enrollment agreement. The uncle then refused to pay. Which best describes the most likely outcome?","o":["The nephew will win, because quitting his job was consideration for the uncle's promise.","The uncle will win, because a gratuitous promise cannot be enforced on any theory.","The uncle will win, because the nephew never communicated an acceptance of the promise.","The nephew will win, because the uncle should have expected the reliance and the nephew relied to his detriment."],"a":3,"w":"The uncle asked for nothing in return, so this is a gratuitous promise with no consideration. Quitting the job was not the price of the promise, so calling it consideration is the wrong reason. But under Restatement § 90 a promise that the promisor should expect to cause reliance, and that does cause it, can be enforced to avoid injustice, as in Ricketts and Scothorn.","id":"d1-13"},{"t":"Promissory Estoppel","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following facts is most likely to defeat a claim of promissory estoppel under Restatement § 90?","o":["The defendant said only, \"We are thinking about bringing you on next year.\"","The defendant's promise was spoken and was never put in writing.","The defendant received no benefit from what the plaintiff did.","The plaintiff gave nothing and promised nothing in return for the defendant's promise."],"a":0,"w":"Promissory estoppel starts with a real promise, and reliance on it must be reasonable. \"We are thinking about it\" is a maybe, not a promise. The lack of anything given in return is the tempting choice, but that only shows there was no consideration, which is exactly when § 90 is used as plan B.","id":"d1-14"},{"t":"Promissory Estoppel","k":"cl","q":"A developer told a caterer, \"The cafe space in my new building is yours. Start getting ready to open.\" Both understood that they would not be bound to a lease until one was signed, and none ever was. Trusting the developer, the caterer paid $9,000 for custom counters that fit only that space and have no resale value, and $3,000 for permits. The caterer had projected a first-year profit of $40,000. The developer then leased the space to someone else. If a court finds for the caterer on promissory estoppel and awards the regular measure of damages for that claim, the caterer will recover:","o":["$52,000","$40,000","$12,000","$28,000"],"a":2,"w":"The regular measure for promissory estoppel is reliance damages: what you lost by trusting the promise, not what you hoped to gain. The caterer spent $9,000 on counters and $3,000 on permits, which comes to $12,000. The $40,000 of projected profit is expectation, the benefit of the bargain, and as in the Aretha Franklin case that is not what § 90 usually gives.","id":"d1-15"},{"t":"Boilerplate","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following is the least accurate statement about standard form contracts under Restatement § 211?","o":["A party who signs a standard form adopts its terms whether or not the party read them.","A standard form is interpreted the same way for every customer, whatever each one knew of its terms.","A term is not part of the deal if the drafter had reason to believe the signer would refuse it if aware of it.","A contract of adhesion is void because the customer had no chance to negotiate its terms."],"a":3,"w":"A contract of adhesion is a take-it-or-leave-it form, and it is not void just for that reason. Under § 211 the signer adopts the terms, read or not, and the form means the same thing for every customer. The only escape in that section is a term the drafter had reason to believe you would never have accepted.","id":"d2-1"},{"t":"Boilerplate","k":"cl","q":"A rafting company based in Colorado sells trips to customers across the country. The booking form each customer receives before paying states that any lawsuit against the company must be filed in Colorado. A customer from Ohio, who saw the clause but had no chance to negotiate it, is injured on a trip and sues in Ohio. What is the most likely result?","o":["The clause will be enforced, because a reasonable forum clause with notice binds even without negotiation.","The clause will be enforced, because under the last shot rule the company's form controls.","The clause will not be enforced, because forum clauses bind only sophisticated parties who bargained over them.","The clause will not be enforced, because a contract of adhesion is voidable at the customer's election."],"a":0,"w":"This follows Carnival Cruise Lines and Shute. A forum selection clause in a form contract is enforced when it is reasonable and the customer had notice, because a company with customers everywhere has a real interest in one home court. The tempting wrong answer comes from The Bremen, where the parties were bargaining equals, but Carnival extended the rule to forms nobody negotiated.","id":"d2-2"},{"t":"Online Contracts","k":"cl","q":"What is the least accurate statement about assent to online terms?","o":["Clickwrap is usually enforced because clicking \"I agree\" is an outward manifestation of assent.","Browsewrap binds a user who knew, or reasonably should have known, that the terms existed.","A user who clicked \"I agree\" can escape the terms by proving that he never actually read them.","Inquiry notice, without actual knowledge of the terms, can be enough to bind a user."],"a":2,"w":"Under the duty to read, a user who clicks on something that is plainly an agreement is bound whether or not he read it. Assent is judged from the outside, so the question is notice plus an act showing agreement, not what the user actually studied. The other three statements are accurate descriptions of clickwrap, browsewrap and inquiry notice.","id":"d2-3"},{"t":"Online Contracts","k":"cl","q":"Priya downloaded a language-learning app. To register, she typed in her email address and pressed a button marked \"Start learning.\" Nothing on the registration screen mentioned any terms. A link labelled \"Legal\" appeared in small grey type at the very bottom of the app's home page, reachable only by scrolling past several screens of lessons. The page behind that link contained an arbitration clause. When Priya later sued the company over unauthorized charges, the company moved to compel arbitration. Which of the following statements is most accurate?","o":["The clause is binding, because pressing \"Start learning\" made the terms an enforceable clickwrap.","The clause is likely not binding, because it sits in a browsewrap and Priya had neither actual nor inquiry notice.","The clause is binding, because the duty to read applies whether or not a user could find the terms.","The clause is not binding, because the statute of frauds requires arbitration agreements to be signed by hand."],"a":1,"w":"This is browsewrap, and it looks like Specht and Netscape. Under the objective theory of assent, pressing a button shows a wish to use the app, not agreement to terms that a reasonable user would never have known were there. It is not clickwrap, because nothing near the button told Priya that pressing it meant accepting any terms.","id":"d2-4"},{"t":"Battle of the Forms","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following best describes the common law's last shot rule?","o":["The final form sent before performance is a counteroffer, which the other party accepts by performing.","The first form sent controls, because the offeror is the master of the offer.","Conflicting terms in the two forms cancel each other, and default rules fill the gap.","Whichever party's lawyer speaks last at trial chooses the terms that govern."],"a":0,"w":"At common law an acceptance had to be the mirror image of the offer, so a reply with different terms was a rejection and a counteroffer. When the other side went ahead and performed, that performance accepted the counteroffer, so the last form sent won. Terms cancelling each other out is the knockout rule, which belongs to the UCC, not the common law.","id":"d2-5"},{"t":"Battle of the Forms","k":"ucc","q":"A hotel chain sent a lamp manufacturer a purchase order for 400 desk lamps. The order said nothing about warranties. The manufacturer's acknowledgment agreed to the order and added, \"Seller disclaims all implied warranties.\" It did not make acceptance conditional on that term, and the hotel chain never responded to it. The lamps were delivered and later overheated. Both parties are merchants. Is the disclaimer part of the contract?","o":["Yes, because under the last shot rule the acknowledgment was the final form sent before shipment.","Yes, because between merchants every additional term becomes part of the contract unless the offeror objects.","No, because the acknowledgment was a counteroffer, so no contract was ever formed.","No, because a contract formed on the forms and a warranty disclaimer materially alters it."],"a":3,"w":"Under section 2-207, the acknowledgment was a definite acceptance, so there is a contract on the forms. Between merchants an additional term usually slides in, but not if it materially alters the deal, and wiping out warranties is the classic material alteration. The offeror's silence is not enough to let it in, because objection is only one of three ways an additional term is kept out.","id":"d2-6"},{"t":"Battle of the Forms","k":"ucc","q":"Buyer, a plumbing wholesaler, sent Seller a purchase order for 2,000 brass valves at $12 each. Seller's acknowledgment listed the same goods and price but stated, \"Our acceptance is expressly conditional on Buyer's assent to the terms of this form.\" One of those terms capped Seller's liability at the purchase price. Buyer never replied. Seller shipped the valves, and Buyer accepted and paid for them. Several valves later leaked and caused water damage. Both parties are merchants. Which terms govern the contract?","o":["Seller's terms, because Buyer accepted Seller's counteroffer by taking and paying for the valves.","Buyer's terms only, because the offeror is the master of the offer.","The terms on which both forms agree, plus UCC gap-fillers, because the contract arose from conduct.","No terms at all, because forms that do not match cannot produce a contract."],"a":2,"w":"Because the acknowledgment was expressly conditional on assent to its terms, it was not an acceptance, and the forms made no contract. But both sides performed, so section 2-207, subsection three, finds a contract by conduct made up of the terms the forms share plus the UCC's gap-fillers. Giving Seller its liability cap because Buyer took the goods is the common law's last shot rule, which the UCC rejects for goods.","id":"d2-7"},{"t":"Interpretation","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following best describes a course of dealing?","o":["The way the parties have carried out the very contract that is now in dispute.","The pattern of conduct between the same parties in their earlier transactions.","The regular practice of the trade or industry in which the parties operate.","The specific words the parties chose and wrote into their agreement."],"a":1,"w":"A course of dealing is how these same two parties behaved in their earlier deals together. The tempting wrong answer, how they have behaved under the present contract, is course of performance. The order of priority under § 203 is express terms first, then course of performance, then course of dealing, then trade usage.","id":"d2-8"},{"t":"Interpretation","k":"cl","q":"A bakery signed a one-year contract with a mill for \"weekly deliveries of flour.\" The contract does not name a delivery day. For the first seven months the mill delivered every Thursday, and the bakery accepted each delivery without complaint. The bakery now insists on Monday deliveries and offers proof that mills in the region customarily deliver on Mondays. How should a court resolve the dispute?","o":["For the bakery, because trade usage outranks the conduct of two individual parties.","For the mill, because course of performance under this contract outranks trade usage.","For the mill, because the Thursday deliveries are a course of dealing, which outranks express terms.","For neither party, because the parol evidence rule bars all evidence from outside the four corners."],"a":1,"w":"Seven months of Thursday deliveries accepted without complaint is a course of performance, meaning how the parties behaved under this very contract. Under § 203 that ranks above trade usage, so the regional Monday custom loses. The Thursday pattern is not a course of dealing, which means earlier contracts between the parties, and nothing ever outranks the express terms.","id":"d2-9"},{"t":"Parol Evidence Rule","k":"cl","q":"A homeowner hired a landscaper to rebuild her garden. They signed a two-page writing that set the price at $28,000 and the completion date at July 1. The writing has no merger clause, and the court finds it partially integrated. At trial the homeowner offers testimony that, the day before signing, the landscaper orally agreed that the price would be $25,000. How should the court rule on the testimony?","o":["Admit it, because a partially integrated writing may be supplemented by any earlier term.","Admit it, because an oral agreement counts as parol evidence only if it is made after signing.","Exclude it, because even a partially integrated writing discharges earlier agreements that contradict it.","Exclude it, because the statute of frauds requires every price term to appear in a signed writing."],"a":2,"w":"Under § 213, a final writing wipes out earlier agreements that contradict it, and that is true even when the writing is only partially integrated. A price of $25,000 flatly contradicts the written $28,000, so it stays out. Partial integration only opens the door to consistent additional terms, not to a different version of a term the writing already states.","id":"d2-10"},{"t":"Parol Evidence Rule","k":"cl","q":"A homeowner hired a landscaper to rebuild her garden. They signed a two-page writing that set the price at $28,000 and the completion date at July 1. The writing has no merger clause, and the court finds it partially integrated. This time the homeowner offers testimony that, the day before signing, the landscaper orally promised to give her a one-hour lesson on caring for the new plants. The writing says nothing about lessons. How should the court rule on the testimony?","o":["Exclude it, because nothing outside the four corners may be considered once a contract is signed.","Admit it, because a partially integrated writing may be supplemented by consistent additional terms.","Admit it, because contra proferentem requires every gap to be filled against the landscaper.","Exclude it, because a consistent earlier term is discharged just as a contradictory one is."],"a":1,"w":"Under § 210, a partially integrated writing is final on what it says, but consistent extra terms may still be added. The lesson does not contradict anything in the writing, so the testimony comes in. A consistent term within the writing's scope would be shut out only if the writing were completely integrated, and the court has found that it is not.","id":"d2-11"},{"t":"Parol Evidence Rule","k":"cl","q":"A homeowner hired a landscaper to rebuild her garden. They signed a two-page writing that set the price at $28,000 and the completion date at July 1. The writing has no merger clause, and the court finds it partially integrated. This time the landscaper offers testimony that, two weeks after signing, the parties orally agreed to move completion to July 15 in exchange for a $500 price reduction. The homeowner objects under the parol evidence rule. How should the court rule on the objection?","o":["Sustain it, because the later agreement contradicts the completion date stated in the writing.","Sustain it, because a partially integrated writing admits only consistent additional terms.","Overrule it, because a completion date is never an essential term of a contract.","Overrule it, because the rule reaches only agreements made before or at signing, not later modifications."],"a":3,"w":"The parol evidence rule only blocks agreements made before or at the signing. A change agreed afterwards is a modification, and the rule does not touch it. It is tempting to say the new date contradicts the writing, but contradiction matters only for earlier agreements that the final writing was meant to replace.","id":"d2-12"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following oral agreements is least likely to require a signed writing under the statute of frauds?","o":["The sale of a small vacant lot for $900.","A promise to a bank to repay a brother's loan if the brother defaults.","An agreement made today to manage a shop for six months, starting ten months from now.","A promise to employ a gardener for the rest of the gardener's life."],"a":3,"w":"The one-year rule in § 130 asks whether the deal could possibly be fully performed within a year of its making, and a job for life can be, because a life can end within a year. The shop agreement is the trap. It lasts only six months, but the clock runs from the day the contract is made, so it cannot be finished within a year. Land sales and promises to pay another person's debt need a writing whatever the amount.","id":"d2-13"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"ucc","q":"A sporting goods store orally agreed to buy 200 bicycle helmets from a supplier at $30 each. The only writing is a note signed by the supplier that reads, \"Sold to the store: 120 helmets.\" The note states no price. The supplier later refused to deliver any helmets, and the store sued. Assuming the store can prove the oral agreement, what is the largest contract it can enforce against the supplier?","o":["$3,600","$6,000","$2,400","$0"],"a":0,"w":"Under section 2-201, the writing must show a deal, be signed by the party being sued, and state a quantity, and the quantity in the writing is a ceiling. The supplier signed a note for 120 helmets, so the contract is enforceable up to 120 helmets at $30, which is $3,600. The missing price does not sink the note, because price can be proved by other evidence. The full 200 helmets cannot be enforced, because 80 of them are not in ink.","id":"d2-14"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"ucc","q":"Which of the following is NOT required for a writing to satisfy the statute of frauds for goods under UCC § 2-201(1)?","o":["The signature of the party to be charged.","A quantity term.","The price of the goods.","Some indication that a contract for sale was made."],"a":2,"w":"The UCC asks for a bare writing: it must show that a deal was made, carry the signature of the party being sued, and state a quantity. Price is not required and can be supplied by other evidence. That is the contrast with the common law, where the writing must set out the essential terms.","id":"d2-15"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"both","q":"Which of the following is NOT a factor that points toward a finding of unconscionability?","o":["A price far above the value of what was sold","A harsh term hidden deep in the fine print","Terms negotiated at length by two parties who each had a lawyer","A written contract in a language one party cannot read"],"a":2,"w":"Unconscionability looks for an unfair process and unfair terms: an inflated price, a language barrier, unequal bargaining power, unfair surprise and hidden fine print. A deal hammered out by two advised parties has a rough balance of bargaining power, which points the other way. Remember that a hard bargain between equals still binds.","id":"d3-1"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"both","q":"A home user downloads photo-editing software and clicks \"I agree\" to a 40-page license without reading it. Page 37 states that the user owes the company $8,000 upon the tenth use of the program. After the user's tenth use, the company sues for $8,000. What is the most likely result?","o":["The clause will not be enforced, because a buried term that causes unfair surprise is unconscionable.","The clause will be enforced, because clicking \"I agree\" to a clickwrap makes every term binding.","The whole license is void, because failing to read a contract is fraud in the inception.","The clause will be enforced, because under the last shot rule the company's terms control."],"a":0,"w":"Under Restatement § 208 a court may refuse to enforce a term that was unconscionable when the contract was made, and it can strike that one term and leave the rest standing. An $8,000 fee buried on page 37 is unfair surprise joined to a wildly one-sided term. Clicking \"I agree\" does show assent to the license in general, but assent does not rescue a term that shocks the conscience.","id":"d3-2"},{"t":"Mistake","k":"cl","q":"Which is the most accurate definition of a \"mistake\" under Restatement § 151?","o":["A prediction about future events that turns out to be wrong","A false statement by one party on which the other party relies","A poor judgment about what the subject matter will be worth later","A belief that is not in accord with the facts"],"a":3,"w":"Section 151 defines a mistake as a belief that is not in accord with the facts, meaning the facts as they stand when the contract is made. A wrong prediction or a bad guess about future value is only the ordinary risk of doing business. A false statement by the other side belongs to misrepresentation, which is a different doctrine.","id":"d3-3"},{"t":"Mistake","k":"cl","q":"A collector sells a framed letter to a buyer for $60. Both rely on an auction house's written appraisal describing it as a printed facsimile. In fact it is a handwritten original worth $90,000. The contract says nothing about who bears the risk of authenticity. The collector learns the truth before delivery and sues to rescind. What is the most likely result?","o":["The collector will lose, because a unilateral mistake requires proof that the buyer knew of the error.","The collector will win, because both parties were mistaken about a basic assumption that materially affected the exchange.","The collector will win, because a price that low makes the contract unconscionable.","The collector will lose, because a mistake about what an item is worth is never a ground for rescission."],"a":1,"w":"Under § 152, a mistake of both parties about a basic assumption that materially affects the exchange makes the contract voidable by the party who is hurt, unless that party bore the risk. Both sides believed the letter was a facsimile, and nothing put the risk on the collector. The tempting wrong answer calls this a mistake about value, but as with the cow in Sherwood and Walker, the mistake goes to the very nature of the thing sold.","id":"d3-4"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"Which is the most accurate statement of the difference between fraud in the inception and fraud in the inducement?","o":["Inception makes the contract voidable at the victim's option; inducement makes the contract void from the start.","Inception deceives the victim about what is being signed, so it is void; inducement persuades by lies, so it is voidable.","Inception requires scienter on the part of the speaker; inducement can be committed by an honest speaker.","Inception concerns lies told before the contract is signed; inducement concerns lies told after performance begins."],"a":1,"w":"In fraud in the inception, the victim does not even know what she is agreeing to, so there is no real assent and the contract is void, as in Duick and Toyota. In fraud in the inducement, she knows it is a contract but was talked into it by lies, so it is voidable at her choice. The answer that swaps void and voidable is the classic trap.","id":"d3-5"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"A buyer looking at a used fishing boat asks the seller whether the hull has ever been damaged. The seller answers, \"Never.\" The seller honestly believes this, because the previous owner patched a crack below the waterline and never told him. A month after the sale the patch fails. As the best case for excuse, the buyer would assert:","o":["Fraud in the inducement","Fraud in the inception","Unconscionability","Misrepresentation"],"a":3,"w":"The seller made a false statement of fact about something important, and the buyer justifiably relied on it. Under § 164 a material misrepresentation makes the contract voidable even when the speaker was honest. Fraud is the tempting answer, but fraud needs scienter, meaning the speaker knew the statement was false or did not care, and this seller believed what he said.","id":"d3-6"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"A buyer looking at a used fishing boat asks the seller whether the hull has ever been damaged. The seller answers, \"Never.\" In fact the seller cracked the hull on a rock last spring and patched it himself. A month after the sale the patch fails. The buyer's best ground for excuse is:","o":["Fraud in the inducement, because the seller knowingly made a false statement of fact to bring about the sale.","Fraud in the inception, because the buyer did not know the true condition of the boat.","Non-disclosure, because the seller kept silent about the crack.","Duress, because the buyer had no reasonable alternative to trusting the seller."],"a":0,"w":"Now the seller knew the statement was false and made it to get the sale, and the buyer relied on it. That is fraud in the inducement, and the contract is voidable by the buyer. It is not non-disclosure, because the seller did not stay silent; he answered the question with a lie. It is not fraud in the inception, because the buyer knew perfectly well he was signing a contract to buy a boat.","id":"d3-7"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"A buyer looks over a used fishing boat and never asks about the hull. The seller knows he cracked the hull last spring and patched it below the waterline, where an inspection would not reveal it. The seller says nothing about it. A month after the sale the patch fails. The buyer's best argument for excuse is:","o":["Misrepresentation, because the seller made a false statement of material fact.","Fraud in the inception, because the buyer was deceived about the nature of the document.","Non-disclosure, because the seller stayed silent about a known condition the buyer could not discover.","Impracticability, because the failed patch made the boat impossible to use."],"a":2,"w":"This time nobody said anything false, so the buyer cannot point to a misrepresentation. The argument has to be non-disclosure: the seller knew of a serious condition that no inspection would reveal and kept quiet. The usual rule is caveat emptor, but as in Stambovsky and Ackley, silence can be enough when the seller knows of a condition the buyer has no way to find.","id":"d3-8"},{"t":"Impossibility and Frustration","k":"cl","q":"A stonemason contracts to restore a particular antique fountain in a homeowner's garden for $12,000. Before work begins, lightning strikes the fountain and shatters it beyond repair. The contract has no force majeure clause. The homeowner sues the stonemason for failing to perform. What is the most likely result?","o":["The stonemason will lose, because pacta sunt servanda: a promise must be kept whatever happens.","The stonemason will win on mutual mistake, because neither party expected the lightning.","The stonemason will win, because lightning is an Act of God and a force majeure clause is implied in every contract.","The stonemason will win, because the fountain's continued existence was a basic assumption and its loss without fault made performance impracticable."],"a":3,"w":"Under § 261, a duty is discharged when an event after the contract is made, through no fault of the party, makes performance impracticable, and the contract assumed that event would not happen. The fountain is like the music hall in Taylor and Caldwell: the thing essential to performance is gone. Mutual mistake does not fit, because a mistake concerns the facts at signing, and the fountain was whole on that day. A force majeure clause exists only if the parties write one.","id":"d3-9"},{"t":"Impossibility and Frustration","k":"cl","q":"Which statement best describes the difference between impracticability under § 261 and frustration of purpose under § 265?","o":["In impracticability the performance itself is blocked; in frustration performance is still possible, but the principal purpose both parties understood has been destroyed.","Impracticability excuses a party whose costs have risen; frustration excuses a party whose profits have fallen.","Impracticability applies to events before the contract is signed; frustration applies to events after it is signed.","Impracticability requires a force majeure clause; frustration applies only when the contract has none."],"a":0,"w":"Impracticability says, I cannot do it. Frustration says, I can do it, but the whole point is gone, as when the coronation procession was cancelled in Krell and Henry. Neither one is about money: a rise in cost or a fall in profit is the ordinary risk of a fixed price deal and excuses nobody.","id":"d3-10"},{"t":"Capacity","k":"cl","q":"What is the least accurate statement about contracts made by minors?","o":["A minor's contract is voidable, not void.","A minor must pay the reasonable value of necessaries such as food and medical care.","Either party may cancel the contract once the minor's age comes to light.","In many states an emancipated minor who lives independently can be bound like an adult."],"a":2,"w":"Under § 14 a minor's contract is voidable at the minor's option only. The adult gets no such choice, and the minor can hold the adult to the deal. The other three statements are accurate: voidable does not mean void, and necessaries and emancipation are recognised exceptions to the infancy defence.","id":"d3-11"},{"t":"Capacity","k":"cl","q":"A 17-year-old buys a used motorcycle from an adult dealer for $3,000. She pays $2,200 and owes the rest in installments. Three months later the engine seizes through no fault of hers, and the motorcycle is now worth $1,200. Still 17, she returns it and disaffirms. The dealer claims $1,800 for depreciation. Under the general rule, how much must the dealer refund?","o":["$3,000","$2,200","$1,200","$400"],"a":1,"w":"A minor who disaffirms hands back whatever is left and recovers what she paid, which is $2,200. As in Halbman and Lemke, she owes nothing for use or depreciation, because charging her would enforce the contract by the back door. The $400 figure comes from subtracting the $1,800 of depreciation, which is exactly the deduction the rule forbids. A motorcycle, like a car, is not a necessary.","id":"d3-12"},{"t":"Duress","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following is NOT an improper threat under Restatement § 176?","o":["A threat to file a lawsuit, made in good faith, to collect a debt the creditor honestly believes is owed","A threat to commit a tort against the other party","A threat to have the other party criminally prosecuted unless he signs","A bad faith threat to break an existing contract with the other party unless she agrees to pay more"],"a":0,"w":"Section 176 treats a threat as improper when it threatens a crime or a tort, criminal prosecution, a lawsuit brought in bad faith, or a breach of the duty of good faith under an existing contract. Telling someone to pay what you honestly believe is owed or you will sue is a lawful use of the courts. The word that matters is bad faith; without it, a threat to sue is just hard bargaining.","id":"d3-13"},{"t":"Duress","k":"cl","q":"A printer agrees to print 10,000 festival programs for an organizer for $6,000. A month before the festival, the printer announces that it will stop work unless the organizer agrees to pay $9,000. The organizer knows that three other printers in town could finish the job on time for about $6,500, but she agrees in order to avoid the hassle and pays the $9,000. After the festival she sues to recover the extra $3,000, claiming economic duress. On the duress claim, what is the most likely result?","o":["The organizer will win, because the threat to breach was improper, and an improper threat is all that duress requires.","The organizer will win, because the printer's demand was unfair persuasion amounting to undue influence.","The printer will win, because duress is limited to threats of physical harm and cannot be claimed by a business.","The printer will win, because the organizer had a reasonable alternative: hire another printer and sue for damages."],"a":3,"w":"Under § 175, duress needs an improper threat and no reasonable alternative. The printer's threat may well have been improper, but the organizer could have hired one of three other printers and sued for the difference, so she had a way out. Compare Austin Instrument and Loral, where the buyer called ten suppliers and none could deliver in time. An improper threat alone is not enough.","id":"d3-14"},{"t":"Undue Influence","k":"cl","q":"Walter, an 82-year-old widower, is mentally sharp. Two days after he comes home from the hospital, his long-time financial adviser and the adviser's business partner arrive at his house at 10 p.m. They urge him to sign, that night, a contract selling his lake cabin to the adviser for half its value. They say the offer ends at midnight and that there is no need to bother his daughter or his lawyer. Nobody threatens him. Walter signs. In court, Walter's best defense is:","o":["Duress, because an improper threat left him no reasonable alternative.","Lack of capacity, because his age and recent illness make the contract voidable.","Undue influence, because a trusted adviser used unfair persuasion: multiple persuaders, an unusual time and place, and no chance to get advice.","Fraud in the inception, because he signed without reading the contract closely."],"a":2,"w":"Under § 177, undue influence is unfair persuasion of a person who is justified in trusting the persuader. Walter trusted his adviser, and the warning signs from Odorizzi are all here: two persuaders, late at night in his home, insistence on signing at once, and no time for a lawyer. Duress fails because nobody made a threat. Capacity fails because Walter was mentally sharp, and as the Burren case shows, competence does not defeat undue influence.","id":"d3-15"},{"t":"Damages","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three interests that contract remedies protect under Restatement §344?","o":["The expectation interest, meaning the benefit of the bargain.","The reliance interest, meaning the out-of-pocket loss caused by trusting the promise.","The punitive interest, meaning a sum set to deter future breaches.","The restitution interest, meaning a benefit conferred on the other party."],"a":2,"w":"Section 344 protects expectation, reliance and restitution. Contract damages compensate the injured party, and section 355 rules out punitive damages unless the breach is also a tort. Reliance may sound like the odd one out because it is the fallback measure, but it is one of the three.","id":"d4-1"},{"t":"Damages","k":"cl","q":"A landscaper agreed to build a stone patio for a homeowner for $30,000. The landscaper expected the whole job to cost $22,000 in labour and materials. After the landscaper had spent $9,000 on the job, and before the homeowner had paid anything, the homeowner repudiated the contract. The landscaper stopped work at once and could not reuse any of the materials. How much would the landscaper collect under the expectation (benefit of the bargain) measure?","o":["$30,000","$8,000","$9,000","$17,000"],"a":3,"w":"Expectation puts the landscaper where full performance would have. That is the $8,000 profit plus the $9,000 already spent, which is $17,000. You reach the same figure by taking the $30,000 price and subtracting the $13,000 of cost the landscaper avoided by stopping. The full $30,000 ignores the costs saved, and $9,000 alone is only the reliance measure.","id":"d4-2"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"cl","q":"A caterer left a portable generator with a repair shop, which promised to return it by Friday. The caterer did not say what the generator was used for. The shop returned it the following Wednesday. On Saturday, when the caterer called to complain, she told the shop that she was missing a weekend festival at which she would have cleared $6,000. She sues the shop for the $6,000. What is the most likely outcome?","o":["The caterer will recover, because expectation damages cover every loss that the breach in fact caused.","The caterer will not recover, because the shop had no reason to foresee the festival loss when the contract was made.","The caterer will not recover, because lost profits can never be proved with enough certainty.","The caterer will recover, because the shop learned of the festival before it returned the generator."],"a":1,"w":"Under section 351 and Hadley and Baxendale, the breaching party pays only for loss it had reason to foresee when the contract was made. The shop was told nothing about the festival at that time. Telling the shop on Saturday, after the deal was done, comes too late, because foreseeability is judged at the moment of contracting and not at the moment of breach.","id":"d4-3"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"cl","q":"Why will a court refuse to enforce a stipulated damages clause that it classifies as a penalty?","o":["Because contract remedies exist to compensate the injured party, not to punish the party in breach.","Because parties have no freedom to agree on their remedies in advance.","Because the doctrine of avoidable consequences forbids fixing damages before a breach occurs.","Because stipulated damages are a form of caveat emptor that binds only merchants."],"a":0,"w":"Parties may fix damages in advance, but under section 356 only at a reasonable forecast of the loss. An unreasonably large sum is a penalty, and contract law compensates. It does not punish. It is wrong to say parties cannot agree on remedies at all, because a fair forecast is enforced.","id":"d4-4"},{"t":"Specific Performance","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following is NOT a factor a court weighs under Restatement §360 in deciding whether money damages are an adequate remedy?","o":["How hard it is to prove the amount of the loss with reasonable certainty.","How hard it is to buy a suitable substitute with the money awarded.","How likely it is that an award of damages could actually be collected.","How deliberate the breach was."],"a":3,"w":"Section 360 asks three things about money: is the loss hard to value, is a substitute hard to purchase, and are damages unlikely to be collected. The breaching party's state of mind is not on that list. Specific performance turns on whether money is inadequate, not on how badly the other side behaved.","id":"d4-5"},{"t":"Specific Performance","k":"cl","q":"A regional theatre company signed a well-known stage director to direct its three autumn productions for a fee of $90,000. The contract contained no promise by the director not to work for anyone else. Two weeks before rehearsals were to begin, the director told the company she was leaving to direct a film instead. The company, which had already sold season tickets on the strength of her name, asks a court to order her to direct the productions as promised. What is the most likely outcome?","o":["The court will grant specific performance, because the director's talent is unique and no substitute exists.","The court will grant specific performance, because the company relied on her promise when it sold tickets.","The court will deny specific performance, because a court will not compel and supervise personal services.","The court will deny specific performance, because the contract was to last less than one year."],"a":2,"w":"Personal service is the classic case where specific performance is refused. A court cannot supervise the quality of the work, and forcing a person to labour looks like involuntary servitude. Her talent may well be unique, but uniqueness alone does not get an order to perform, so the company is left to claim money.","id":"d4-6"},{"t":"Substantial Performance","k":"cl","q":"Which is the most accurate statement of the common law doctrine of substantial performance?","o":["A party whose performance departs from the contract in any respect may recover nothing.","A party who substantially performs may recover the contract price, less damages for the defect.","A party who substantially performs has not breached, so nothing is deducted from the price.","A buyer of goods must accept any tender that substantially conforms to the contract."],"a":1,"w":"Under Jacob and Youngs and Kent, a party who substantially performs gets paid, minus damages for the defect. The tempting mistake is to think substantial performance excuses the breach. It does not: the shortfall is still a breach and the builder still pays for it. And for goods the rule is perfect tender, not substantial performance.","id":"d4-7"},{"t":"Substantial Performance","k":"cl","q":"A contractor built a swimming pool for a homeowner for $80,000. The contract called for a depth of eight feet at the deep end. Through an innocent measuring error the pool is three inches shallower. It is fully usable for everything the homeowner wanted, and its market value is $500 lower than promised. Rebuilding to the specified depth would cost $45,000. The homeowner refuses to pay the final $20,000 instalment. What is the most likely outcome?","o":["The contractor recovers nothing, because under the perfect tender rule any nonconformity justifies refusing payment.","The contractor recovers the full $20,000, because an innocent mistake is not a breach.","The contractor recovers nothing, because the $45,000 cost of completion exceeds the unpaid balance.","The contractor recovers $19,500, because the cost to rebuild is clearly out of proportion to the loss in value."],"a":3,"w":"The contractor substantially performed: the slip was innocent, the defect is petty and the pool serves its purpose. Section 348 normally awards the cost to fix, but when that cost is clearly out of proportion to the loss in value, the owner gets only the loss in value, here $500. The perfect tender rule is a UCC rule for goods and has no place in a construction contract.","id":"d4-8"},{"t":"Perfect Tender","k":"ucc","q":"A café owner ordered 60 identical oak chairs from a furniture maker, to arrive in a single shipment on May 1. The shipment arrived on May 1. Inspecting it that day, the owner found that four chairs were stained a noticeably darker shade than the contract specified. She immediately notified the furniture maker that she was rejecting the entire shipment. Was the rejection rightful?","o":["Yes, because goods that fail to conform to the contract in any respect may be rejected in whole.","No, because the furniture maker substantially performed and the owner's remedy is a price deduction.","No, because a buyer may reject only the nonconforming units and must keep the rest.","Yes, but only because the darker stain substantially impaired the value of the whole shipment."],"a":0,"w":"This is a single delivery of goods, so section 2-601 applies. If the goods fail to conform in any respect, the buyer may reject all, accept all, or accept some commercial units and reject the rest. Substantial performance is the common law rule for services and construction, and substantial impairment is not required before acceptance.","id":"d4-9"},{"t":"Perfect Tender","k":"ucc","q":"Same facts as the previous question, except that the contract called for the 60 chairs to arrive in three separate lots of 20, on May 1, June 1 and July 1. The four darker chairs arrived in the first lot, and the furniture maker promptly offered to swap them for correctly stained chairs within the week. May the café owner reject the first lot?","o":["Yes, because the perfect tender rule applies to every delivery of goods.","No, because under an instalment contract a lot may be rejected only if the defect substantially impairs its value and cannot be cured.","No, because the owner accepted the chairs by inspecting them.","Yes, because the furniture maker is a merchant and is held to a stricter standard."],"a":1,"w":"Separate deliveries make this an instalment contract, and section 2-612 switches perfect tender off. The buyer may reject an instalment only if the defect substantially impairs its value and cannot be cured. Four chairs that the seller offers to swap within the week do not meet that test. Inspecting goods is not accepting them; a buyer accepts by failing to reject after a fair chance to inspect.","id":"d4-10"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"A brewery contracted to buy 500 pounds of hops from a grower at $8 per pound. The grower failed to deliver. On the day the brewery learned of the breach, the market price of hops was $10 per pound. Acting promptly and in good faith, the brewery bought 500 pounds from another grower at $11 per pound. Ignoring incidental and consequential damages, how much may the brewery recover under UCC §2-712?","o":["$5,500","$1,000","$1,500","$4,000"],"a":2,"w":"A buyer who covers in good faith and without unreasonable delay recovers the cover price minus the contract price. That is $5,500 minus $4,000, which is $1,500. The $1,000 figure is the market price formula in section 2-713, and that is for a buyer who does not cover. Cover has to be reasonable, not the cheapest deal available.","id":"d4-11"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"Under the UCC, which of the following best describes a lost volume seller?","o":["A seller with enough supply to have made both the original sale and the later resale, so that the breach cost it one sale's profit.","A seller that resold the goods for less than the contract price and claims the difference.","A seller that seeks cover by buying substitute goods from another supplier.","A seller whose custom goods cannot be resold to anyone and that sues for the full price."],"a":0,"w":"A lost volume seller could have supplied both buyers, so reselling the goods at the same price does not make it whole. Under section 2-708, subsection two, and Neri and Retail Marine, it recovers its lost profit. The contract price minus resale price formula is the ordinary resale remedy in section 2-706, and cover is a buyer's remedy, not a seller's.","id":"d4-12"},{"t":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","k":"cl","q":"Which is the least accurate statement about third-party beneficiaries?","o":["An intended beneficiary may enforce the promise even though it signed nothing and gave no consideration.","The promisor may raise against the beneficiary the defences it would have had against the promisee.","Anyone who would have been better off had the contract been performed may sue to enforce it.","A creditor of the promisee, whom the promisor has agreed to pay, is a classic intended beneficiary."],"a":2,"w":"Benefit is not the test. Intent is. Under section 302 only an intended beneficiary can sue, and someone who merely happens to gain is incidental and gets nothing. The other statements are accurate: Lawrence and Fox let a creditor sue without privity or consideration, and under section 309 the promisor keeps its defences.","id":"d4-13"},{"t":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","k":"cl","q":"A developer hired a builder to construct a hotel on the developer's land by June 1. The owner of a restaurant next door, expecting hotel guests, hired extra staff for the summer. The builder abandoned the job and the hotel never opened. The restaurant owner sues the builder for breach of the construction contract. What is the most likely outcome?","o":["The restaurant owner will win, because performance of the contract would have benefited her.","The restaurant owner will win, because she relied on the contract when she hired extra staff.","The restaurant owner will lose, because she is an incidental beneficiary whom the parties did not intend to benefit.","The restaurant owner will lose, because a person who is not in privity can never enforce a contract."],"a":2,"w":"The developer and the builder made their deal for themselves, not for the neighbour. She would have gained from it, but a happy side effect makes her only an incidental beneficiary, and incidental beneficiaries cannot sue. The privity answer reaches the right result for the wrong reason, because intended beneficiaries can enforce contracts they never signed.","id":"d4-14"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"cl","q":"A homeowner hired a roofing company to replace her roof with standard asphalt shingles for $18,000. The roofing company, which was overbooked, handed the job to a second roofer without asking the homeowner. The second roofer did the work badly, and the roof leaks. The homeowner sues the original roofing company. What is the most likely outcome?","o":["The company is liable, because a party that delegates a duty stays responsible unless the other side agrees to a novation.","The company is not liable, because it validly delegated the duty and the second roofer took its place.","The company is liable, because duties under a service contract can never be delegated.","The company is not liable, because the homeowner's only claim is as a beneficiary against the second roofer."],"a":0,"w":"Under section 318 a duty like ordinary roofing can be delegated, but delegating does not get you off the hook. The delegator stays liable if the delegate performs badly. Only a novation, a new agreement in which the homeowner consents to the swap, would release the original company. It is wrong to say service duties can never be delegated; the bar is for duties that depend on personal skill or trust.","id":"d4-15"},{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"both","q":"Which of the following statements about Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code is least accurate?","o":["It is a statute, enacted by the legislature in every state except Louisiana.","It covers a private individual who sells a used bicycle to a neighbour.","It applies only where at least one party to the sale is a merchant.","Where it is silent on a point, the common law fills the gap."],"a":2,"w":"Article 2 covers every sale of goods, between anyone, so a merchant is not needed to bring a deal inside it. Merchant status matters only for a handful of special rules under section 2-104, such as firm offers. The other three statements are all true: the code is a binding statute, it reaches casual sellers, and the common law fills its gaps.","id":"b1"},{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"both","q":"A marina owner buys a boat lift from a dock company. The invoice lists $14,000 for the lift and $1,000 for installing it on the owner's dock. Three months later the lift's motor burns out because of a manufacturing defect. In a jurisdiction that uses the predominant purpose test, which best describes the law that governs the owner's claim?","o":["The UCC, because the contract taken as a whole is mainly a sale of goods.","The UCC, because the dock company is a merchant that deals in goods of the kind.","The common law, because the contract included a service that the company performed.","The common law, because the lift became part of the land once it was installed."],"a":0,"w":"The predominant purpose test asks what the deal is mainly about, and here nearly all of the money was for the lift itself. So the whole contract goes under the UCC. The tempting answer points to the company being a merchant, but merchant status does not decide which rulebook applies. It only switches on a few special rules once you are already inside the UCC.","id":"b2"},{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"both","q":"Same facts as the boat lift purchase ($14,000 for the lift, $1,000 for installation), except that the lift works perfectly. During installation, the company's crew carelessly cracked two of the owner's dock pilings. In a jurisdiction that uses the gravamen of the complaint test, the owner's claim for the cracked pilings is governed by:","o":["The UCC, because most of the contract price was paid for goods.","The UCC, because any contract that includes goods falls under Article 2.","The common law, because the Restatement is the binding statute for service contracts.","The common law, because the complaint is about the installation service and not the goods."],"a":3,"w":"The gravamen test asks what the complaint is really about. Here the lift is fine and the grievance is careless installation work, which is a service, so the common law governs. Pointing to the share of the price paid for goods is the predominant purpose test, which this jurisdiction does not use. And the Restatement is never a binding statute. It only persuades.","id":"b3"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the functions of consideration identified by Lon Fuller?","o":["The evidentiary function: an exchange is proof that a deal was really made.","The equalising function: an exchange ensures that each side receives roughly equal value.","The cautionary function: having to pay a price makes a person think before promising.","The channelling function: it gives people a recognised route to a binding promise."],"a":1,"w":"Fuller's three functions are evidence, caution and channel. Nothing in the doctrine tries to make the two sides of a deal equal in value. In fact, under Restatement section 79, courts refuse to weigh the adequacy of consideration at all, so a peppercorn will do if it was truly bargained for.","id":"b4"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"During a storm, a boat mechanic sees a stranger's sailboat breaking loose from its mooring and, without being asked, spends two hours securing it. The next day the grateful owner says, \"For what you did last night, I will pay you $800.\" The owner later refuses to pay. If the mechanic sues on the promise, the most likely result is:","o":["The mechanic wins, because the owner received a real and valuable benefit.","The mechanic wins, because he relied on the promise when he secured the boat.","The mechanic loses, because a promise to pay for services must be in writing.","The mechanic loses, because the work was finished before the promise and was not bargained for."],"a":3,"w":"Consideration under Restatement section 71 has to be the price of the promise, and the mechanic's work was already done before the owner promised anything. Past consideration is no consideration, which is the lesson of Mills and Wyman. The benefit to the owner is real, but a benefit alone is not a bargain. Reliance does not help either, because the mechanic acted before the promise existed.","id":"b5"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"After an afternoon of genuine haggling, a retired teacher signs a note agreeing to sell her late husband's coin collection to a neighbour for $900. A week later an appraiser tells her it is worth about $9,000. She refuses to deliver, arguing that $900 is not consideration for a $9,000 collection. Nothing suggests fraud, duress or incapacity. Which best describes the result?","o":["Her promise is enforceable, because courts do not weigh the adequacy of a bargained-for exchange.","Her promise is unenforceable, because consideration must be roughly equal in value to the promise.","Her promise is enforceable only if the neighbour relied on it to his detriment.","Her promise is unenforceable, because so lopsided a price makes the promise gratuitous."],"a":0,"w":"Under Restatement section 79, a court asks only whether there was a real bargain, not whether the price was a good one. The two of them haggled and settled on $900, so that sum was the price of her promise. A low price does not turn a sale into a gift, and reliance is beside the point because consideration is already present.","id":"b6"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"In which of the following is a court most likely to find consideration?","o":["A grandfather promises his grandson $5,000 for his birthday \"because you have always made me proud.\"","After a friend has helped her move house, Dana promises to pay him $200 for his trouble.","An aunt promises her adult niece $3,000 if the niece gives up skydiving for a year, and the niece does so.","A man tells a colleague, \"If you come by my office, you can have my old desk chair.\""],"a":2,"w":"The niece gave up something she had a legal right to do, because her aunt asked for it in exchange for the money. That is forbearance, and under Hamer and Sidway forbearance is consideration. The birthday promise is a gratuitous promise, the moving help was already done before the promise, and walking to the office is only the way to collect a gift.","id":"b7"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following most accurately states the objective theory of assent?","o":["A party is bound only by the terms that he or she privately intended to accept.","A party is bound by what his or her words and conduct would lead a reasonable person to understand.","A contract is formed only when the agreement is recorded in an objective form, such as a signed writing.","A court decides, from the outside, whether the price the parties agreed on was fair."],"a":1,"w":"The law looks at what you show, not at what you secretly think. If a reasonable person would take your words and conduct as agreement, you are bound, which is why the secret joke in Lucy and Zehmer did not save the seller. The objective theory has nothing to do with requiring a writing or with checking whether the price was fair.","id":"b8"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"A hotel hires a muralist to paint its lobby for $12,000. The signed contract adds that the muralist \"will also paint the ballroom next year, for a fee to be agreed upon by the parties at that time.\" The lobby is finished and paid for. The next year the two cannot agree on a fee, and the muralist sues to enforce the ballroom term. The most likely result is:","o":["The muralist wins, and the court will set a reasonable fee as of the time for performance.","The muralist wins, because the hotel was under a pre-existing duty to hire her.","The muralist loses, because an agreement to agree on an essential term such as price is too uncertain to enforce.","The muralist loses, because the offeror is master of the offer and the hotel never named a fee."],"a":2,"w":"Painting a mural is a service, so the common law applies, and Restatement section 33 requires terms certain enough for a court to enforce. An agreement to agree on price fails that test, as in Joseph Martin Delicatessen and Schumacher. The tempting answer has the court fill in a reasonable price, but that is the UCC's open price rule for sales of goods, and it does not reach a service contract.","id":"b9"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following is least likely to be treated as an offer?","o":["A store flyer reading, \"Saturday, 9 a.m.: one floor-model treadmill, worth $700, for $25. First come, first served.\"","A newspaper advertisement reading, \"Winter coats at great prices this weekend, from $89.\"","A poster reading, \"$300 will be paid to whoever returns my lost drone to this address.\"","A signed letter reading, \"I will sell you my tractor for $4,000. Let me know by Friday.\""],"a":1,"w":"An ordinary advertisement is only an invitation to deal, because it does not say how many coats there are, which coats, or who gets them. The treadmill flyer is different. Like the advertisement in Lefkowitz, it is clear, definite, and leaves nothing to negotiate. The reward poster and the letter each show a willingness to be bound as soon as the other person does what is asked.","id":"b10"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"On June 1, a landscaper mails a homeowner a written offer to redo her garden for $7,000. The offer says nothing about how it must be accepted. On June 2 the landscaper mails a letter revoking the offer. On June 3, before that letter arrives, the homeowner mails a properly addressed acceptance. The revocation reaches her on June 4, and her acceptance reaches the landscaper on June 5. Which best describes the result?","o":["There is no contract, because the revocation was sent before the acceptance was sent.","There is no contract, because the acceptance arrived after the revocation had taken effect.","A contract was formed on June 5, when the landscaper received the acceptance.","A contract was formed on June 3, because an acceptance works when sent and a revocation only when received."],"a":3,"w":"Under the mailbox rule in Restatement section 63, a properly addressed acceptance takes effect the moment it is sent. A revocation is different: it counts only when it arrives. The homeowner posted her acceptance on June 3, a day before the revocation reached her, so the contract was already made. The date the landscaper posted his revocation does not matter.","id":"b11"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"A farmer tells a painter, \"I don't want your promise. If you paint my whole barn red by the end of the month, I will pay you $4,000.\" The painter buys paint and rents scaffolding, but has not yet touched the barn when the farmer tells him the offer is withdrawn. Under the modern Restatement approach, which best describes the result?","o":["The farmer may revoke, because merely preparing to perform does not create an option contract.","The farmer cannot revoke, because buying the paint was the beginning of performance.","The farmer may revoke, because an offer of this kind stays revocable until the whole barn is painted.","The farmer cannot revoke, because the painter's silence operated as an acceptance."],"a":0,"w":"Under Restatement section 45, an option contract arises once the offeree begins or tenders the performance the offer asked for. Buying paint and renting scaffolding is only getting ready, so the farmer was still free to revoke. Be careful with the answer that says the offer stays revocable until the job is finished. That is the old rule from Petterson and Pattberg, not the modern one.","id":"b12"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"Same facts as the barn offer (\"paint my whole barn by the end of the month and I will pay you $4,000\"), except that the painter has painted two of the barn's four walls when the farmer announces that the offer is withdrawn. Under the modern Restatement approach:","o":["The farmer may revoke, because an offer accepted by performance is accepted only when performance is complete.","The farmer may revoke, but must pay for the two finished walls in quantum meruit.","The farmer may not revoke, because beginning performance created an option contract and the painter must be allowed to finish.","The farmer may not revoke, and the painter is entitled to the $4,000 at once whether or not he finishes."],"a":2,"w":"Once the painter actually started painting, Restatement section 45 gave him an option contract, so the farmer has to let him finish. That is the same idea as Brackenbury and Hodgkin, where the couple had begun caring for the mother. The painter does not get paid immediately, though. The farmer's duty to pay the $4,000 still depends on the barn being completed as the offer required.","id":"b13"},{"t":"UCC Formation","k":"ucc","q":"For three years a brewery has bought \"all the hops it requires\" from a grower at a fixed price, taking about 400 pounds a month. When the market price of hops triples, the brewery orders 6,000 pounds for the next month, planning to resell most of it at a profit. The grower refuses to ship more than the usual amount. Which best describes the result?","o":["The grower is within its rights, because requirements must be set in good faith and cannot be unreasonably out of line with normal past amounts.","The grower is in breach, because a requirements contract obliges the seller to supply whatever quantity the buyer orders.","There was never an enforceable contract, because the agreement did not state a quantity.","There was never an enforceable contract, because the brewery's promise to buy was illusory."],"a":0,"w":"Under UCC section 2-306, a requirements contract is enforceable even though it names no fixed number, because the quantity is whatever the buyer needs in good faith. That same section sets the limit. A demand cannot be unreasonably out of line with normal past amounts, and ordering fifteen times the usual quantity to resell it is not a good faith requirement. The promise was never illusory, because the brewery bound itself to buy its actual needs from this grower.","id":"b14"},{"t":"Firm Offers","k":"ucc","q":"Which of the following is NOT required for an offer to be a firm offer under UCC § 2-205?","o":["The offeree gives consideration for the promise to hold the offer open.","The offeror is a merchant.","The offer is made in a signed writing.","The writing gives assurance that the offer will be held open."],"a":0,"w":"The whole point of section 2-205 is that a merchant's signed, written assurance makes an offer irrevocable without any consideration, for the time stated or a reasonable time, up to three months. Paying to keep an offer open is how you create an option contract at common law. The other three choices are the SAM elements: signed writing, assurance, merchant.","id":"b15"},{"t":"Promissory Estoppel","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following is the most accurate statement about the remedy for promissory estoppel under Restatement § 90?","o":["The promisee always receives the full value of the promise, exactly as if consideration had been given.","The remedy may be limited as justice requires, and is often measured by what the promisee lost in reliance.","The promisee recovers only the benefit the promisor gained, never the promisee's own losses.","The court must order specific performance, because money damages are unavailable without consideration."],"a":1,"w":"Section 90 says the remedy may be limited as justice requires. Courts sometimes enforce the whole promise, as with the pension in Feinberg and Pfeiffer, but more often they award reliance damages, as in the Aretha Franklin case. So it is wrong to say the promisee always gets the full value of the promise. The measure looks at what the promisee lost by trusting, not at what the promisor gained.","id":"b16"},{"t":"Promissory Estoppel","k":"cl","q":"The owner of a seaside market tells a food-truck operator, \"The corner pitch is yours for next summer. Go ahead and get ready.\" They exchange drafts of a licence but never settle the fee, and both understand that nothing is final until a licence is signed. As the owner expected, the operator spends $6,000 on a local permit and on refitting her truck to suit the pitch. The owner then gives the pitch to someone else. The operator proves she would have earned $25,000 in profit over the summer. If a court applies promissory estoppel and awards reliance damages, how much will she recover?","o":["$0","$6,000","$25,000","$31,000"],"a":1,"w":"There was no contract, because the fee was never settled and neither side meant to be bound before signing. But the owner made a promise, expected her to act on it, and she did, at a cost of $6,000. Reliance damages put her back where she was before the promise, so she recovers the $6,000 she spent. The $25,000 in hoped-for profit is the expectation measure, which is what a contract claim would give.","id":"b17"},{"t":"Boilerplate","k":"cl","q":"Under Restatement § 211, a term in a signed standard form is NOT part of the agreement when:","o":["The signer did not read the form before signing it.","The form was offered on a take-it-or-leave-it basis, with no chance to negotiate.","The drafter has reason to believe the signer would not have assented had the signer known the term was there.","The term appears in the fine print and not among the terms the signer shopped on."],"a":2,"w":"Section 211 starts from the rule that signing a standard form adopts its terms, read or not. The one safety valve is in subsection three: a term drops out if the drafter has reason to believe the signer would refuse to sign if they knew it was there. Failing to read is not a defence, and being a contract of adhesion is not enough by itself. Treating fine print as non-binding is Rakoff's proposal, not the Restatement rule.","id":"b18"},{"t":"Online Contracts","k":"cl","q":"A graphic designer visits a website offering a free font pack and clicks a large \"Download now\" button. The site's licence terms, including an arbitration clause, appear only on a part of the page that can be seen by scrolling well below the button, and nothing near the button mentions them. When the designer later sues over tracking software hidden in the download, the company moves to compel arbitration. The most likely result is:","o":["Arbitration is compelled, because every user has a duty to read the terms before downloading.","Arbitration is compelled, because the Federal Arbitration Act makes arbitration clauses binding on anyone who uses a product.","Arbitration is not compelled, because the designer never actually read the licence terms.","Arbitration is not compelled, because a reasonable user would not have known the terms existed, so the click did not show assent to them."],"a":3,"w":"This is browsewrap, and under Specht and Netscape there is no assent without reasonable notice that terms exist. Clicking a download button shows a wish for free fonts, not agreement to terms hidden further down the page. Notice that the right reason is lack of notice, not the fact that the designer did not read the terms. Failing to read terms you were plainly shown is never an excuse. And the Federal Arbitration Act enforces agreements to arbitrate, so there has to be an agreement first.","id":"b19"},{"t":"Online Contracts","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following is the least accurate statement about online contracts?","o":["Clickwrap, which makes the user tick a box or click \"I agree,\" is usually enforced.","Browsewrap is enforced only if the user knew, or reasonably should have known, that the terms were there.","A user is on inquiry notice when a reasonable user would have realised that terms existed.","A user is bound by a website's terms only if the user actually read them."],"a":3,"w":"Online assent is judged from the outside, just like any other assent. Courts do not ask whether the user actually read the terms, because almost nobody does. They ask whether a reasonable user would have noticed that terms existed, and whether the user did something, like clicking, that shows agreement. That is why the user in Fteja and Facebook was bound without reading a word.","id":"b20"},{"t":"Battle of the Forms","k":"ucc","q":"A restaurant chain sends a manufacturer a purchase order for 40 commercial ovens, stating \"Delivery within 14 days.\" The manufacturer replies with an acknowledgment that agrees on the ovens, the price and the quantity, is not made conditional on assent to its own terms, and states \"Delivery within 90 days.\" Both parties are merchants. Nothing more is said, and a dispute arises over the delivery date. Under the knockout rule followed by most courts, delivery is due:","o":["Within 90 days, because under the last shot rule the final form sent controls.","Within 14 days, because the offeror is master of the offer.","Within a reasonable time, because the conflicting terms cancel each other and a UCC gap-filler applies.","At no time, because the forms were not mirror images and so no contract was formed."],"a":2,"w":"Under UCC section 2-207, the acknowledgment was a definite expression of acceptance, so there is a contract even though the forms disagree. The two delivery terms flatly contradict each other, which makes them different terms. Under the knockout rule both drop out, and the code's gap-filler supplies a reasonable time. The last shot rule and the mirror image rule are common law ideas that section 2-207 replaced for sales of goods.","id":"b21"},{"t":"Interpretation","k":"both","q":"A quarry agrees in writing to deliver gravel to a road builder \"by the load\" for one year, at a fixed price per load. The contract does not say how big a load is. For the first eight deliveries under this contract, the quarry sent 20-ton loads, and the builder accepted and paid for them without objection. In the parties' two earlier contracts, loads were 22 tons. In the gravel trade generally, a load is 25 tons. Absent other evidence, a court will most likely read \"load\" to mean:","o":["20 tons, because course of performance under this very contract outranks the other sources.","22 tons, because course of dealing shows the parties' settled understanding over time.","25 tons, because trade usage is the most objective evidence of meaning.","Whichever size favours the builder, because under contra proferentem ambiguity is always read against the seller."],"a":0,"w":"Restatement section 203 sets the pecking order: express terms, then course of performance, then course of dealing, then trade usage. The express term does not define a load, so the next rung controls, and that is how the parties have actually behaved under this contract. Eight accepted deliveries of 20 tons beat both their earlier deals and the custom of the trade. Reading a term against the drafter is a last resort, and it runs against whoever wrote the term, not automatically against the seller.","id":"b22"},{"t":"Parol Evidence Rule","k":"cl","q":"Even where a writing is completely integrated, evidence of the parties' earlier negotiations may be admitted for each of the following purposes EXCEPT:","o":["To show that the agreement was induced by fraud, duress or mistake.","To show whether the writing was in fact intended to be an integrated agreement.","To explain the meaning of an ambiguous term in the writing.","To add a consistent extra term on a subject that the writing covers."],"a":3,"w":"Under Restatement section 213, a completely integrated writing wipes out earlier agreements within its scope, even ones that are consistent with it. Consistent additional terms can come in only when the integration is partial. The other three purposes are the exceptions in section 214: invalidity such as fraud, whether the writing is integrated at all, and the meaning of its words.","id":"b23"},{"t":"Parol Evidence Rule","k":"cl","q":"A retailer signs a written contract to buy \"all bicycles located in the seller's warehouse\" for $90,000. The contract has a merger clause stating that the writing is the parties' entire agreement. The seller later offers testimony that, during negotiations, both sides understood \"bicycles\" to mean pedal bicycles only and not the electric bicycles also stored there. The court finds the word clear on its face. In a plain-meaning jurisdiction that keeps to the four corners of the writing, the court will most likely:","o":["Admit the testimony, because words have no fixed meaning apart from their context.","Exclude the testimony, because a clear and complete writing speaks for itself.","Admit the testimony, because the parol evidence rule does not apply to written contracts that contain a merger clause.","Exclude the testimony, because the statute of frauds requires every term of the deal to be in writing."],"a":1,"w":"A four corners court, like New York's in W W W Associates and Giancontieri, will not look at negotiations when the words are clear and the writing is complete. The tempting answer, that words have no fixed meaning apart from context, is the California approach from Pacific Gas and Electric, which lets evidence in if the language is reasonably susceptible to the meaning offered. A merger clause makes the writing more closed, not less. And the statute of frauds is about whether a writing exists, not about what evidence explains it.","id":"b24"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"ucc","q":"A carpenter and a café owner orally agree that the carpenter will sell the owner 300 standard bar stools from his stock at $50 each. Before they part, the carpenter writes on a page of his receipt pad, \"Sold to the café, 120 stools,\" and signs it. The owner signs nothing, and no money or stools change hands. The carpenter later refuses to deliver and raises the statute of frauds. The café owner can enforce the agreement for at most:","o":["No stools, because the writing does not state the price.","No stools, because the café owner never signed the writing.","120 stools, because the quantity stated in the signed writing is a ceiling.","300 stools, because once a signed writing exists, oral evidence may prove the true quantity."],"a":2,"w":"Under UCC section 2-201, a sale of goods for $500 or more needs a writing signed by the party to be charged that shows a deal and states a quantity. The carpenter is the one being sued and he signed, so the owner's missing signature does not matter, and a missing price is no problem either. But the contract is enforceable only up to the quantity written down. The page says 120, so 120 is the most the owner can enforce, whatever was agreed out loud.","id":"b25"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"cl","q":"A court finds that one term of a contract was unconscionable when the contract was made. Which of the following is NOT something the court may do under the Restatement?","o":["Refuse to enforce the contract as a whole.","Enforce the rest of the contract without the unconscionable term.","Limit how the term applies so as to avoid an unconscionable result.","Award punitive damages against the party who drafted the term."],"a":3,"w":"Section 208 gives the court three tools: refuse the whole contract, strike the bad term, or limit that term so the result is fair. Unconscionability is a shield against enforcement. It is not a source of punishment, and contract law does not award punitive damages for a harsh term.","id":"b26"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"cl","q":"A regional grocery chain, represented by its lawyers, spends two months negotiating a cleaning services contract. In return for a lower price, the chain agrees that the cleaning company may cancel on 5 days' notice while the chain must give 180 days' notice. A year later the chain wants out quickly and asks a court to strike the notice term as unconscionable. What is the most likely result?","o":["The chain will win, because a term this one-sided shocks the conscience no matter how it was agreed.","The chain will lose, because a term bargained for by a sophisticated, advised party shows no unfair process; a hard bargain still binds.","The chain will win, because unconscionability is judged when enforcement is sought, and the term now causes hardship.","The chain will lose, because unconscionability protects only consumers who buy goods under the UCC."],"a":1,"w":"Most courts want both parts: an unfair process and unfair terms. Here the term is lopsided, but the chain had lawyers, real choice and two months to negotiate, and it got a lower price in exchange. Hindsight does not help either, because section 208 judges the deal as of the moment it was made. A merely bad bargain still binds.","id":"b27"},{"t":"Mistake","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following is most clearly a \"mistake\" as the Restatement defines that word?","o":["Both parties to a share sale expect the company's stock price to rise next year, and it falls.","A buyer of a sports car decides a week later that she paid too much.","Both parties to a land sale believe the parcel contains 40 acres, when it contains 25.","A contractor assumes that lumber prices will stay low for the length of the job."],"a":2,"w":"Under section 151 a mistake is a belief that is not in accord with the facts as they exist when the contract is made. The size of the parcel is a present fact, and both parties had it wrong. A prediction about future prices that turns out badly is not a mistake, and neither is buyer's remorse.","id":"b28"},{"t":"Mistake","k":"cl","q":"At an estate sale, a seller offers an old violin from her late uncle's attic. She tells a buyer, \"It might be something, it might be nothing. I have not had it appraised and I am not going to.\" The buyer, who also has no idea what it is, pays $500. An expert later identifies it as an eighteenth-century instrument worth $80,000. The seller sues to rescind for mistake. What is the most likely result?","o":["The seller will lose, because she knew her knowledge was limited and sold anyway, so she bore the risk of the mistake.","The seller will win, because both parties were mistaken about a basic assumption, as with the cow thought to be barren.","The seller will win, because this is a unilateral mistake and enforcing so uneven a sale would be unconscionable.","The seller will lose, because the parol evidence rule bars proof of the violin's true value."],"a":0,"w":"Section 154 puts the risk of a mistake on a party who is aware that she does not know and goes ahead anyway. The seller said out loud that the violin might be something and chose not to find out. That is different from the barren cow case, where both sides were confident about what the animal was. Conscious ignorance is a gamble, and she lost it.","id":"b29"},{"t":"Warranties","k":"ucc","q":"An appliance dealer wants its written sales contract to exclude the implied warranty of merchantability. Which of the following would NOT be effective to do so?","o":["A bold, capitalised line stating that the goods are sold \"AS IS.\"","A bold, capitalised line stating that the goods are sold \"with all faults.\"","A conspicuous clause stating that the seller disclaims the implied warranty of merchantability.","A sentence in small print on the back stating, \"Seller makes no other promises of any kind.\""],"a":3,"w":"Under UCC section 2-316, a written disclaimer of merchantability must mention the word merchantability and must be conspicuous. The shortcut is language like as is or with all faults, which tells any buyer there are no implied warranties. A vague line buried in small print does neither, so the implied warranty survives.","id":"b30"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"The owner of a laundromat tells a prospective buyer, \"The business took in $180,000 last year.\" The owner honestly believes this, but his bookkeeper had made an error; the true figure was $110,000. The buyer, who had no way to check the figure, buys the business in reliance on it. On learning the truth, can the buyer rescind?","o":["No, because the owner did not know the statement was false, and a misrepresentation defence requires awareness of the falsehood.","Yes, because a material misrepresentation that the buyer justifiably relied on makes the contract voidable, even if it was innocent.","No, because under caveat emptor a buyer takes the risk of anything a seller says about the business.","Yes, because this is fraud in the inception, which makes the contract void from the start."],"a":1,"w":"Under section 164 a contract is voidable if assent was induced by a misrepresentation that is either fraudulent or material, and the reliance was justified. Last year's income plainly matters to a buyer, so the statement is material even though the owner was honest. Do not assume you must prove a liar. Awareness of the falsehood is needed for fraud, not for rescission.","id":"b31"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"Same facts as the laundromat sale, except that the owner knew the true figure was $110,000 and had altered the books himself to show $180,000. The buyer understood that he was signing a contract to buy the business. Which best describes the contract?","o":["It is voidable by the buyer, because this is fraud in the inducement.","It is void, because this is fraud in the inception.","It is voidable by either party, because fraud removes the mutual assent of both.","It is enforceable, because a statement about past income is sales puffery."],"a":0,"w":"The buyer knew he was signing a contract of sale. He was lied to about a fact that persuaded him to sign, and that is fraud in the inducement, which makes the contract voidable at the victim's choice. Fraud in the inception is different: there the victim is tricked about the very nature of the document, so there is no assent and the contract is void. Only the buyer holds the key here, not the owner who lied.","id":"b32"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"Ordinarily a party who simply stays silent has made no misrepresentation. In which of the following is non-disclosure most likely to be treated as an assertion?","o":["A stranger buying a used piano does not mention that she thinks the asking price is low.","A homeowner selling at arm's length gives her honest opinion that the area is \"up and coming.\"","A financial adviser buying a client's land does not mention a planned highway exit that he knows will raise its value.","A job applicant does not volunteer the lowest salary he would be willing to accept."],"a":2,"w":"Silence counts as a statement when a relationship of trust requires you to speak. A financial adviser is a fiduciary to his client, so he must disclose material facts before dealing with the client for his own benefit. Parties bargaining at arm's length, like the piano buyer and the job applicant, have no such duty to volunteer what they know.","id":"b33"},{"t":"Impossibility and Frustration","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following is the least accurate statement about excuses for non-performance?","o":["Impracticability discharges a duty when an unexpected event, not the party's fault, upsets a basic assumption of the deal.","A sharp rise in the cost of performing a fixed-price contract is ordinarily enough to excuse the performing party.","A force majeure clause is read closely, and its catch-all words reach only events similar to those listed.","Frustration of purpose applies when performance is still possible but the reason for the deal has been destroyed."],"a":1,"w":"A mere rise in cost is not an excuse. A fixed price exists precisely to put market swings on the party who agreed to it. The other three statements are accurate: section 261 covers impracticability, section 265 covers frustration, and courts read a force majeure clause word by word.","id":"b34"},{"t":"Impossibility and Frustration","k":"cl","q":"A fan club contracts with a bus company for six buses to carry its members to the championship game for $4,000, payable on the day. Both parties know the trip is only for the game. The day before, the stadium roof collapses under heavy snow and the game is cancelled. The bus company says its buses are ready and demands payment. What is the fan club's strongest ground for refusing to pay?","o":["Impossibility, because the bus company is no longer able to perform its side of the contract.","Mutual mistake, because both parties wrongly believed the game would be played.","Act of God, because a natural event automatically cancels every contract it touches.","Frustration of purpose, because the event both parties knew to be the whole point of the trip will not take place."],"a":3,"w":"The buses can still drive to the stadium, so nothing is impossible. What has gone is the reason for the trip, which both sides knew was the foundation of the contract. That is frustration of purpose under section 265, the same idea as the rooms rented to watch a procession that never happened. It is not mistake, because a mistake concerns the facts when the contract was signed, not events that come later.","id":"b35"},{"t":"Capacity","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following is the least accurate statement about contracts made by a minor?","o":["The contract is voidable at the minor's option.","The minor may disaffirm while under age or within a reasonable time after reaching majority.","The adult party may cancel the contract on learning that the other party is a minor.","A minor must pay the reasonable value of necessaries such as food, clothing and medical care."],"a":2,"w":"Under section 14 the choice belongs to the minor alone. The minor may keep the deal or disaffirm it, but the adult gets no such option and is bound unless the minor walks away. The other statements are accurate, including the exception for necessaries.","id":"b36"},{"t":"Capacity","k":"cl","q":"During a manic episode of bipolar disorder, Hal signs a contract to buy three vintage tractors from a dealer for $90,000. Hal understands exactly what he is signing and what it will cost. That morning, Hal's daughter had telephoned the dealer, explained that her father was in the middle of an episode, and asked the dealer not to sell him anything. Once the episode passes, Hal seeks to avoid the contract. What is the most likely result?","o":["The contract is voidable, because Hal could not act reasonably in the transaction and the dealer had reason to know of his condition.","The contract is enforceable, because Hal understood the nature and consequences of the transaction.","The contract is void, because a person with a mental illness has no power to contract at all.","The contract is enforceable, because no court had appointed a guardian for Hal."],"a":0,"w":"Section 15 has two routes. The older test asks only whether the person understood the deal, and Hal did. The modern test adds that a contract is voidable if illness left the person unable to act reasonably and the other party had reason to know. The daughter's call gave the dealer that knowledge, so Hal can get out.","id":"b37"},{"t":"Duress","k":"cl","q":"A hotel has a one-year contract with a laundry service at a fixed monthly rate. In month four the laundry says, \"Pay 20 percent more, or we stop collecting your linen tomorrow.\" Three other laundries in town could start the next day at about the same rate. The hotel signs the amendment anyway, pays the higher rate for months, and then sues to recover the extra, arguing economic duress. Is the duress argument likely to succeed?","o":["Yes, because any threat to break a contract is an improper threat.","Yes, because the laundry is a merchant and is held to good faith under the UCC.","No, because the hotel had a reasonable alternative: hire another laundry and sue for damages.","No, because duress requires a threat of physical harm."],"a":2,"w":"Duress under section 175 needs an improper threat that leaves the victim no reasonable alternative. A threat to breach is not automatically duress, because usually you can go elsewhere and sue, and here three other laundries were ready the next day. Economic duress is real, so a physical threat is not required, but the hotel was never stuck. Its better argument would be the pre-existing duty rule.","id":"b38"},{"t":"Undue Influence","k":"cl","q":"For ten years an accountant has managed all the finances of an elderly widow, who trusts him completely. The accountant suggests that her lake cabin is \"more trouble than it is worth\" and buys it from her for one third of its market value. The widow is mentally sharp and was not threatened. Her family later asks a court to set the sale aside. What is the most likely result?","o":["The sale stands, because the widow was mentally sharp and understood what she was selling.","The sale is voidable, because a trusted adviser who benefits from the deal is presumed to have used undue influence and must prove it was fair.","The sale is voidable for duress, because a price that low is itself an improper threat.","The sale stands, because it did not take place at an unusual time or in an unusual place."],"a":1,"w":"Under section 177, undue influence is unfair persuasion of someone who is justified in trusting the persuader to look after her welfare. When a fiduciary such as an accountant or lawyer benefits from a deal with the person who trusts him, the law presumes undue influence and he must prove the deal was fair. A sharp mind does not defeat the claim, and no threat is needed. Duress twists your arm, while undue influence bends your ear.","id":"b39"},{"t":"Damages","k":"both","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three interests that contract remedies protect?","o":["The expectation interest: the benefit of the bargain.","The restitution interest: the return of a benefit conferred on the other party.","The reliance interest: out-of-pocket loss caused by trusting the promise.","The retribution interest: punishment of the party who broke the promise."],"a":3,"w":"Section 344 names three interests: expectation, reliance and restitution. Contract damages compensate. Under section 355 there are no punitive damages for breach unless the breach is also a tort. Contract law makes you whole, and it does not make them sorry.","id":"b40"},{"t":"Damages","k":"cl","q":"A caterer agrees to cater a company's anniversary dinner for $12,000. The caterer's total cost of performing would have been $8,000. After the caterer has spent $1,500 on printed menus and custom decorations that cannot be reused or resold, the company cancels without excuse. The company has paid nothing, and the caterer could not have booked another event for that night. What are the caterer's expectation damages?","o":["$4,000","$5,500","$6,500","$12,000"],"a":1,"w":"Use the expectation formula in section 347. The caterer lost the $12,000 price, and it avoided the $6,500 of costs it had not yet spent, which leaves $5,500. You can check it another way: the $4,000 profit it expected, plus the $1,500 it had already sunk into the job. Awarding only the $4,000 profit would leave the caterer out of pocket for the wasted spending.","id":"b41"},{"t":"Damages","k":"cl","q":"An entrepreneur preparing to launch her first podcast contracts with a recording studio for ten sessions. She pays the studio a $2,000 deposit and spends $3,500 on advertising that cannot be refunded. The studio then repudiates in order to take a better-paying client. She claims $50,000 in sponsorship profits, but she has no track record and no signed sponsors. Which recovery is she most likely to obtain?","o":["Reliance damages: the $5,500 she spent because of the contract.","Expectation damages: the $50,000 in sponsorship profits she hoped to earn.","Punitive damages, because the studio's breach was deliberate.","Nothing, because a party who cannot prove lost profits has no remedy."],"a":0,"w":"Expectation is the default, but lost profits must be proved with reasonable certainty, and a first venture with no track record cannot do that. So she falls back on reliance, her out-of-pocket loss, which puts her back where she started. A deliberate breach does not change this, because contract law does not punish unless the breach is also a tort.","id":"b42"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"cl","q":"A photographer leaves her only camera at a repair shop that mostly serves hobbyists. The shop promises it back in three days. She says nothing about her work. The shop, without excuse, takes three weeks, and she loses a $9,000 wedding booking because she has no camera and none can be rented in time. Can she recover the lost $9,000 fee from the shop?","o":["No, because the shop had no reason to foresee that loss when the contract was made.","No, because lost income is never recoverable for breach of contract.","Yes, because the delay in fact caused the loss of the booking.","Yes, because the fee was a fixed sum and can be proved with certainty."],"a":0,"w":"Under section 351 and Hadley and Baxendale, damages cover loss that arises naturally from the breach, or special loss the breaching party had reason to know about when the deal was made. A shop for hobbyists would not expect a late repair to cost a $9,000 job, and she never told it. Causation and certainty are both satisfied here, but they are separate fences. The loss still has to be foreseeable.","id":"b43"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"cl","q":"Same facts as the camera repair, with two changes. When she left the camera, the photographer told the clerk, \"I am shooting a wedding on Saturday for $9,000 and this is my only camera.\" And when the shop missed its deadline, she could have rented an identical camera for $150 and shot the wedding, but she did not try. The court finds that renting was a reasonable step. What is the most she can recover for the lost booking?","o":["$0","$150","$9,000","$9,150"],"a":1,"w":"The loss is now foreseeable, because she told the shop about the wedding. But section 350 bars damages for loss she could have avoided without undue risk or burden. Had she rented a camera, her only loss would have been the $150 rental, so that is the most she recovers. She cannot let the loss keep baking and then send the shop the bill for $9,000.","id":"b44"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"both","q":"Which is the most accurate statement about a stipulated damages clause?","o":["It is enforced whenever both parties signed freely, because courts do not review agreed terms.","It is enforced only if the agreed sum turns out to match the actual loss exactly.","If the agreed sum is a penalty, the entire contract is void.","It is enforced if the sum is a reasonable forecast of the loss, but not if it is a penalty."],"a":3,"w":"Under section 356 the parties may fix damages in advance, but only at an amount that is a reasonable forecast of the loss. An unreasonably large figure is a penalty and is unenforceable. That does not sink the whole contract. The court simply ignores the clause and goes back to ordinary damages.","id":"b45"},{"t":"Specific Performance","k":"both","q":"In which of the following is a court least likely to order the breaching party to perform?","o":["A landowner refuses to convey a parcel of land she contracted to sell.","A collector refuses to deliver a one-of-a-kind painting he contracted to sell.","A chef refuses to cook at a restaurant for the second year of a two-year contract.","A company refuses to complete an agreed merger whose lost value could only be guessed at."],"a":2,"w":"Personal service is the classic no. A court cannot supervise how well the chef cooks, and forced labour looks like involuntary servitude. Land is treated as unique, a one-of-a-kind painting cannot be replaced, and the value of a lost merger is too uncertain to price, so in each of those money is inadequate and an order to perform is available.","id":"b46"},{"t":"Substantial Performance","k":"cl","q":"A builder's deviation from the plans is innocent and trivial, and correcting it would mean tearing out finished work. Why will a court usually measure the owner's damages by the loss in the building's value and not by the cost to correct?","o":["Because the cost to correct would be clearly out of proportion to the loss in value, producing economic waste.","Because a builder who has substantially performed owes no damages at all.","Because the cost to correct is a penalty, and penalties are unenforceable.","Because the perfect tender rule allows only the difference in value."],"a":0,"w":"Section 348 says the owner gets the cost to fix, unless that cost is clearly out of proportion to the loss in value. Tearing down sound walls to swap one brand of pipe for an equal brand is economic waste, which is the lesson of Jacob and Youngs and Kent. The builder still owes damages, though. Close enough gets paid, less the damage made.","id":"b47"},{"t":"Perfect Tender","k":"ucc","q":"A restaurant orders 40 dining chairs in a walnut finish, to be delivered by June 1. On May 20 the seller delivers 40 chairs in an oak finish. The restaurant rejects them the same day. The seller at once notifies the restaurant that it will deliver walnut chairs by May 28. The restaurant replies that the deal is off. Which is the most accurate statement?","o":["The restaurant may cancel, because under the perfect tender rule any nonconformity ends the contract at once.","The restaurant must keep the oak chairs, because the seller substantially performed.","The restaurant must keep the oak chairs, because it accepted them by taking delivery.","The seller may still cure, because the time for performance has not expired and it gave notice."],"a":3,"w":"Under UCC section 2-601 the restaurant was entitled to reject, because the chairs did not conform. But rejection is not the end of the story. Under section 2-508, a seller who gives notice may make a conforming delivery while the contract time is still running, and June 1 has not arrived. Substantial performance has no place in a single delivery of goods.","id":"b48"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"A brewery contracts to buy 1,000 pounds of hops from a grower at $8 a pound, payment on delivery. The grower fails to deliver. Acting promptly and in good faith, the brewery buys 1,000 pounds of the same hops from another grower at $11 a pound and pays $300 for rush freight to get them in time. The brewery saved no expenses because of the breach. What may the brewery recover?","o":["$2,700","$3,000","$3,300","$11,300"],"a":2,"w":"This is cover under UCC section 2-712. The brewery recovers the cover price minus the contract price, which is $11,000 minus $8,000, or $3,000. Then it adds incidental damages under section 2-715, the $300 in rush freight, for a total of $3,300. It does not get the whole cover price, because it never paid the original $8,000.","id":"b49"},{"t":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","k":"cl","q":"Tess owes a caterer $4,000. She sells her van to Raj, who promises that instead of paying Tess he will pay the $4,000 directly to the caterer. Raj then discovers that Tess rolled back the odometer to induce him to buy, and he refuses to pay. The caterer sues Raj on his promise. Which is the most accurate statement?","o":["The caterer will win, because an intended beneficiary takes free of disputes between the contracting parties.","The caterer is an intended beneficiary, but Raj may raise Tess's fraud as a defence against it.","The caterer will lose, because it is only an incidental beneficiary of the van sale.","The caterer will lose, because a party not in privity can never enforce a promise."],"a":1,"w":"Raj's promise was meant to pay off Tess's debt, so the caterer is a creditor beneficiary and can sue under section 302, just as in Lawrence and Fox. But under section 309 the beneficiary takes the contract warts and all. Raj keeps the defences he would have against Tess, and fraud is one of them.","id":"b50"},{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"both","q":"Dot signs a one-page contract with Splash Brothers for a backyard koi pond. The invoice lists $2,000 for the liner, pump and rocks, and $9,000 for design, digging and labour. Dot pays a $1,000 deposit. The finished pond leaks slowly because the digging was sloppy. Dot announces that the pond \"is not perfect\" and that she may therefore reject the whole thing and pay nothing more. The brothers reply that the pond is \"basically fine.\" Which issue must a student resolve before anything else?","o":["Whether the brothers made a firm offer","Whether the perfect tender rule lets Dot reject the pond","Whether Article 2 or the common law governs a mixed deal for goods and services","Whether Dot's deposit counts as consideration"],"a":2,"w":"The giveaway is one contract that bundles movable things, the liner, pump and rocks, with a much larger amount of labour. UCC section 2-105 covers goods, so for a mixed deal most courts ask what the predominant purpose was, and that choice decides everything that follows. Perfect tender is tempting because Dot says the pond is not perfect, but you cannot know whether perfect tender or substantial performance applies until you have picked the rulebook.","id":"i1"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"While Mrs. Okoro is away on holiday, her neighbour Zed notices her fence has blown down. Without being asked, he spends a weekend and $80 of his own lumber rebuilding it. When she returns she is delighted and writes on a sticky note, \"For fixing my fence I will pay you $300 on Friday. Signed, Mrs. O.\" On Friday she changes her mind and offers him a fruit basket. Zed sues for the $300. What is the strongest issue these facts raise?","o":["Whether a promise made after the work was already done is supported by consideration","Promissory estoppel","The statute of frauds","Economic duress"],"a":0,"w":"The giveaway is the order of events: Zed finished the fence before Mrs. Okoro promised anything. Under Restatement section 71, consideration must be bargained for, meaning the promise and the performance are each given in exchange for the other, and a past favour was not given in exchange for a later promise. Promissory estoppel is tempting because Zed spent $80, but he spent it before the promise, so he did not rely on the promise at all.","id":"i2"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"Gil mows lawns in the summer. He texts his neighbour Wanda, \"Thinking about maybe taking on one more lawn this year. I would want at least $50 a week.\" Wanda texts back within a minute, \"Accepted! See you Saturday,\" and that afternoon she buys a new sprinkler to celebrate. Gil never shows up and says he decided to take up fishing. Wanda insists they have a contract. A student writing this exam answer should start with which issue?","o":["Promissory estoppel","The mailbox rule","Whether Wanda's acceptance mirrored the terms of the offer","Whether Gil's text was an offer or only an invitation to negotiate"],"a":3,"w":"The giveaway is Gil's hedged language: thinking about, maybe, and at least. Under Restatement section 24, an offer is a manifestation of willingness to enter a bargain that justifies the other person in believing her assent will conclude it, and floating a minimum price usually does not do that. Promissory estoppel is tempting because of the sprinkler, but Gil made no promise for Wanda to rely on, so the first question is whether there was an offer at all.","id":"i3"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"A bakery owner hands Raj a written job offer for an at-will position. It says, \"To accept, sign this letter and hand it to me in person at the bakery by 5 p.m. Friday. No other form of acceptance will do.\" On Thursday Raj leaves her a cheerful voicemail saying \"I accept!\" and turns down a job at a rival doughnut shop. On Friday at 6 p.m. he slides the signed letter under the bakery's locked door. On Saturday the owner hires her cousin instead. Which issue should you analyse first?","o":["Promissory estoppel","Whether Raj accepted in the manner and within the time the offer required","The statute of frauds","Whether the offer was too indefinite to be accepted"],"a":1,"w":"The giveaway is that the offer spells out exactly how and when to accept and says nothing else will do. Under Restatement section 60, when an offer prescribes the manner of acceptance, its terms must be complied with, and under section 36 an offer lapses at the time it sets. Promissory estoppel is tempting because Raj turned down another job, but the owner only made an offer with conditions, so you start by asking whether a voicemail and a late letter could accept it.","id":"i4"},{"t":"UCC Formation","k":"ucc","q":"A farmer and a grocer swap friendly emails saying, \"We should do pumpkins again this autumn.\" Nobody mentions a price and nothing formal is signed. In October the farmer delivers 300 pumpkins. The grocer stacks them by the front door and sells half of them, although a few are a bit lumpy. When the farmer sends a bill, the grocer refuses to pay, saying, \"We never agreed on a price, so there was never a contract.\" Which issue does the grocer's argument raise?","o":["Whether conduct by both sides can form a sale contract even though the price was left open","Whether the pumpkins breached the implied warranty of merchantability","The battle of the forms","Whether the farmer made a firm offer"],"a":0,"w":"The giveaway is that both sides behaved as if they had a deal: one delivered and the other displayed and sold the goods. Under UCC section 2-204, a contract for sale can be made in any manner that shows agreement, including conduct, and it does not fail just because a term such as price is left open. The lumpy pumpkins tempt you toward warranty, but the grocer is not complaining about quality; he is denying that any contract ever formed.","id":"i5"},{"t":"Firm Offers","k":"ucc","q":"Tile Barn, a tile wholesaler, emails a contractor a quotation signed by its sales manager: \"2,000 slate tiles at $3 each. This price will be held open for you until the end of the month.\" The contractor pays nothing for this promise and does nothing with it for two weeks. Slate prices then jump, and Tile Barn emails, \"Quote withdrawn.\" The next day, still within the month, the contractor replies, \"I accept your quote.\" Tile Barn says it was free to pull the offer. What is the strongest issue these facts raise?","o":["The mailbox rule","Promissory estoppel","Whether a merchant's signed promise to hold an offer open is irrevocable without consideration","The merchant confirmation exception to the statute of frauds"],"a":2,"w":"The giveaway is a merchant, a signed writing, and the words held open until the end of the month. Under UCC section 2-205, a merchant's signed written assurance that an offer to buy or sell goods will be held open makes it irrevocable for the stated time, up to three months, even with no consideration. Promissory estoppel is tempting, but the contractor did nothing in reliance for two weeks, so the firm offer rule is the doctrine doing the work.","id":"i6"},{"t":"Promissory Estoppel","k":"cl","q":"Pia runs a small bakery. A sandwich chain's regional agent tells her several times, \"Sell your bakery and move to Elm City, and a franchise is yours for $18,000.\" The lease, the royalty rate and the territory are never discussed, and no contract is ever signed. Pia sells her bakery at a loss, moves her family and her very large cat to Elm City, and rents a flat. The chain then says it has changed its mind. Pia wants to recover what the move cost her. Which doctrine should you analyse first?","o":["Whether the franchise agreement satisfies the statute of frauds","Unilateral mistake","Whether the agent made an offer that Pia accepted by moving","Whether Pia's reliance on the agent's assurances makes them enforceable without a bargain"],"a":3,"w":"The giveaway is a repeated promise, no finished contract, and a promisee who gave up a great deal because of what she was told. Under Restatement section 90, a promise that the promisor should reasonably expect to induce action, and that does induce it, is binding if injustice can be avoided only by enforcement. Offer and acceptance is tempting, but the key terms were never settled, so there is no bargain to enforce and reliance is Pia's route.","id":"i7"},{"t":"Battle of the Forms","k":"ucc","q":"A brewery sends a bottle maker its standard purchase order for 20,000 amber bottles. The bottle maker replies with its own printed acknowledgment, which matches the order on price, quantity and delivery date, but adds on the back, \"All disputes will be arbitrated in the seller's home city.\" Nobody reads either form. The bottles arrive, the brewery fills them, and later some of them crack. The brewery sues in court and the bottle maker demands arbitration. What is the strongest issue these facts raise?","o":["Whether the acknowledgment was a counter-offer under the mirror image rule","Whether an additional term in the seller's acknowledgment became part of the contract","Whether the arbitration clause is unconscionable","The parol evidence rule"],"a":1,"w":"The giveaway is two merchants trading printed forms that agree on the main terms but differ on a detail in the small print. For goods, UCC section 2-207 says a definite acceptance forms a contract even though it adds terms, and it then asks whether the additional term became part of the deal or dropped out, for example because it materially alters the contract. The mirror image rule is tempting, but that is the common law approach that section 2-207 replaces for sales of goods.","id":"i8"},{"t":"Interpretation","k":"cl","q":"Bo and Fay hire a wedding band called The Soggy Biscuits. The signed contract says the band will play \"three sets\" for $1,500, paid in advance. The band plays three sets of twenty minutes each and goes home at nine o'clock. Bo and Fay are furious, because they understood a set to mean forty-five minutes, which is how every other wedding band in town uses the word. The band says that to them a set has always been twenty minutes. The drummer also arrived ten minutes late. Which issue should you analyse first?","o":["Mutual mistake about a basic assumption","Whether the contract is too indefinite to enforce","Whose meaning of an ambiguous contract term controls","Liquidated damages"],"a":2,"w":"The giveaway is one written word, sets, that each side honestly reads differently, plus a local trade usage about what it means. Under Restatement sections 201 and 202, a court asks whose meaning should prevail and uses tools such as usage of trade and the circumstances to decide. Mutual mistake is tempting, but the parties are not wrong about a fact in the world; they disagree about what their own words mean, and that is an interpretation problem.","id":"i9"},{"t":"Parol Evidence Rule","k":"cl","q":"Juno rents a music studio from a landlord under a signed five-page lease. The lease describes the studio, the rent and the term, and ends with, \"This document is the entire agreement between the parties.\" It says nothing about the roof. Juno insists that the week before signing, the landlord shook her hand and said she could use the rooftop for free concerts. The landlord, who honestly does not remember saying it, has now padlocked the roof door. Juno wants to testify about the handshake promise. A student writing this exam answer should start with which issue?","o":["Whether the parol evidence rule bars proof of the earlier oral promise","Whether the rooftop promise fails for lack of consideration","Fraudulent misrepresentation","Whether Juno's interpretation of the word studio controls"],"a":0,"w":"The giveaway is a signed writing that calls itself the entire agreement, and a party who wants to add an oral promise made before it was signed. Under Restatement sections 209, 210 and 213, a completely integrated writing discharges earlier agreements within its scope, so the first question is whether the lease is integrated and whether the rooftop promise can come in at all. Fraud is tempting, but the facts say the landlord was honest, so the fraud exception has nothing to work with.","id":"i10"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"ucc","q":"At a crowded barbecue, Lou, a retired dentist, agrees out loud to sell his vintage pinball machine to Bea for $1,200. They shake hands in front of a dozen guests and agree that Bea will collect it and pay on Sunday. Nothing is written down, no money changes hands and the machine stays in Lou's garage. On Saturday a collector offers Lou $2,000, and Lou tells Bea the deal is off. Bea points out that twelve people heard him. Which defence do these facts most clearly set up for Lou?","o":["Lack of consideration","The parol evidence rule","Revocation of a firm offer","The statute of frauds for a sale of goods priced at $500 or more"],"a":3,"w":"The giveaway is an oral deal for goods at $1,200 with no writing, no payment and no delivery. Under UCC section 2-201, a contract for the sale of goods for $500 or more is not enforceable without a writing signed by the party to be charged, unless an exception such as part payment or accepted goods applies, and none does here. The parol evidence rule is tempting because both involve writings, but that rule is about adding to a writing that exists, and here there is no writing at all.","id":"i11"},{"t":"Warranties","k":"ucc","q":"Marge buys a new blender from an appliance shop. The shop window has a banner reading \"Best prices in town!\" The clerk rings up the sale without saying a word about the blender, and Marge does not mention what she plans to make. At home, the blender cannot get through a ripe banana and the motor smokes. The shop refuses a refund, saying, \"We never promised you anything about it.\" Marge simply wanted a blender that blends. Which issue should you analyse first?","o":["Express warranty based on the banner","The implied warranty of merchantability","The implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose","Fraudulent misrepresentation"],"a":1,"w":"The giveaway is a merchant that sells appliances, goods that fail at their ordinary job, and no statements about the goods by anyone. Under UCC section 2-314, a merchant who deals in goods of the kind automatically warrants that they are fit for the ordinary purposes for which such goods are used, so the shop's silence does not help it. Fitness for a particular purpose is tempting, but under section 2-315 that needs a special purpose the seller knew about, and blending a banana is as ordinary as it gets.","id":"i12"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"cl","q":"On moving day, a moving company's foreman hands Oleg a pre-printed service contract in his driveway while the truck idles. He says, \"Standard form, sign at the bottom,\" and says nothing else. The terms cannot be changed. In pale grey type on the back, the form says the customer gives up every claim for damaged belongings, that the company may raise its price at any time, and that a customer who sues must pay three times the company's legal fees. Oleg signs without turning the page. The movers later drop his piano. Which defence do these facts most clearly set up for Oleg against those clauses?","o":["Unconscionability","Economic duress","Unilateral mistake","Fraudulent misrepresentation"],"a":0,"w":"The giveaway is the pairing of an unfair process, a take-it-or-leave-it form with harsh clauses hidden in pale type, and unfairly one-sided terms. Under Restatement section 208, a court may refuse to enforce a term that was unconscionable when the contract was made, and courts usually look for both procedural and substantive unfairness. Duress is tempting because Oleg was hurried, but nobody made an improper threat; being rushed through a form is a bargaining process problem, which is the home ground of unconscionability.","id":"i13"},{"t":"Mistake","k":"cl","q":"A driver bumps a cyclist named Kit. A clinic takes an X-ray and tells them both that Kit has nothing worse than a bruised knee. Relying on that report, Kit and the driver sign a settlement: the driver pays $800 and Kit gives up his claim. Kit signs quickly because he wants the money for a new bicycle bell. A month later a specialist finds that the knee ligament was torn all along and needs a $40,000 operation. The driver never said anything untrue. Which doctrine should Kit's lawyer analyse first?","o":["Unconscionability","Fraudulent misrepresentation","Mutual mistake about a basic assumption of the contract","Lack of consideration for the release"],"a":2,"w":"The giveaway is that both parties shared the same wrong belief, from the same clinic report, about a fact that existed when they signed. Under Restatement section 152, a contract is voidable when both parties are mistaken about a basic assumption, the mistake materially affects the exchange, and the party seeking relief does not bear the risk under section 154. Misrepresentation is tempting because someone gave bad information, but it was the clinic, not the driver, and the driver believed it too.","id":"i14"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"Tess is viewing a flat and asks the landlord directly, \"Has this place ever had mice?\" The landlord, who has paid an exterminator to visit every month for two years, smiles and says, \"Never. Not one.\" The rent is unusually low and Tess does not look inside the cupboards. She signs a one-year lease. On her first night she meets a large and confident family of mice. Tess wants out of the lease. Which defence do these facts most clearly set up?","o":["Unilateral mistake","Fraudulent misrepresentation of a material fact","Unconscionability","Frustration of purpose"],"a":1,"w":"The giveaway is a direct question, a flatly false answer, and a speaker who knew the truth. Under Restatement sections 162 and 164, a contract is voidable when assent is induced by a fraudulent or material misrepresentation on which the other party was justified in relying. Unilateral mistake is tempting because Tess was wrong about the mice, but her mistake was caused by the landlord's lie, and misrepresentation is the more direct and much easier route.","id":"i15"},{"t":"Capacity","k":"cl","q":"Mr. Pym, aged 60, is in the middle of a manic episode that his doctor has documented. He arrives at a builder's yard wearing three hats, talks at length to a hedge, and signs a contract for a $90,000 replica pirate ship in his garden. The builder remarks to a colleague that the customer \"seems seriously unwell\" but takes the deposit anyway. The price is fair for a pirate ship, and Mr. Pym read the whole contract aloud before signing. The builder did nothing to push him. A week later his family wants the contract cancelled. What is the strongest issue these facts raise?","o":["Undue influence","Unconscionability","Economic duress","Whether mental illness made the contract voidable because the builder had reason to know of his condition"],"a":3,"w":"The giveaway is a documented mental illness, wildly out-of-character behaviour, and a builder who noticed something was wrong. Under Restatement section 15, a contract is voidable if a person cannot understand the transaction, or cannot act reasonably about it and the other party has reason to know of the condition. Undue influence is tempting, but the builder did not persuade or pressure him at all; the problem lies in Mr. Pym's own condition, which is a capacity question.","id":"i16"},{"t":"Duress","k":"cl","q":"Ned and Vera are partners in a noodle stall. Vera has a video of Ned singing very badly at an office party. She tells him, \"Sign your half of the stall over to me for $1,000 tonight, or this goes to your new employer and your mother-in-law.\" Ned's half is worth about $30,000. Vera tells no lies about the stall or its value. Ned, mortified and seeing no other way out, signs. He now wants the transfer undone. Which defence do these facts most clearly set up?","o":["Duress by improper threat","Undue influence","Unconscionability","Fraudulent misrepresentation"],"a":0,"w":"The giveaway is the phrase sign or else: Vera threatens to harm Ned in a way that gains her nothing except his signature. Under Restatement sections 175 and 176, a contract is voidable if assent is induced by an improper threat that leaves the victim no reasonable alternative, and a threat is improper when the exchange is unfair and the threatened act would harm the victim without really benefiting the person making it. Undue influence is tempting because they are partners, but that doctrine is about unfair persuasion by someone you trust, and this is a threat.","id":"i17"},{"t":"Undue Influence","k":"cl","q":"Aunt Bea is 88, sharp as a tack, and does the crossword in ink. She depends on her nephew Cy for her shopping, her banking and her lifts to the doctor. Over several weeks Cy visits daily and urges her to sell him her house for half its value. He tells her that lawyers \"will only confuse things,\" and one evening he insists she sign before he drives her home. He makes no threats and tells no lies about the house. She signs, with nobody else to advise her. Which defence do these facts most clearly set up?","o":["Lack of mental capacity","Duress by improper threat","Undue influence","Fraudulent misrepresentation"],"a":2,"w":"The giveaway is a dependent person, a trusted relative, steady pressure, a rushed signing, no independent advice and a lopsided price. Under Restatement section 177, a contract is voidable when assent is induced by unfair persuasion from someone who dominates the other party or whom that party is justified in trusting. Lack of capacity is tempting because of her age, but the facts go out of their way to say she is sharp, and duress fails because Cy never threatened anything.","id":"i18"},{"t":"Impossibility and Frustration","k":"cl","q":"A couple hires Sky, a drone pilot, to film their wedding in Maple Park from the air for $2,000. The contract calls specifically for aerial drone footage of the ceremony. Two weeks before the wedding, the city passes an ordinance banning all drone flights in public parks, with fines for pilots. Nobody saw the ban coming. Sky tells the couple she cannot fly, and the couple, who have already bought matching drone-themed cufflinks, sue her for breach. Which defence do these facts most clearly set up for Sky?","o":["Frustration of purpose","Mutual mistake about a basic assumption","Unilateral mistake","Impracticability because a new law prohibits the performance"],"a":3,"w":"The giveaway is an unforeseen event after signing, here a new law, that stops Sky from doing the very thing she promised. Under Restatement sections 261 and 264, a duty is discharged when performance becomes impracticable through an event whose non-occurrence was a basic assumption of the contract, and a new government regulation is a classic example. Frustration of purpose is tempting, but under section 265 that applies when performance is still possible and has merely become pointless, whereas here the performance itself is forbidden.","id":"i19"},{"t":"Substantial Performance","k":"cl","q":"Hugo hires a carpenter to build a garden deck for $12,000, payable on completion. The plans call for a deck twenty feet wide with a chestnut stain. The carpenter finishes a day early, but the deck is nineteen feet ten inches wide and the stain is walnut, a shade darker. It would cost about $300 to restain it. Hugo, who admits the deck is sturdy and handsome, refuses to pay a single dollar because \"it is not what the contract says.\" Which issue should you analyse first?","o":["Whether the perfect tender rule allows Hugo to reject the deck","Whether the carpenter substantially performed, so that Hugo must pay the price less damages","Impracticability","Whether the contract price is a penalty"],"a":1,"w":"The giveaway is a finished service with small, cheap-to-fix departures from the plans, and an owner who wants to pay nothing. Under Restatement sections 237 and 241, only a material failure of performance excuses the other party's duty, so a builder who substantially performs can recover the price minus the cost of the defects. Perfect tender is tempting because Hugo demands exact compliance, but that rule belongs to sales of goods under the UCC, and building a deck is a service.","id":"i20"},{"t":"Perfect Tender","k":"ucc","q":"A bike shop orders forty medium helmets from a distributor for a single delivery on the first of the month. On that day exactly forty medium helmets arrive, but three of them have cracked shells. The shop owner, who has just found a cheaper supplier, sends the entire shipment back the same afternoon. The distributor protests that thirty-seven good helmets out of forty is \"substantial performance\" and demands payment for the lot. What is the strongest issue these facts raise?","o":["Whether the buyer may reject the whole shipment because the goods fail in any respect to conform","Whether the seller substantially performed","The implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose","Whether this is an installment contract"],"a":0,"w":"The giveaway is a single delivery of goods with a small defect and a buyer who rejects everything. Under UCC section 2-601, if the goods fail in any respect to conform to the contract, the buyer may reject the whole, accept the whole, or accept any commercial units and reject the rest. Substantial performance is tempting because the seller uses those words, but that is the common law standard for services, and the softer installment rule in section 2-612 does not apply to a one-shot delivery.","id":"i21"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"cl","q":"Nia pays a print shop $60 to print five hundred yard-sale flyers by Friday. The shop delivers them on Monday. Nia then claims $40,000 in damages. She explains, for the first time, that a wealthy collector was flying in on Saturday only, that she had planned to use the flyers to lure him to her yard sale, and that he would have bought her late uncle's painting of a goose. The print shop knew only that the flyers were for a yard sale. The shop admits it was late. Which issue should you analyse first?","o":["Whether the damages clause is an unenforceable penalty","Whether Nia is entitled to specific performance","Whether the loss was foreseeable to the print shop when the contract was made","Whether the print shop substantially performed"],"a":2,"w":"The giveaway is a tiny contract, a huge and unusual loss, and special circumstances the breaching party was never told about. Under Restatement section 351, following Hadley and Baxendale, damages are not recoverable for loss the party in breach had no reason to foresee as a probable result of breach when the contract was made. A penalty clause is tempting whenever a number looks outrageous, but this contract has no damages clause at all; the $40,000 is simply what Nia claims she lost.","id":"i22"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"A café chain agrees to buy one hundred patio heaters from a manufacturer at $200 each. A week before delivery, after an unusually warm forecast, the chain says it will not take them. The manufacturer tells the chain it intends to resell, and then in good faith sells all one hundred heaters to a garden centre at $150 each, which is a fair price in a falling market. The manufacturer no longer has the heaters. It now wants to recover from the café chain. Which remedy should you analyse first?","o":["The buyer's cover damages","An action for the full contract price","Specific performance","The seller's damages measured by the resale price against the contract price"],"a":3,"w":"The giveaway is a breaching buyer and a seller who gave notice and then resold the goods in a commercially reasonable way. Under UCC section 2-706, that seller may recover the difference between the contract price and the resale price, plus incidental damages and less expenses saved, which here starts at $50 a heater. An action for the price is tempting, but section 2-709 is mainly for goods the buyer accepted or goods the seller cannot reasonably resell, and these heaters were resold.","id":"i23"},{"t":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","k":"cl","q":"A grandfather signs a contract with a piano teacher and pays $3,000 in advance. The contract says the teacher will give \"weekly lessons for one year to my granddaughter Zoe.\" Zoe is fifteen and signed nothing. After two lessons the teacher moves abroad to join a circus and keeps the money. The grandfather is on a long cruise and has no interest in suing anyone. Zoe, through her mother, sues the teacher. Which issue should you analyse first?","o":["Whether the grandfather validly assigned his contract rights to Zoe","Whether Zoe is an intended beneficiary who can enforce the contract herself","Whether the teacher improperly delegated the lessons","Whether Zoe lacked capacity because she is a minor"],"a":1,"w":"The giveaway is a plaintiff who never signed the contract but is named in it as the person who receives the performance. Under Restatement section 302, a third party is an intended beneficiary, with her own right to sue, when giving her that right fits the parties' intention and the promisee meant to give her the benefit of the performance. Assignment is tempting, but an assignment is a later transfer of an existing right, and here Zoe's benefit was built into the contract from the start. Her age is a red herring, because capacity matters for people who make contracts, and Zoe did not make one.","id":"i24"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"cl","q":"Lars, a landscaper, finishes replanting a hotel's gardens, and the hotel owes him $4,000. The contract says nothing about transfers. Lars owes his sister Inga money, so he signs a note giving her his right to the hotel's $4,000, and Inga sends the hotel a copy. The hotel manager refuses to pay Inga, saying, \"We never made a contract with you, and we never agreed to this.\" The gardens look splendid and Lars has no work left to do. What is the strongest issue these facts raise?","o":["Whether a right to payment can be assigned without the obligor's consent","Whether Inga is an intended third-party beneficiary of the landscaping contract","Whether Lars's duties were too personal to delegate","The statute of frauds"],"a":0,"w":"The giveaway is a finished job, a simple right to be paid, and the holder of that right handing it to someone else afterwards. Under Restatement section 317, a contract right can generally be assigned without the obligor's consent unless the assignment would materially change the obligor's duty or risk, or is barred by law or by the contract, and paying Inga is no harder than paying Lars. Third-party beneficiary is tempting, but the hotel and Lars did not intend to benefit Inga when they made the contract, and delegation is about handing off duties, which Lars has already performed.","id":"i25"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"By letter, Olga offers to sell her boat to Ravi. On Tuesday Olga mails a revocation. On Wednesday Ravi, knowing nothing of this, mails a properly addressed acceptance. On Thursday Ravi receives the revocation. Is there a contract?","o":["No, because Olga revoked first.","No, because Olga had not yet received the acceptance.","Yes, because the acceptance took effect when mailed, before the revocation was received.","Yes, because an offer made by letter can never be revoked."],"a":2,"w":"Under section 63 an acceptance works on dispatch. A revocation works only on receipt. Ravi posted his acceptance on Wednesday, before the revocation reached him on Thursday, so a contract was formed on Wednesday.","id":"acceptance-m1"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"A painter offers to paint Lena's house for $4,000. Lena replies, \"I'll pay $3,500.\" The painter says, \"No thanks,\" and nothing more. Lena then says, \"Fine, I accept your $4,000 offer.\" Is there a contract?","o":["No, because Lena's counteroffer ended her power to accept and the painter did not renew the offer.","Yes, because Lena finally agreed to the painter's price.","Yes, because the original offer was never withdrawn.","No, because painting contracts must be in writing."],"a":0,"w":"Under sections 36 and 59 a counteroffer is a rejection, and it ends the power to accept. In Livingstone and Evans the seller's reply renewed the offer. Here the painter just said no, so there was nothing left to accept.","id":"acceptance-m2"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"An owner says, \"I will pay $1,000 to anyone who paints my whole fence by Friday. I want the job done, not a promise.\" A painter starts on Monday. With half the fence painted, the owner says, \"I revoke.\" What is the result under Restatement section 45?","o":["The revocation works, because the painter had not finished.","The owner must pay $1,000 at once.","The revocation works, because the painter never promised anything.","The owner cannot revoke, and must give the painter the chance to finish and earn the $1,000."],"a":3,"w":"Section 45 says that once the offeree begins the requested performance, an option contract arises. The offeror cannot revoke, but only owes the price if the job is completed. Four-five: once you start, the offer stays alive.","id":"acceptance-m3"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"Which statement best describes the general rule on silence as acceptance?","o":["Silence is acceptance whenever the offeror says it will be.","Silence is not acceptance, except in limited cases such as knowingly taking the benefit of offered services or a history of prior dealings.","Silence is acceptance if the offer was made in writing.","Silence is acceptance after thirty days."],"a":1,"w":"Section 69 says silence normally means nothing. An offeror cannot force a contract on you by announcing that no reply means yes. The exceptions are narrow: taking the benefit of services, invited silence you intend as a yes, and prior dealings.","id":"acceptance-m4"},{"t":"Acceptance","k":"cl","q":"On Monday, a homeowner offers to sell her land to a neighbour and adds, \"I'll keep this open for you until Saturday.\" The neighbour gives nothing for that assurance. On Wednesday, before any acceptance, she tells the neighbour the offer is withdrawn. Is the revocation effective at common law?","o":["No, because she promised to keep the offer open.","No, because offers to sell land cannot be revoked.","Yes, because without consideration the promise to keep the offer open is not a binding option.","Yes, but only if she has already sold the land to someone else."],"a":2,"w":"This is Dickinson and Dodds. An offer can be revoked any time before acceptance, even if the offeror said it would stay open. Only an option contract, usually bought with consideration under sections 25 and 87, takes away the power to revoke.","id":"acceptance-m5"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"cl","q":"A web designer builds a site for a client, who owes her $5,000. The designer assigns her right to the $5,000 to her landlord. When the landlord demands payment, the client shows that the site was never finished. What is the landlord's position?","o":["The landlord takes free of the client's complaint, because the landlord did nothing wrong.","The assignment is void, because rights to money cannot be assigned.","The landlord takes the right subject to the defences the client had against the designer.","The landlord can sue the client for the full $5,000 and the client must then sue the designer."],"a":2,"w":"An assignee steps into the assignor's shoes. Under section 317 the right passes to the landlord, but it is the same right, with the same weaknesses. Whatever defence the obligor had against the assignor works against the assignee.","id":"assignment-m1"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"cl","q":"A gardener contracts to weed a homeowner's borders every week, then delegates the job to a competent friend. The friend stops turning up. The homeowner never agreed to release the gardener. Whom can the homeowner hold liable?","o":["The gardener, who remains liable on the original contract.","No one, because the duty was validly delegated.","Only the friend, because the gardener dropped out of the contract.","The gardener, but only if the friend cannot pay."],"a":0,"w":"Section 318: delegating a duty does not discharge the delegator. Three-one-eight, you still carry the weight. The gardener is released only if the homeowner agrees to a novation.","id":"assignment-m2"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"cl","q":"What distinguishes a novation from an ordinary delegation of duties?","o":["A novation transfers only rights, never duties.","A novation must be approved by a court.","A novation happens automatically whenever a duty is delegated.","A novation is a new contract, agreed by all the parties, that substitutes a new party and releases the old one."],"a":3,"w":"A delegation is done by one party alone, and leaves the delegator liable. A novation is a fresh agreement, needing the consent of everyone involved, under which the new party takes over and the original party is discharged.","id":"assignment-m3"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"cl","q":"A gallery commissions a celebrated sculptor to carve a statue. The sculptor, busy with other work, hands the commission to a talented former student without asking the gallery. Is the delegation effective?","o":["Yes, because any duty may be delegated to a competent person.","No, because the duty depends on the sculptor's personal skill and reputation.","Yes, provided the student charges the same price.","No, because duties under a contract can never be delegated."],"a":1,"w":"Under section 318, a duty cannot be delegated where the other party has a substantial interest in having that particular person perform. The gallery bargained for this sculptor's hands, not for a sculpture by someone else.","id":"assignment-m4"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"both","q":"A coffee roaster appoints a firm as its exclusive distributor in one state, with a duty to use best efforts to sell the roaster's beans. The firm is then bought by a company owned by the roaster's biggest competitor, which takes over the distributorship. Under the majority's approach in Sally Beauty v. Nexxus Products, may the roaster refuse the substitution?","o":["No, because UCC 2-210 allows every duty to be delegated.","No, unless the roaster first proves that sales have actually fallen.","Yes, because the roaster has a substantial interest in not having its best-efforts duty performed by a competitor's subsidiary.","Yes, because distributorships are outside the UCC altogether."],"a":2,"w":"UCC section 2-210 lets a party perform through a delegate unless the other side has a substantial interest in having the original promisor perform. The Sally Beauty majority held that best efforts under an exclusive distributorship cannot be delegated to a competitor's subsidiary without consent. Judge Posner, dissenting, would have required proof of real harm.","id":"assignment-m5"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"An aunt tells her adult niece, \"If you stay off all social media for one year, I will pay you $2,000.\" The niece does so. The aunt refuses to pay, saying she gained nothing from it. Is the promise enforceable?","o":["No, because the aunt received no benefit.","Yes, because the niece gave up something she had a legal right to do, at the aunt's request.","No, because family promises are never enforceable.","Yes, because all promises of money are enforceable."],"a":1,"w":"This is Hamer and Sidway. Giving up a legal right at the promisor's request is forbearance, and under section 71 forbearance is good consideration. It does not matter whether the promisor got any benefit.","id":"bargain-m1"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"While Priya is abroad, her neighbour repairs Priya's storm-damaged fence without being asked. When Priya returns she says, \"That was kind. I'll pay you $300 for it.\" She never pays. Can the neighbour enforce the promise under the traditional rule?","o":["Yes, because the neighbour did valuable work.","Yes, because Priya's promise was clear and definite.","No, because the promise was not in writing.","No, because the work was done before the promise and was not given in exchange for it."],"a":3,"w":"Past consideration is no consideration. As in Mills and Wyman, the work was finished before any promise was made, so it could not be the price of that promise. A moral duty to pay is not enough.","id":"bargain-m2"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"Sam agrees to sell his vintage guitar, worth about $4,000, to Dana for $500. Before delivery Sam regrets the deal and argues the contract fails for lack of consideration. Who is right?","o":["Dana, because courts do not ask whether the consideration was adequate.","Sam, because $500 is far too little.","Sam, because consideration must be roughly equal in value.","Dana, but only if she pays the full $4,000."],"a":0,"w":"Section 79 says there is no requirement of equal value. If the price was truly bargained for, a court will not weigh it. Seven-nine: a peppercorn is fine. A huge gap matters only as a clue to something like fraud or duress.","id":"bargain-m3"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"Under Restatement section 71, when is a performance or return promise \"bargained for\"?","o":["When it has a market value greater than zero.","When it benefits the promisor financially.","When the promisor seeks it in exchange for the promise and the promisee gives it in exchange for that promise.","When it is written down and signed by both sides."],"a":2,"w":"Section 71 defines consideration as something bargained for: sought by the promisor in exchange for the promise, and given by the promisee in exchange for it. Value alone, benefit alone, or writing alone will not do.","id":"bargain-m4"},{"t":"The Bargain","k":"cl","q":"A manager tells a junior colleague, \"Stop by my office on Friday and you can have my old laptop.\" The colleague stops by, but the manager has given the laptop to someone else. Why is the promise most likely unenforceable?","o":["Because laptops cannot be the subject of a contract.","Because stopping by was only a condition of collecting a gift, not something the manager bargained for.","Because the colleague arrived too late in the day.","Because the promise was made at work."],"a":1,"w":"This is a conditional gift. Coming to the office is just how the gift gets collected. Under section 71 consideration must be sought by the promisor as the price of the promise, and the manager was not bargaining for a visit.","id":"bargain-m5"},{"t":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","k":"cl","q":"A city hires a contractor to build a new stadium by March. A café across the street expects a flood of customers. The contractor finishes a year late, and the café sues the contractor for its lost profits. What result?","o":["The café wins, because it was foreseeably harmed.","The café wins, because it is a creditor beneficiary.","The café loses, because only the city's residents can sue.","The café loses, because it is only an incidental beneficiary of the contract."],"a":3,"w":"Under section 302, only an intended beneficiary can enforce a contract. The city and the contractor did not make their deal in order to benefit the café. Its gain would have been a happy accident, so it is incidental.","id":"beneficiaries-m1"},{"t":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","k":"cl","q":"A father buys a car from a dealer on credit, and the dealer promises to deliver it to the father's daughter. The father never pays a cent. The daughter sues the dealer for the car. Can the dealer rely on the father's non-payment?","o":["No, because the daughter did nothing wrong.","Yes, because the promisor can raise against the beneficiary the defences it has under the contract.","No, because defences are personal to the promisee.","Yes, but only if the daughter knew about the non-payment."],"a":1,"w":"Section 309: the beneficiary's right comes through the contract, so it is subject to the promisor's contract defences. If the promisee's failure to perform excuses the promisor, it excuses the promisor against the beneficiary as well.","id":"beneficiaries-m2"},{"t":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","k":"cl","q":"Priya owes Quinn $500. Priya sells her bike to Rob, and in return Rob promises Priya that he will pay the $500 price directly to Quinn. Rob does not pay. What is Quinn?","o":["A creditor beneficiary, who can sue Rob on his promise.","An incidental beneficiary, who cannot sue anyone.","A donee beneficiary, who can sue only Priya.","An assignee of the bike."],"a":0,"w":"This is Lawrence and Fox. Rob's performance would pay off a debt the promisee, Priya, owes to Quinn. That makes Quinn an intended beneficiary of the creditor kind, and Quinn can sue the promisor directly.","id":"beneficiaries-m3"},{"t":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","k":"cl","q":"Which statement best describes when a third party is an intended beneficiary?","o":["Whenever the third party would be better off if the contract were performed.","Only when the third party is named in the contract and has signed it.","When giving the third party a right to performance carries out what the contracting parties intended.","Only when the third party paid part of the consideration."],"a":2,"w":"Section 302 turns on the intention of the parties. The beneficiary need not sign, pay, or even know about the contract at the time. But a mere benefit, without that intention, is incidental.","id":"beneficiaries-m4"},{"t":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","k":"cl","q":"A grandmother pays a carpenter $4,000, and the carpenter promises her that he will build a treehouse in her grandson's garden as a birthday present. The carpenter never builds it. What is the grandson?","o":["A creditor beneficiary, because the grandmother owed him a treehouse.","A donee beneficiary, who can enforce the carpenter's promise.","An incidental beneficiary, because he paid nothing.","A promisee, because the treehouse was for him."],"a":1,"w":"The grandmother meant the performance as a gift to her grandson, like the aunt in Seaver and Ransom. He is an intended beneficiary of the donee kind under section 302. It does not matter that he gave no consideration.","id":"beneficiaries-m5"},{"t":"Boilerplate","k":"cl","q":"Dana joins a gym by signing its standard two-page membership form without reading it. The form requires 30 days' written notice to cancel, which is typical for gyms. Dana later says she is not bound because she never read that term. Is she bound?","o":["No, because a term is binding only if it was actually read.","No, because standard forms are never enforceable against consumers.","Yes, because signing a standard form adopts its terms, read or not.","Yes, but only if the gym read the term aloud to her."],"a":2,"w":"Under section 211, a person who signs a standard form adopts its terms whether or not she read them. An ordinary notice clause is nothing the gym had reason to think she would refuse. Not reading is not a defence.","id":"boilerplate-m1"},{"t":"Boilerplate","k":"cl","q":"Under Restatement section 211(3), when is a term in a standardized agreement NOT part of the agreement?","o":["When the drafter has reason to believe the other party would not have assented had they known the term was there.","Whenever the other party did not read the form.","Whenever the form was offered on a take-it-or-leave-it basis.","When the term appears on the back of the form."],"a":0,"w":"Section 211, subsection three, removes a term when the drafter has reason to believe the signer would not agree if they knew about it. If they know you'd say no, the term must go. Merely failing to read, or having no chance to bargain, is not enough.","id":"boilerplate-m2"},{"t":"Boilerplate","k":"cl","q":"In Carnival Cruise Lines v. Shute, why did the majority enforce the forum selection clause printed on the ticket?","o":["Because the Shutes had personally negotiated the clause.","Because consumers can never challenge a forum clause.","Because the injury happened in Florida.","Because the clause was reasonable: it limited where the line could be sued, cut costs, and was not adopted in bad faith."],"a":3,"w":"The Court accepted that the clause was not negotiated, but found it reasonable. One home court saves litigation costs, passengers benefit through lower fares, and Florida was no remote alien forum. The injury actually happened off the coast of Mexico.","id":"boilerplate-m3"},{"t":"Boilerplate","k":"cl","q":"What is the key difference between the forum selection clause in The Bremen v. Zapata and the one in Carnival Cruise Lines v. Shute?","o":["The Bremen clause was oral; the Carnival clause was written.","The Bremen clause was bargained over by sophisticated businesses; the Carnival clause was fine print on a consumer ticket.","The Bremen clause was struck down; the Carnival clause was enforced.","The Bremen clause chose a United States court; the Carnival clause chose a foreign court."],"a":1,"w":"In The Bremen, two commercial parties of roughly equal strength negotiated the London forum clause, and it was enforced. Carnival applied that approach to a form ticket that nobody negotiated. That stretch is exactly what the dissent objected to.","id":"boilerplate-m4"},{"t":"Boilerplate","k":"cl","q":"A phone contract states the monthly price on page one. On page 31, in small print, it limits the company's liability for any loss. Under Rakoff's approach to contracts of adhesion, how should these two terms be treated?","o":["Both are fully binding, because the customer signed.","Neither is binding, because the contract was not negotiated.","The price is a visible term and binds; the liability limit is an invisible term that should give way to background law unless justified.","The liability limit binds, but the price can be reset by the court."],"a":2,"w":"Rakoff separates visible terms, the ones customers actually shop on, from invisible fine print. Visible terms are enforced. Invisible terms should yield to the ordinary background law unless the drafter can justify them. Visible, you chose. Invisible, who knows?","id":"boilerplate-m5"},{"t":"Capacity","k":"cl","q":"Jo, aged 17, buys a laptop for $900. Three months later it is scratched and worth $500. Still 17, Jo returns it and demands her $900. Under the approach in Halbman v. Lemke, what does Jo recover?","o":["Nothing, because she used the laptop.","$900, because a disaffirming minor is not charged for use or depreciation.","$500, the laptop's present value.","$400, the amount of the depreciation."],"a":1,"w":"Under section 14 a minor's contract is voidable at the minor's option. In Halbman and Lemke the court said a minor who disaffirms hands back what is left and is not charged for use or depreciation. Making her pay would enforce the contract by the back door.","id":"capacity-m1"},{"t":"Capacity","k":"cl","q":"Which statement best describes a contract made by a 16-year-old for a non-necessary item?","o":["It is voidable at the minor's option.","It is void from the start and binds no one.","It is voidable by either party.","It is fully binding if the price was fair."],"a":0,"w":"Section 14 makes a minor's contract voidable, not void. The choice belongs to the minor alone, and it does not matter that the deal was fair. Picture the fourteen-year-old signing in pencil.","id":"capacity-m2"},{"t":"Capacity","k":"cl","q":"A dealer agrees to sell a motorbike to Sam. Before delivery, the dealer gets a better offer, learns that Sam is 17, and refuses to deliver, saying the contract is invalid. Sam wants the bike. Who is right?","o":["The dealer, because a minor's contract is void.","The dealer, because either side may cancel a voidable contract.","Neither, because the contract needs a parent's signature.","Sam, because only the minor has the power to avoid the contract."],"a":3,"w":"Voidable is not void. The contract stands unless the protected person cancels it, and only the minor holds that power. The adult is bound. The rule is a shield for the minor, not an escape hatch for the dealer.","id":"capacity-m3"},{"t":"Capacity","k":"cl","q":"Ray has a serious mental illness. During an episode he fully understands that he is selling his house for half its value, but his illness leaves him unable to act reasonably about it. The buyer knows of Ray's condition. Can Ray avoid the sale?","o":["No, because he understood the transaction.","No, unless a court had already declared him incompetent.","Yes, because he could not act reasonably and the buyer had reason to know.","Yes, because any contract made by a person with a mental illness is void."],"a":2,"w":"Section 15 has two routes. One: he could not understand the deal. Two: he could not act reasonably about it, and the other side had reason to know. Ray fits the second, which is the modern test from Ortelere.","id":"capacity-m4"},{"t":"Capacity","k":"cl","q":"Lee, aged 17 and living on his own, receives emergency medical treatment and signs the clinic's payment form. He later refuses to pay, saying he was a minor. What is the most likely result?","o":["Lee owes nothing, because minors can disaffirm any contract.","Lee owes the reasonable value of the treatment, because it was a necessary.","Lee owes whatever price the form states, however high.","The clinic must sue Lee's parents and no one else."],"a":1,"w":"Necessaries are the N in N, E, S. A minor who receives things such as food, clothing or medical care must pay their reasonable value. Note that it is the reasonable value, not necessarily the contract price.","id":"capacity-m5"},{"t":"Damages","k":"cl","q":"A painter agrees to paint a house for $9,000. The job would cost him $6,000 in labour and paint. The owner cancels before he buys anything or starts work. What are the painter's expectation damages?","o":["$9,000, the full contract price.","$6,000, the cost of doing the job.","$3,000, the profit he would have made.","Nothing, because he had not started."],"a":2,"w":"Section 347 takes the value he lost, nine thousand dollars, and subtracts the six thousand in costs he avoided. That leaves his three thousand dollar profit. Expectation puts him where performance would have, no better.","id":"damages-m1"},{"t":"Damages","k":"cl","q":"Which interest is protected by making the breaching party hand back a benefit that the injured party conferred on them?","o":["The restitution interest.","The expectation interest.","The reliance interest.","The punitive interest."],"a":0,"w":"Section 344 lists three interests. Restitution looks at what the breacher gained, and makes them give it back. Expectation and reliance both look at what the injured party lost.","id":"damages-m2"},{"t":"Damages","k":"cl","q":"A dentist guarantees a patient \"a perfect smile\" from a painful procedure. The procedure hurts exactly as expected, but the result is worse than before. Under the reasoning of Hawkins v. McGee, why is the ordinary pain of the procedure left out of expectation damages?","o":["Because pain can never be valued in money.","Because the patient signed a consent form.","Because dentists cannot make binding promises.","Because the patient would have suffered that pain even if the promise had been kept."],"a":3,"w":"Hawkins and McGee measures the value promised minus the value received. Pain the patient would have gone through anyway is part of the price of the bargain. It was not caused by the breach.","id":"damages-m3"},{"t":"Damages","k":"cl","q":"A promoter signs a singer for a one-night show at a brand-new venue, then pays $8,000 in non-refundable deposits. The singer cancels. No one can say how many tickets would have sold. What is the promoter's most realistic recovery?","o":["Expectation damages for the full lost profit.","Reliance damages of $8,000.","Punitive damages to deter singers from cancelling.","Nothing, because the profit cannot be proved."],"a":1,"w":"When lost profits are too speculative, the injured party can fall back on the reliance interest in section 344. That is the money spent because of the contract. Here, the eight thousand dollars in deposits.","id":"damages-m4"},{"t":"Damages","k":"cl","q":"A buyer pays $1,000 in advance for a watch that is worth $1,800 on the market. The seller keeps the money and never delivers. Using the points A, B and C, what does the contract expectation measure give the buyer?","o":["$800, the lost bargain only.","$1,000, the return to where she started.","$1,800, the distance from A to C.","$2,800, the price plus the market value."],"a":2,"w":"She is at A, one thousand down. Performance would put her at C, eight hundred up. C minus A is what they pay: one thousand eight hundred dollars. Going back only to B would be a tort measure.","id":"damages-m5"},{"t":"Duress","k":"cl","q":"A painter agrees to paint an office for $8,000. Halfway through, with nothing about the job changed, the painter says, \"Promise me $10,000 or I walk.\" The owner, with a grand opening in two days, says yes. The painter finishes. Under the traditional rule, how much is owed?","o":["$10,000, because the owner agreed to it.","$10,000, because the painter finished the job.","$8,000, because the painter gave nothing new for the extra promise.","Nothing, because the painter's threat cancels the whole contract."],"a":2,"w":"This is Alaska Packers on dry land. Under section 73, doing what you already owe is not consideration, so the promise of the extra two thousand dollars is not enforceable. The owner could also argue economic duress.","id":"duress-m1"},{"t":"Duress","k":"cl","q":"Under Restatement section 175, a contract is voidable for duress when a party's assent is induced by:","o":["An improper threat that leaves the victim no reasonable alternative.","Any threat at all, however minor.","A price that turns out to be unfair.","Financial need that makes the party desperate for a deal."],"a":0,"w":"Section 175 needs both halves. T, I, N, A: a threat, that is improper, leaving no alternative. A bad price or a tight spot, without an improper threat from the other side, is not duress.","id":"duress-m2"},{"t":"Duress","k":"cl","q":"After a storm, a homeowner badly needs a generator. The only shop with stock charges $3,000, well above the usual price, and says, \"That's the price.\" The homeowner pays, then claims duress. What is the likely result?","o":["Duress, because the homeowner had no alternative.","Duress, because the price was above market.","Duress, because the shop knew the homeowner was desperate.","No duress, because the shop made no improper threat."],"a":3,"w":"Hard bargaining is not duress. The storm created the pressure, not the shop, and take it or leave it is not an improper threat under section 176. Other doctrines might help the homeowner, but duress will not.","id":"duress-m3"},{"t":"Duress","k":"cl","q":"A supplier tells a manufacturer, \"Pay 20% more or I stop delivering.\" The manufacturer could buy identical parts from another supplier the next day at a slightly higher price, but agrees anyway. Later it sues to recover the increase as paid under duress. What result?","o":["It wins, because a threat to breach is always duress.","It loses, because it had a reasonable alternative.","It wins, because the supplier acted in bad faith.","It loses, because duress applies only to physical threats."],"a":1,"w":"Compare Austin Instrument and Loral, where the buyer rang ten suppliers and none could deliver in time. Here the goods were available elsewhere. With a reasonable alternative, section 175 is not met, even if the threat was improper.","id":"duress-m4"},{"t":"Duress","k":"cl","q":"Which of these threats is improper under Restatement section 176?","o":["\"Accept my price or I'll sell to someone else.\"","\"Pay the debt you honestly owe me or I'll sue.\"","\"Sign this contract or I'll report you to the police for that old offence.\"","\"I won't renew our contract when it ends unless you pay more.\""],"a":2,"w":"Section 176 lists a threat of criminal prosecution as improper, even if the person is guilty. One seven six: crimes, torts and bad-faith tricks. Refusing to deal, or suing in good faith on a real debt, is ordinary bargaining.","id":"duress-m5"},{"t":"Promissory Estoppel","k":"cl","q":"An uncle tells his nephew, \"I'm proud of you. I'll give you $10,000 toward a car next month.\" Relying on this, the nephew signs a purchase contract and pays a non-refundable deposit, as the uncle knew he would. The uncle then refuses to pay. What is the nephew's best theory?","o":["Breach of contract, because the uncle's pride was consideration.","Promissory estoppel, because he reasonably and foreseeably relied on the promise to his cost.","No theory at all, because gift promises are never enforceable.","Breach of contract, because the promise named a definite sum."],"a":1,"w":"There is no bargain here, so no consideration. But section 90 makes a promise binding when the promisor should expect reliance, the reliance happens, and injustice would otherwise result. Run PRIDE, and every element is met.","id":"estoppel-m1"},{"t":"Promissory Estoppel","k":"cl","q":"What does Restatement section 90 say about the remedy for promissory estoppel?","o":["The court must always award full expectation damages.","The court may only order the promise to be performed.","No money may be awarded, only an apology.","The remedy may be limited as justice requires, so a court may award only reliance losses."],"a":3,"w":"Section 90 ends by saying the remedy may be limited as justice requires. Some courts enforce the full promise, as in Feinberg and Pfeiffer. Many award only what was lost in reliance, as in Elvin Associates and Franklin.","id":"estoppel-m2"},{"t":"Promissory Estoppel","k":"cl","q":"A manager tells a job applicant, \"We might have an opening next year. Stay in touch.\" The applicant turns down a firm job offer elsewhere. No opening ever appears. Will promissory estoppel help the applicant?","o":["No, because there was no clear promise on which it was reasonable to rely.","Yes, because she relied to her detriment.","Yes, because the manager should have foreseen her disappointment.","No, because estoppel never applies to employment."],"a":0,"w":"The first element of section 90 is a promise. A statement that something might happen is not a commitment, and relying on a maybe is not reasonable. Detriment alone is never enough.","id":"estoppel-m3"},{"t":"Promissory Estoppel","k":"cl","q":"A friend says, \"I'm going to give you my old couch next week.\" You are pleased but do nothing differently. The friend then gives the couch to someone else. Why does a promissory estoppel claim fail?","o":["Because couches are goods.","Because the promise was not in writing.","Because you did not rely on the promise in any way that cost you something.","Because friends cannot make promises to each other."],"a":2,"w":"Section 90 needs action or forbearance induced by the promise, and a real cost. Here there was a promise but no reliance and no detriment. In PRIDE terms, the I and the D are missing.","id":"estoppel-m4"},{"t":"Promissory Estoppel","k":"cl","q":"Which statement best describes how promissory estoppel relates to consideration?","o":["It is another name for consideration.","It applies only when there is already a valid contract.","It lets a court enforce any promise that seems morally deserving.","It can make a promise enforceable without consideration, where the promise induced reasonable reliance."],"a":3,"w":"Promissory estoppel is plan B. When a promise lacks consideration but the promisee reasonably relied on it at real cost, section 90 can make it binding. It is not a general licence to enforce every promise that sounds deserving.","id":"estoppel-m5"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"A courier hands a homeowner a tablet and says, \"Just sign to confirm delivery.\" The screen shows only a signature box, and there is no chance to see anything else. In fact the signature is attached to a three-year service contract. What is the status of that contract?","o":["Valid, because a signature always binds the signer.","Void, because this is fraud in the inception: he did not know he was signing a contract at all.","Voidable, because this is fraud in the inducement.","Valid, unless the homeowner can prove he suffered a loss."],"a":1,"w":"When someone is deceived about the very nature of what he is signing, there is no assent at all. That is fraud in the inception, and the contract is void, as in Duick and Toyota. Fraud in the inducement is different: you know it is a contract, but lies talked you into it.","id":"fraud-m1"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"A seller honestly believes the roof of her house was replaced last year, and tells the buyer so. She is wrong: the roof is twenty years old. The buyer relied on her statement in agreeing to the price. Can the buyer avoid the contract?","o":["No, because the seller did not intend to deceive.","No, because buyers must always inspect for themselves.","Yes, because a material misrepresentation, justifiably relied on, makes the contract voidable even if it was innocent.","Yes, but only if the buyer first proves the seller was reckless."],"a":2,"w":"Section 164 says fraudulent or material. A statement about the age of the roof would matter to any reasonable buyer, so it is material, and the buyer was justified in relying on it. One-six-four: lied to, even innocently? There's the door.","id":"fraud-m2"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"A used-car dealer tells a customer, \"This is the best little truck in the county. You'll love it.\" The truck turns out to be thoroughly ordinary. Can the customer avoid the sale for misrepresentation?","o":["No, because the statement was opinion and sales puffery, not a statement of fact.","Yes, because the statement was false.","Yes, because dealers always have superior knowledge.","No, because misrepresentation never applies to used goods."],"a":0,"w":"A misrepresentation must be a false statement of fact. Loose praise like best in the county is puffery, and a reasonable buyer does not rely on it. It would be different if the dealer had said the truck had never been in an accident.","id":"fraud-m3"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"A professional appraiser tells a first-time collector, \"In my expert opinion this vase is museum quality and you will easily resell it for double.\" The appraiser knows the vase is a common reproduction. The collector buys it. The appraiser's best defence is that he only gave an opinion. Will that defence work?","o":["Yes, because opinions can never be misrepresentations.","Yes, because the collector could have hired a second appraiser.","No, because every false opinion is automatically fraud.","No, because an opinion from a party with superior knowledge, who knows the facts do not support it, can be relied on."],"a":3,"w":"This is Vokes and Arthur Murray with a vase. Opinion is normally safe, but not when the speaker has superior knowledge and the listener reasonably trusts it. An expert who states an opinion he knows is baseless is implying facts that are false.","id":"fraud-m4"},{"t":"Fraud and Misrepresentation","k":"cl","q":"A trustee who manages land for a beneficiary offers to buy it from her at last year's valuation. He does not mention that he has just learned a new railway station will be built next door, which will triple its value. He tells no lies. She agrees. Can she avoid the sale?","o":["No, because silence is never a misrepresentation.","No, because of caveat emptor.","Yes, because a fiduciary has a duty to disclose material facts, so his silence is treated as a misrepresentation.","Yes, because all contracts between a trustee and a beneficiary are void."],"a":2,"w":"Ordinarily a party may keep quiet. But a fiduciary, a person in a relation of trust, must speak, and under section 161 his silence counts as an assertion that the fact does not exist. That was the reasoning in Wal-Mart Stores and Coughlin. The sale is voidable.","id":"fraud-m5"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"cl","q":"A restaurant owner orally promises a chef, \"You have a job here for the rest of your life.\" Two years later the chef is fired and sues. The owner raises the one-year provision of the statute of frauds. Does it bar the claim?","o":["Yes, because the chef has already worked more than a year.","Yes, because lifetime contracts always last longer than a year.","No, because the contract could have been fully performed within a year if the chef had died.","No, because employment contracts are never within the statute."],"a":2,"w":"Section 130 covers only contracts that cannot possibly be performed within a year of their making. A lifetime contract could be completed within a year, because a life can end at any time. If it can be done within a year, the statute is not here.","id":"frauds-m1"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"cl","q":"A contract within the statute of frauds is recorded in a memo signed only by the buyer. The buyer sues the seller, who signed nothing. Can the buyer enforce the contract against the seller on the strength of that memo?","o":["No, because the writing must be signed by the party to be charged, here the seller.","Yes, because one signature is enough for both parties.","Yes, because the buyer wrote the memo.","No, because both parties must always sign the same page."],"a":0,"w":"Under section 131 the writing must be signed by the party to be charged, meaning the person against whom enforcement is sought. The seller signed nothing, so the memo does not satisfy the statute against the seller. It would work against the buyer.","id":"frauds-m2"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"cl","q":"A nephew borrows $8,000 from a bank. His uncle telephones the bank and says, \"If he doesn't pay, I will.\" The nephew defaults and the bank sues the uncle. What is the uncle's best defence?","o":["The promise was made by telephone, so it was never an offer.","The debt was under $10,000.","Only contracts for land need a writing.","A promise to answer for another person's debt is within the statute of frauds and needs a signed writing."],"a":3,"w":"This is suretyship, the S in my legs. Under section 110 a promise to pay the debt of another must be evidenced by a writing signed by the promisor. The uncle's oral promise is not enforceable against him.","id":"frauds-m3"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"cl","q":"On January 1, a designer orally agrees to work for a studio for eleven months, starting on March 1. Is the agreement within the one-year provision?","o":["No, because the work itself lasts less than a year.","Yes, because performance cannot be completed within a year of the making of the contract.","No, because the year is measured from the first day of work.","Yes, because all employment contracts need a writing."],"a":1,"w":"The year is measured from the making of the contract, not from the start of performance. Eleven months of work beginning two months after the deal ends thirteen months after it was made. Under section 130 it needs a writing.","id":"frauds-m4"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"cl","q":"An employee's two-year oral contract is reflected in an unsigned office memo listing the terms, and in two payroll forms signed by company officers that state his salary. Under Crabtree v. Elizabeth Arden Sales, can these satisfy the statute of frauds?","o":["Yes, because separate writings may be read together when they clearly relate to the same transaction and at least one is signed by the party to be charged.","No, because the statute requires one single signed document.","No, because payroll forms are not contracts.","Yes, because an unsigned memo is always enough."],"a":0,"w":"Crabtree held that the memorandum may be pieced together from several writings. The signed payroll forms and the unsigned memo plainly referred to the same employment deal, so together they supplied the essential terms and the signature.","id":"frauds-m5"},{"t":"Impossibility and Frustration","k":"cl","q":"An artist agrees to paint a mural on the side of a farmer's old barn. The night before she is due to start, lightning strikes and the barn burns to the ground. Neither party is at fault, and the contract says nothing about fire. Is the artist liable for not painting the mural?","o":["Yes, because promises must be kept.","Yes, unless she offers to paint a different building.","No, because the thing necessary for performance was destroyed without her fault.","No, because contracts for art are never enforceable."],"a":2,"w":"This is Taylor and Caldwell with a barn. The contract assumed the barn would exist. When the thing essential to performance is destroyed and nobody is to blame, section 261 discharges the duty. Two-six-one: it cannot be done.","id":"impossibility-m1"},{"t":"Impossibility and Frustration","k":"cl","q":"A contractor agrees to build a garage for a fixed price of $60,000. Halfway through, timber prices rise and the job will now cost her $12,000 more than she planned. Is she excused from finishing?","o":["No. An ordinary rise in cost is a risk a fixed-price contract puts on the contractor.","Yes, because performance has become impracticable.","Yes, because her purpose of making a profit has been frustrated.","No, unless the owner agrees that timber is too expensive."],"a":0,"w":"A mere increase in cost is not impracticability. Market swings are exactly the risk that a fixed price hands to the party who quoted it. And losing your profit is not frustration of purpose, or nobody would ever finish a bad deal.","id":"impossibility-m2"},{"t":"Impossibility and Frustration","k":"cl","q":"A fan pays a deposit to rent a balcony for one afternoon, at ten times the usual rate, to watch a championship victory parade. The owner advertised the balcony for exactly that. The city then cancels the parade. The balcony is still available. Which doctrine best helps the fan avoid paying the balance?","o":["Impracticability, because the fan cannot use the balcony.","Mutual mistake, because both expected a parade.","Unconscionability, because the price was ten times the usual rate.","Frustration of purpose, because the shared reason for the deal has been destroyed."],"a":3,"w":"This is Krell and Henry. The fan can still stand on the balcony, so nothing is impossible. But the parade was the foundation of the deal, as both sides knew, and it is gone. That is section 265, frustration of purpose.","id":"impossibility-m3"},{"t":"Impossibility and Frustration","k":"cl","q":"Which statement best captures the difference between Restatement §261 and §265?","o":["§261 applies to goods and §265 applies to services.","Under §261 performance itself has become impracticable; under §265 performance is still possible but its principal purpose is gone.","§261 requires fault and §265 does not.","§261 covers events before signing and §265 covers events after signing."],"a":1,"w":"Section 261 says, I cannot do it. Section 265 says, I can, but why bother? Both deal with events after the contract is made, both require no fault, and both require that the event upset a basic assumption of the deal.","id":"impossibility-m4"},{"t":"Impossibility and Frustration","k":"cl","q":"A restaurant's lease excuses performance for \"fire, flood, war, labour strikes, or other similar causes.\" The restaurant stops paying rent after its largest catering customer goes bankrupt, and points to the clause. Is it excused?","o":["Yes, because the bankruptcy was beyond its control.","Yes, because force majeure clauses are read broadly in favour of tenants.","No, because losing a customer is not listed and is not similar to the listed events.","No, because force majeure clauses are never enforceable."],"a":2,"w":"Courts read force majeure clauses narrowly, as in Kel Kim and Central Markets. Only the listed events excuse, and catch-all words reach only things of the same kind. A customer's bankruptcy is an ordinary business risk, nothing like a fire or a flood.","id":"impossibility-m5"},{"t":"Interpretation","k":"cl","q":"A printed lease says, in its general rules, \"No animals may be kept on the premises.\" A clause typed in for this tenant says, \"Tenant may keep one cat.\" The landlord demands the cat go. Which term controls?","o":["The general rule, because it appears first.","Neither; the conflict makes the lease void.","The cat clause, because specific and separately added terms outweigh general standard language.","The general rule, because printed terms outweigh typed ones."],"a":2,"w":"Section 203 sets the pecking order. Specific terms are given greater weight than general language, and terms added for this deal outweigh standard printed ones. The cat stays.","id":"interpretation-m1"},{"t":"Interpretation","k":"cl","q":"An insurer drafts a policy excluding \"water damage\". After weighing the whole policy and all the evidence, the phrase could reasonably mean either flooding only or any leak. Neither meaning is favoured by the other rules of interpretation. How should a court read it?","o":["Against the insurer, because it drafted the ambiguous term.","In favour of the insurer, because it knows insurance best.","By striking the whole policy.","By splitting the loss equally."],"a":0,"w":"This is section 206, contra proferentem. When a term remains ambiguous, the meaning that works against the drafter is generally preferred. You wrote the mess, you get less.","id":"interpretation-m2"},{"t":"Interpretation","k":"cl","q":"In Frigaliment Importing v. B.N.S. International Sales, why did the buyer lose?","o":["Because the word \"chicken\" has only one possible meaning.","Because the contract was never signed.","Because trade usage is never admissible.","Because the buyer failed to prove that the contract used \"chicken\" in the narrower sense of young birds."],"a":3,"w":"The word was ambiguous, and the evidence pointed both ways. The buyer, who claimed the narrower meaning, bore the burden of proving it and fell short. The contract price, too low for young birds, counted against him.","id":"interpretation-m3"},{"t":"Interpretation","k":"cl","q":"A written supply contract says, \"Delivery by 9 a.m. each Monday.\" In this industry, the custom is to deliver by noon. The supplier delivers at 11:30 a.m. and points to the custom. Who is right?","o":["The supplier, because trade usage always controls.","The buyer, because an express term outranks trade usage.","The supplier, because noon is close enough.","Neither, because the contract is too uncertain."],"a":1,"w":"Under section 203, express terms sit at the top of the ladder, above course of performance, course of dealing and trade usage. Eat pizza during trials. Custom fills gaps, but it cannot override what the parties actually wrote.","id":"interpretation-m4"},{"t":"Interpretation","k":"cl","q":"Clause 4 of a contract sets a 15% royalty on all sales. Clause 5 begins, \"Notwithstanding the foregoing, the royalty on licensed uses is 50%.\" A use fits both clauses. Which rate applies?","o":["50%, because \"notwithstanding the foregoing\" makes clause 5 override what came before it.","15%, because the earlier clause controls.","The average of the two rates.","Neither, because the clauses cancel each other out."],"a":0,"w":"That was the lesson of F.B.T. Productions and Aftermath Records. Notwithstanding the foregoing signals that the clause wins over anything before it. Reading the agreement as a whole under section 202, the fifty percent rate applies.","id":"interpretation-m5"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"cl","q":"A print shop hires a technician to repair its only press by Friday. Nothing is said about deadlines. The technician finishes the following Wednesday. Only then does the shop reveal it lost a $30,000 rush order that had to print over the weekend. Can the shop recover the $30,000?","o":["Yes, because the breach in fact caused the loss.","No, because the technician had no reason to foresee that loss when the contract was made.","Yes, because consequential damages are always recoverable.","No, because lost profits can never be recovered in contract."],"a":1,"w":"This is Hadley and Baxendale, now section 351. Special losses are recoverable only if the breaching party had reason to foresee them when the contract was made. Telling the technician afterwards comes too late.","id":"limits-m1"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"cl","q":"A homeowner hires a builder to construct a garage, then clearly cancels when the work is one-third done. The builder finishes the garage anyway and sues for the full price. What can the builder recover?","o":["The full price, because the builder performed in full.","Nothing, because the builder ignored the cancellation.","The full price, less a small discount for annoying the owner.","Its costs up to the cancellation plus the profit it would have made, but nothing for work done afterwards."],"a":3,"w":"Section 350 and Rockingham County and Luten Bridge. After notice of repudiation, you cannot keep working and run up the bill. The builder gets what it had spent by then, plus its lost profit.","id":"limits-m2"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"cl","q":"When will a court enforce a clause that fixes in advance the amount payable on breach?","o":["When the amount is a reasonable forecast of the loss, given how hard that loss would be to prove.","Whenever both parties signed the contract.","Only when the clause uses the words \"liquidated damages\".","Only when the amount is larger than the actual loss, so that breach is deterred."],"a":0,"w":"Section 356 allows damages to be fixed in advance at an amount that is reasonable in light of the expected or actual loss and the difficulty of proof. A figure set unreasonably high is a penalty, and penalties are unenforceable.","id":"limits-m3"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"cl","q":"A first-time restaurateur's landlord wrongfully refuses to hand over the premises. The restaurateur claims $2 million in lost profits, based only on her own optimism. What is the best statement of the law?","o":["A new business can never recover lost profits.","Lost profits are presumed once breach is shown.","A new business may recover lost profits, but only with evidence that establishes them with reasonable certainty.","Lost profits are recoverable only if the contract states the amount."],"a":2,"w":"Section 352 requires reasonable certainty. As MindGames and Western Publishing explains, being a new business is not an automatic bar. But with no track record or other solid basis, the claim is speculation.","id":"limits-m4"},{"t":"Limits on Damages","k":"cl","q":"A caterer agrees to cater a company gala for $10,000, then cancels. The company hires a comparable caterer for $13,000. Which item is general damages, as opposed to consequential damages?","o":["Profit lost when a client, unimpressed by the gala, took its business elsewhere.","The $3,000 extra cost of the substitute caterer.","The chief executive's embarrassment.","A bonus the events manager failed to earn."],"a":1,"w":"General damages are the loss in the very thing promised, here the extra cost of getting the same catering elsewhere. Consequential damages are knock-on losses, and under section 351 they must have been foreseeable when the contract was made.","id":"limits-m5"},{"t":"Mistake","k":"cl","q":"A seller and a buyer sign a contract for a plot of land. Both believe a house can legally be built on it; that is the whole reason for the sale. Unknown to either, the land had been declared protected wetland a year earlier, and nothing can be built there. The contract says nothing about the risk. What is the buyer's best argument?","o":["Unilateral mistake, because only the buyer is hurt.","Mutual mistake: both were wrong about a basic assumption that materially affects the exchange.","The contract fails for lack of consideration.","There is no argument. A signed contract is final."],"a":1,"w":"This is section 152. Both parties shared a false belief about an existing fact, it was a basic assumption of the deal, and it changes the exchange dramatically. Mutual plus basic plus material equals voidable, so long as the buyer did not bear the risk.","id":"mistake-m1"},{"t":"Mistake","k":"cl","q":"Which of these is NOT a \"mistake\" as Restatement §151 uses the word?","o":["A buyer's belief that the shares he is buying will rise in price next year.","A buyer's belief that the painting in front of him was painted by a famous artist.","A seller's belief that the cow she is selling cannot have calves.","A builder's belief that his written bid includes the cost of the roof."],"a":0,"w":"Section 151 defines a mistake as a belief not in accord with the facts, meaning facts that exist when the contract is made. A belief about what the market will do next year is a prediction. Bad predictions are not mistakes.","id":"mistake-m2"},{"t":"Mistake","k":"cl","q":"A subcontractor's bid leaves out an entire floor of wiring because of a spreadsheet error. The bid is 45% lower than every other bid. The general contractor notices, says nothing, and quickly accepts. Can the subcontractor avoid the contract?","o":["No, because only one party was mistaken.","No, because a party always bears the risk of its own arithmetic.","Yes, because any mistake by either party makes a contract void.","Yes, because the other party knew or had reason to know of the mistake."],"a":3,"w":"Section 153 covers unilateral mistake. One side's mistake about a basic assumption with a material effect is enough if the other side knew or should have known of it, or if enforcement would be unconscionable. You may not snap up an offer you know is a slip.","id":"mistake-m3"},{"t":"Mistake","k":"cl","q":"At an estate sale, a buyer pays $50 for a locked trunk sold \"as is, contents unknown.\" He hopes it holds antiques. It holds old newspapers. Can he avoid the contract for mistake?","o":["Yes, because he was mistaken about a basic assumption.","Yes, because the seller was also unaware of the contents.","No, because he knew he did not know what was inside and bought anyway.","No, because mistake never applies to sales under $500."],"a":2,"w":"Section 154 says a party bears the risk when he knows his knowledge is limited and treats it as good enough. That is conscious ignorance, the A in car. He was not mistaken. He was gambling, and he lost.","id":"mistake-m4"},{"t":"Mistake","k":"cl","q":"Two business partners split up. Under their agreement, one takes shares that both value at $1 million, using accurate current figures. A year later the company's market collapses and the shares are worth almost nothing. Can that partner undo the agreement for mutual mistake?","o":["Yes, because both parties were wrong about what the shares were worth.","No, because a later change in value is not a mistake about the facts at the time of the contract.","Yes, because the loss is material.","No, because mistake applies only to sales of goods."],"a":1,"w":"A mistake must concern facts as they stood when the contract was made. Here the shares were worth what the parties thought, and then the world changed. As in Simkin and Blank, a later drop in value is a risk, not a mistake.","id":"mistake-m5"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"Two companies sign a letter of intent for the sale of a factory. It says the deal is \"subject to board approval and a definitive purchase agreement.\" No final agreement is ever signed. Is the letter a binding contract of sale?","o":["Yes, because both companies signed it.","No, because the \"subject to\" language shows they were still negotiating.","Yes, because letters of intent are always binding.","No, because factories cannot be sold by letter."],"a":1,"w":"Words like subject to a definitive agreement show the parties did not yet intend to be bound. That was the result in Empro and Ball-Co. A letter of intent can be binding, but not when it is full of escape hatches.","id":"offer-m1"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"Mia tells Noah, \"I'll sell you my bike for $200.\" Owen, standing nearby, overhears and says, \"I accept!\" before Noah answers. Has Owen formed a contract with Mia?","o":["Yes, because he accepted first.","Yes, because the price was definite.","No, because the offer was too vague.","No, because only the person the offer was made to can accept it."],"a":3,"w":"Under section 29 the offeror is master of the offer and decides who may accept. Mia invited Noah, not Owen. A bystander who overhears an offer has no power to accept it.","id":"offer-m2"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"A dealership's online ad says, \"Used cars from $5,000! Great deals all weekend!\" A customer walks in and demands a car for $5,000. Is the dealership bound?","o":["No, because the ad is an invitation to deal and names no particular car or terms.","Yes, because every advertisement is an offer.","Yes, because the customer came in person.","No, because car sales must always be negotiated."],"a":0,"w":"An ad is an offer only if it is clear, definite, and leaves nothing to negotiate. This one does not say which car, how many, or who gets it. It is an invitation to come in and make an offer.","id":"offer-m3"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"A poster reads, \"$100 reward for the safe return of Biscuit, a lost beagle.\" A neighbour who has seen the poster finds Biscuit and brings him home. What best describes the poster?","o":["An invitation to deal with no legal effect.","A gift promise that cannot be enforced.","An offer that could be accepted by performing the requested act.","A bilateral contract formed when the poster went up."],"a":2,"w":"A reward is an offer for a unilateral contract, like the smoke ball advertisement. It asks for an act, not a promise. The neighbour accepted by returning the dog, so the owner owes the reward.","id":"offer-m4"},{"t":"The Offer","k":"cl","q":"A landowner says to a friend, \"I'll sell you some of my land at a fair price sometime next year.\" The friend says, \"I accept.\" Why is there most likely no contract?","o":["Because the friend did not pay a deposit.","Because the terms are too uncertain for a court to enforce.","Because offers to sell land are illegal.","Because the landowner was secretly joking."],"a":1,"w":"Section 33 requires terms certain enough for a court to find a breach and give a remedy. Here we do not know which land, what price, or when. Thirty-three: the court cannot see what the deal should be.","id":"offer-m5"},{"t":"Online Contracts","k":"cl","q":"A shopping site has a small grey \"Terms\" link in the footer of every page. The terms contain an arbitration clause. A customer buys a lamp without ever scrolling to the footer or being told that terms apply. Is the customer bound to arbitrate?","o":["Yes, because using a website always means accepting its terms.","Probably not, because the customer had no reasonable notice that any terms existed.","Yes, because the customer had a duty to read every page.","No, because arbitration clauses are never enforceable online."],"a":1,"w":"This is browsewrap. As in Specht and Netscape, terms bind only if a reasonable user would have noticed them. A faint footer link, with nothing drawing attention to it, gives no reasonable notice, so there is no assent to the arbitration clause.","id":"online-m1"},{"t":"Online Contracts","k":"cl","q":"To create an account, a user must tick a box beside the words \"I have read and agree to the Terms of Service\", with the terms linked. The user ticks the box without opening the link. Is the user bound by the terms?","o":["No, because the user never opened the terms.","No, because online contracts need a handwritten signature.","Only if the user later reads the terms.","Yes, because ticking the box is an outward sign of assent, and the user was told terms applied."],"a":3,"w":"This is clickwrap. The user had clear notice that terms applied and took an action showing assent. The duty to read means that choosing not to open the link is no excuse. Click, it sticks.","id":"online-m2"},{"t":"Online Contracts","k":"cl","q":"What does \"inquiry notice\" mean in online contracting?","o":["A reasonable person in the user's position would have realised that terms existed and could have looked them up.","The user actually read and understood every term.","The company emailed the user a copy of the terms after the purchase.","The user asked customer service a question about the terms."],"a":0,"w":"Actual notice means you really knew of the terms. Inquiry notice means the circumstances would alert a reasonable person that terms exist, as with the link under the sign-up button in Fteja and Facebook. Either kind of notice can support assent.","id":"online-m3"},{"t":"Online Contracts","k":"cl","q":"In Specht v. Netscape, why were the users not bound by the arbitration clause in the software licence?","o":["Because arbitration clauses in free software are illegal.","Because the users were under eighteen.","Because the licence terms were visible only by scrolling below the download button, so a reasonable user would not have known of them.","Because the users clicked \"I do not agree\"."],"a":2,"w":"The licence terms sat below the download button, on a part of the page users had no reason to visit. Clicking download did not clearly show assent to terms nobody was told about. No reasonable notice means no assent.","id":"online-m4"},{"t":"Online Contracts","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following is NOT a typical feature of arbitration?","o":["Limited rights of appeal.","A jury decides the dispute.","Less discovery than in court.","The decision is usually not explained in a public opinion."],"a":1,"w":"Remember jade: no jury, almost no appeal, limited discovery, and no public explanation. An arbitrator decides, not a jury. That is why it matters so much whether the user ever agreed to the arbitration clause.","id":"online-m5"},{"t":"Parol Evidence Rule","k":"cl","q":"Lena buys a used car under a detailed signed contract that says, \"This writing is the complete and exclusive statement of the parties' agreement.\" It does not mention a roof rack. Lena wants to testify that, the day before signing, the seller orally promised to include one. Is the testimony admissible to add the term?","o":["Yes, because oral promises are always enforceable.","No, because a completely integrated writing discharges earlier agreements within its scope.","Yes, because the roof rack does not contradict the writing.","No, because car sales can never include extras."],"a":1,"w":"Under section 213, a completely integrated agreement discharges prior agreements within its scope, even consistent ones. The merger clause is strong evidence of complete integration. A slice can take extras; the whole pie cannot.","id":"parol-m1"},{"t":"Parol Evidence Rule","k":"cl","q":"A signed contract for the sale of a boat contains a merger clause. The buyer offers evidence that, before signing, the seller knowingly lied about the engine having been rebuilt. Does the parol evidence rule bar this evidence?","o":["Yes, because the merger clause makes the writing complete.","Yes, because the lie was told before signing.","No, but only if the lie was in writing.","No, because evidence of fraud is admissible even when the writing is integrated."],"a":3,"w":"Section 214 allows evidence of prior negotiations to show fraud, duress, mistake or other invalidating causes. That is the F in firm. The rule protects valid final writings; it does not shelter a contract obtained by fraud.","id":"parol-m2"},{"t":"Parol Evidence Rule","k":"cl","q":"Two weeks after signing a fully integrated catering contract, the parties agree by phone to move the event from Friday to Saturday. The caterer later argues the parol evidence rule bars proof of the phone call. Is the caterer right?","o":["No, because the rule covers only agreements made before or at the time of the writing.","Yes, because the writing was fully integrated.","Yes, because phone calls are parol evidence.","No, because catering contracts are exempt from the rule."],"a":0,"w":"The parol evidence rule deals with prior and contemporaneous agreements. A later agreement is a modification, and the rule does not touch it. Whether the modification is itself enforceable is a separate question.","id":"parol-m3"},{"t":"Parol Evidence Rule","k":"cl","q":"Under Pacific Gas & Electric v. G.W. Thomas Drayage, when may a court admit extrinsic evidence to interpret a written contract?","o":["Only when the judge finds the words ambiguous on their face.","Never, if the contract is signed.","When the evidence is relevant to prove a meaning to which the language is reasonably susceptible.","Only when both parties consent."],"a":2,"w":"The California court rejected a pure plain-meaning approach, because words take their meaning from context. Evidence comes in if it shows a meaning the language could reasonably bear. New York, in W.W.W. Associates, keeps to the four corners when the writing is clear.","id":"parol-m4"},{"t":"Parol Evidence Rule","k":"cl","q":"In Mitchill v. Lath, why was the sellers' oral promise to remove the icehouse not enforceable?","o":["Because the promise contradicted the price term.","Because it was the kind of term the parties would ordinarily be expected to include in the written contract of sale.","Because promises about buildings are never collateral.","Because Mrs Mitchill had not paid for the farm."],"a":1,"w":"A collateral oral agreement survives only if it is collateral in form, does not contradict the writing, and is not something the parties would ordinarily put in the writing. The icehouse promise failed the third test, because it was so closely tied to the sale of the farm.","id":"parol-m5"},{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"both","q":"A bakery hires a freelance designer to create a new logo for $3,000. The designer delivers the finished logo as a digital file, and a dispute arises over the fee. Which body of law governs?","o":["UCC Article 2, because the bakery is a merchant.","UCC Article 2, because a file was delivered.","The common law of contracts, because this is a contract for services.","Neither, because creative work cannot be the subject of a contract."],"a":2,"w":"Article 2 covers sales of goods, which UCC section 2-105 defines as movable things. Designing a logo is a service, so the common law applies. Being a business does not pull a service contract into the UCC.","id":"rulebook-m1"},{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"both","q":"What is the legal status of the Restatement (Second) of Contracts?","o":["It is persuasive authority that courts often follow, but it is not binding law.","It is a federal statute that binds every court.","It is a state statute adopted everywhere except Louisiana.","It binds only merchants."],"a":0,"w":"The Restatement is a scholarly summary of the common law. Courts respect it and often adopt its rules, but it is not a statute. The Restatement suggests. The UCC commands.","id":"rulebook-m2"},{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"both","q":"A contractor agrees to refinish a homeowner's floors for $7,000: $6,200 for labour and $800 for varnish and other materials. In a state that uses the predominant purpose test, which law governs the contract?","o":["The UCC governs the varnish and the common law governs the labour.","The UCC, because goods were supplied.","The UCC, because the contractor is a professional.","The common law, because the deal is mainly for services."],"a":3,"w":"The predominant purpose test asks what the deal is mainly about, and then applies one rulebook to the whole contract. Here the labour dwarfs the materials, so it is a service contract. Common law governs all of it.","id":"rulebook-m3"},{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"both","q":"A salon charges a customer $60 for a haircut and $15 for a bottle of shampoo in a single transaction. The shampoo turns out to be defective. In a state that uses the gravamen of the complaint test, which law applies to the customer's claim?","o":["The common law, because most of the price was for the haircut.","The UCC, because the complaint is about the goods.","The common law, because salons provide services.","Neither, because the amount is too small."],"a":1,"w":"The gravamen test looks at what the complaint is actually about. The problem is the shampoo, which is goods, so the UCC applies. Under the predominant purpose test, the answer could well be different.","id":"rulebook-m4"},{"t":"Which rulebook?","k":"both","q":"A retired teacher sells her own used piano to a neighbour. It is the only piano she has ever sold. Which statement is correct?","o":["The UCC does not apply, because she is not a merchant.","The UCC applies, and she is a merchant because she sold goods.","The UCC applies, but the rules reserved for merchants do not apply to her.","The common law applies, because the piano is used."],"a":2,"w":"Article 2 covers every sale of goods, whoever the parties are. But under UCC section 2-104 a merchant is someone who deals in goods of that kind. A one-off seller is not a merchant, so merchant-only rules stay out.","id":"rulebook-m5"},{"t":"Specific Performance","k":"cl","q":"A seller signs a contract to sell her lakeside cottage, then refuses to close because a higher offer has arrived. The buyer asks the court to order the sale to go through. What is the likely result?","o":["Denied, because the buyer can be paid the difference in price.","Denied, because courts never order anyone to perform a contract.","Granted, because land is treated as unique and money is an inadequate substitute.","Granted, but only if the buyer proves the seller acted in bad faith."],"a":2,"w":"Under section 359, specific performance is available where damages are inadequate. Land is the classic example, because every parcel is treated as unique. No sum of money buys that particular cottage.","id":"specific-m1"},{"t":"Specific Performance","k":"cl","q":"A celebrated chef signs a two-year contract to cook at a restaurant, then walks out after a month. The restaurant asks for an order compelling the chef to return to the kitchen. Why will the court refuse?","o":["Because employment contracts are never binding.","Because courts will not specifically enforce a promise of personal service.","Because the restaurant suffered no loss.","Because only land contracts can ever be specifically enforced."],"a":1,"w":"Section 367 says a promise to render personal service will not be specifically enforced. A court cannot supervise the cooking, and compelled labour raises involuntary servitude concerns under the Thirteenth Amendment. The restaurant is left to damages.","id":"specific-m2"},{"t":"Specific Performance","k":"cl","q":"According to the reasoning in Van Wagner Advertising v. S&M Enterprises, when is the subject of a contract \"unique\" enough to support specific performance?","o":["Whenever it is physically different from every other item.","Whenever the injured party says it has sentimental value.","Whenever it is an interest in real property, including a lease.","When its value is so uncertain that damages risk under-compensating the injured party."],"a":3,"w":"Van Wagner says almost everything is physically unique. What matters is how uncertain the valuation is. Billboard space had a market price, so damages were adequate, and specific performance was refused.","id":"specific-m3"},{"t":"Specific Performance","k":"cl","q":"An investor contracts to buy 100 shares of a company whose stock trades every day on a public exchange. The seller refuses to deliver. Will a court order specific performance?","o":["No, because the investor can buy identical shares on the market and recover any price difference in damages.","Yes, because every share certificate has its own number.","Yes, because investment contracts are always specifically enforced.","No, because contracts for shares are not enforceable at all."],"a":0,"w":"Section 360 asks whether a substitute is easy to get and the loss easy to value. Here both are true. Identical shares are on sale every day, so damages are adequate under section 359.","id":"specific-m4"},{"t":"Specific Performance","k":"cl","q":"A court finds that a tenant's lease was wrongfully cancelled. Restoring the lease would block the owner's $50 million redevelopment, while the tenant's loss can be valued at about $30,000. What is the court likely to do?","o":["Order specific performance, because breach always leads to it.","Refuse any remedy, because the owner's project matters more.","Award damages, because equity will not impose hardship far out of proportion to the benefit.","Order specific performance, but only for one year."],"a":2,"w":"Specific performance is an equitable remedy. As in Van Wagner, a court will refuse it where it would impose a burden on the breaching party out of all proportion to the benefit. The tenant still gets damages.","id":"specific-m5"},{"t":"Substantial Performance","k":"cl","q":"A contract for a new house calls for \"Volta brand\" wiring. By an honest oversight the electrician installs another brand of identical quality. Replacing it would mean opening every wall at a cost of $40,000. The house's value is unaffected. The owner refuses to pay the final $30,000. What result?","o":["The owner owes nothing, because the contract was not performed exactly.","The builder must replace the wiring before being paid anything.","The owner must pay, less the difference in value, which is little or nothing.","The owner must pay, but may deduct the $40,000 cost of replacement."],"a":2,"w":"This is Jacob and Youngs and Kent with wires for pipes. The deviation was innocent and trivial, so the builder substantially performed. Because the cost of replacement is grossly out of proportion, damages are the difference in value.","id":"substantial-m1"},{"t":"Substantial Performance","k":"cl","q":"When one party to a construction contract has substantially performed, the other party:","o":["Must still perform, but may recover damages for the defects.","May treat the contract as ended and pay nothing.","Must pay the full price with no deduction.","May demand that the work be redone at any cost."],"a":0,"w":"Close enough gets paid, less the damage made. Substantial performance keeps the other side's duty to pay alive, but it does not erase the breach. Only a material breach lets the other party refuse to perform.","id":"substantial-m2"},{"t":"Substantial Performance","k":"cl","q":"A company leases land to extract gravel and promises to leave it graded level. To save money, it deliberately leaves the land rutted. Grading would cost $60,000 and the graded land would be worth $12,000. Under Groves v. John Wunder Co., what does the landowner recover?","o":["Nothing, because the land has little value.","$12,000, the value of the graded land.","Only nominal damages.","$60,000, the cost of doing the promised work."],"a":3,"w":"Groves awarded the cost of completion. The breach was wilful, and the court would not let a deliberate breacher shelter behind the low value of the land. Compare Peevyhouse, which went the other way.","id":"substantial-m3"},{"t":"Substantial Performance","k":"cl","q":"In Peevyhouse v. Garland Coal, why did the court limit the farmers to $300?","o":["Because the farmers had not performed their own side of the lease.","Because the cost of restoration was grossly out of proportion to the value it would add, and the promise was treated as incidental.","Because the coal company's breach was innocent.","Because the lease never mentioned restoring the land."],"a":1,"w":"The court called restoration an incidental term and said twenty-nine thousand dollars of work for three hundred dollars of value was economic waste. So it awarded diminution in value. Critics reply that the farmers bargained for that promise and the breach was deliberate.","id":"substantial-m4"},{"t":"Substantial Performance","k":"cl","q":"A builder walks off the job leaving a house with no roof and no plumbing, then demands the full contract price less a small allowance. Which statement is most accurate?","o":["This is a material breach, so the owner may withhold payment and treat the contract as ended.","This is substantial performance, because most of the walls are up.","The owner must pay in full and sue separately.","The builder wins, because construction contracts are never forfeited."],"a":0,"w":"Substantial performance needs the purpose of the contract to be served, with only a petty defect. A house with no roof fails that test. This is a material breach, and the owner's duty to pay does not come due.","id":"substantial-m5"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"A shop contracts to buy 500 phone cases at $4 each ($2,000). The seller repudiates. On the day the shop learns of this, the market price is $5 each. The shop promptly and reasonably buys 500 substitute cases from another supplier at $5.50 each ($2,750). Ignoring incidentals, what may the shop recover?","o":["$500, the market price minus the contract price.","$750, the cover price minus the contract price.","$2,750, the full cost of the substitute cases.","Nothing, because it ended up with the phone cases it needed."],"a":1,"w":"The shop covered, so section 2-712 applies: cover price minus contract price. Twenty-seven fifty minus two thousand is seven hundred and fifty dollars. The cover only has to be reasonable and in good faith, not the cheapest available.","id":"ucc-buyer-m1"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"A buyer contracts for a load of steel at $10,000. The seller repudiates, and the buyer does not buy substitute steel. The market price was $10,000 when the contract was made, $12,000 when the buyer learned of the breach, and $15,000 at trial. What are the buyer's market damages?","o":["$2,000.","$5,000.","$0.","$15,000."],"a":0,"w":"Under section 2-713, a buyer who does not cover gets the market price at the time the buyer learned of the breach, minus the contract price. Twelve thousand minus ten thousand is two thousand. The price at trial is irrelevant.","id":"ucc-buyer-m2"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"A seller fails to deliver a standard motor part to a factory, knowing the factory needs it to keep its line running. An identical part was available from a local supplier the next morning, but the factory did nothing for three weeks and now claims three weeks of lost profits. Can it recover them?","o":["Yes, because the seller knew of the factory's needs.","Yes, because consequential damages are automatic under the UCC.","No, because buyers can never recover lost profits.","No, because the loss could reasonably have been prevented by cover."],"a":3,"w":"Section 2-715 allows consequential damages only for losses the seller had reason to know about and that could not reasonably be prevented by cover or otherwise. The factory could have covered the next morning. It sat still, so the lost profits are on the factory.","id":"ucc-buyer-m3"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"both","q":"How do the UCC's buyer's remedies relate to common-law expectation damages?","o":["They are unrelated; the UCC aims to punish the breaching seller.","The UCC gives only restitution, while the common law gives expectation.","They share the same goal of putting the buyer where performance would have, and the UCC supplies specific formulas for goods.","The common law gives more, because the UCC bars all consequential damages."],"a":2,"w":"Both systems protect the expectation interest. The UCC simply spells out how to measure it for goods: cover under section 2-712, market under section 2-713, and the extras under section 2-715. Same destination, clearer road signs.","id":"ucc-buyer-m4"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"A farmer pays $38,000 for a tractor warranted to pull a certain load. It cannot. The farmer keeps the tractor and promptly notifies the seller. As warranted, the tractor would have been worth $40,000; as delivered, it is worth $31,000. What is the basic measure of damages?","o":["$9,000, the value as warranted minus the value as delivered.","$7,000, the price paid minus the value as delivered.","$38,000, a full refund.","$2,000, the value as warranted minus the price paid."],"a":0,"w":"For goods the buyer has accepted and kept, section 2-714 measures damages as the value the goods would have had as warranted, minus their value as accepted. Forty thousand minus thirty-one thousand is nine thousand. The price paid is not the yardstick.","id":"ucc-buyer-m5"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"ucc","q":"A wholesaler contracts to deliver 500 standard cardboard boxes to a shop. Without asking the shop, the wholesaler delegates the job to another supplier, who delivers boxes that fall apart. Whom may the shop hold liable for the breach?","o":["Nobody, because the delegation was valid.","Only the other supplier, because the wholesaler handed over the duty.","The wholesaler, because delegating performance does not relieve the delegator of liability.","Nobody, because the shop never consented to the delegation."],"a":2,"w":"Under section 2-210, a duty to deliver ordinary goods can be delegated, but the delegation does not relieve the delegator of the duty to perform or of liability for breach. Delegate the chore, but you are liable as before.","id":"ucc-delegation-m1"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"ucc","q":"A shampoo maker appoints a firm as its exclusive distributor for a region, which carries a duty to use best efforts to sell the shampoo. The firm is then bought by a subsidiary of the maker's biggest competitor, which takes over the distributorship. The maker never consented. Under the majority view in Sally Beauty v. Nexxus, may the maker refuse the new distributor?","o":["Yes, because the maker has a substantial interest in not having a competitor perform the best-efforts duty.","No, because every duty under a sale of goods contract can be delegated.","No, because the maker can only sue the original firm for damages.","Yes, because distributorship contracts can never be transferred to anyone."],"a":0,"w":"Section 2-210 bars delegation where the other party has a substantial interest in having the original promisor perform. In Sally Beauty, the court held that an exclusive distributor's best-efforts duty cannot be delegated to a competitor's subsidiary without consent.","id":"ucc-delegation-m2"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"ucc","q":"A buyer of machine parts learns that its seller has delegated production to a firm the buyer has never heard of. The contract does not prohibit delegation. What may the buyer do under the UCC?","o":["Cancel at once, because any delegation is a breach.","Nothing at all until the parts arrive.","Refuse to pay the original seller and pay only the delegate.","Treat the delegation as reasonable grounds for insecurity and demand assurances from the delegate."],"a":3,"w":"Section 2-210 lets the other party treat a delegation as creating reasonable grounds for insecurity. The buyer may then demand adequate assurances under section 2-609, without giving up its rights against the original seller. This is the tool Judge Posner pointed to.","id":"ucc-delegation-m3"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"both","q":"Which statement is true of delegation under both Restatement §318 and UCC §2-210?","o":["Delegation always requires the other party's written consent.","A duty may be delegated unless the other party has a substantial interest in the original person performing, and the delegator remains liable.","Once a duty is delegated, the delegator is automatically released.","Only duties to pay money can ever be delegated."],"a":1,"w":"The common law and the UCC are twins here. Under Restatement section 318 and UCC section 2-210, duties are generally delegable unless personal performance matters to the other side. Either way, the delegator stays on the hook unless there is a novation.","id":"ucc-delegation-m4"},{"t":"Assignment and Delegation","k":"ucc","q":"A furniture maker delivers 30 tables to a hotel, which owes $9,000 in sixty days. The maker assigns its right to that payment to a bank. The hotel objects that it never agreed to deal with a bank. Is the assignment effective?","o":["No, because rights under a sale of goods contract are personal.","No, because the hotel did not consent.","Yes, because paying the bank instead of the maker does not materially change the hotel's duty, burden or risk.","Yes, but only if the bank also takes over the duty to deliver tables."],"a":2,"w":"Under section 2-210, rights can be assigned unless the assignment would materially change the other party's duty, or materially increase its burden or risk. Paying the same sum to a different payee changes nothing that matters. Remember D B R.","id":"ucc-delegation-m5"},{"t":"Firm Offers","k":"ucc","q":"A tyre wholesaler sends a retailer a signed letter: \"We offer 400 tyres at $60 each. This offer will be held open for 30 days.\" The retailer pays nothing for the promise. On day 10 the wholesaler tries to revoke. On day 12 the retailer accepts. Is there a contract?","o":["No, because the retailer gave no consideration for the 30-day promise.","Yes, because the letter was a firm offer that could not be revoked during the 30 days.","No, because an offer can always be revoked before acceptance.","Yes, but only if the retailer relied on the offer to its detriment."],"a":1,"w":"This is section 2-205. A merchant, a signed writing, and an assurance that the offer will be held open. SAM is all there, so the offer is irrevocable for the stated time without any consideration.","id":"ucc-firm-offer-m1"},{"t":"Firm Offers","k":"ucc","q":"A merchant's signed written offer to sell goods states: \"This offer will remain open for eight months.\" No consideration is paid. For how long is the offer irrevocable under the UCC?","o":["Eight months, as stated.","A reasonable time, decided by the court.","Not at all, because the stated period is too long.","Three months."],"a":3,"w":"Section 2-205 caps the period of irrevocability at three months. The firm part of the offer ends there. After that the offer may still be open, but the merchant is free to revoke it.","id":"ucc-firm-offer-m2"},{"t":"Firm Offers","k":"both","q":"Dana, an accountant, writes and signs a note to her neighbour: \"I'll sell you my lawnmower for $150. I promise to keep this offer open until Sunday.\" On Friday, before the neighbour answers, Dana says she has changed her mind. Can she revoke?","o":["Yes, because she is not a merchant and received no consideration for her promise.","No, because the promise was in a signed writing.","No, because the UCC makes every written offer to sell goods irrevocable.","Yes, but only because a lawnmower costs less than $500."],"a":0,"w":"Section 2-205 only covers offers made by merchants. Dana is not one, so the common-law rule applies: without consideration for an option, an offer can be revoked any time before acceptance, as in Dickinson and Dodds.","id":"ucc-firm-offer-m3"},{"t":"Firm Offers","k":"ucc","q":"A paper supplier tells a print shop by phone, \"I'll sell you 100 reams at $4 each, and I'll keep that offer open all week.\" Nothing is put in writing. Is this a firm offer under the UCC?","o":["Yes, because the supplier is a merchant.","Yes, because the period is shorter than three months.","No, because a firm offer needs a signed writing.","No, because the price is under $500."],"a":2,"w":"A firm offer under section 2-205 must be in a signed writing that gives assurance it will be held open. An oral promise by a merchant is not enough, so the supplier can still revoke before acceptance.","id":"ucc-firm-offer-m4"},{"t":"Firm Offers","k":"ucc","q":"A buyer sends a seller the buyer's own pre-printed order form. Buried in the form is a clause: \"Seller agrees to hold this offer open for 45 days.\" The seller, a merchant, signs once at the bottom of the form. Is the hold-open clause binding as a firm offer?","o":["Yes, because one signature covers every clause on the form.","No, because the clause is on a form supplied by the offeree and was not separately signed by the seller.","Yes, because both parties are merchants.","No, because firm offers may never appear on a pre-printed form."],"a":1,"w":"Section 2-205 says that an assurance on a form supplied by the offeree must be separately signed by the offeror. The rule protects merchants from being locked in by a clause they never noticed.","id":"ucc-firm-offer-m5"},{"t":"UCC Formation","k":"ucc","q":"A shop orders 200 white mugs \"for prompt shipment.\" The seller, out of white, ships 200 green mugs with no explanation. What is the legal effect of the shipment?","o":["It is a counteroffer that the shop may accept or reject.","It has no effect, because the goods do not match the order.","It is both an acceptance of the order and a breach of the contract.","It is a rejection of the shop's offer."],"a":2,"w":"Under section 2-206, shipping non-conforming goods in response to an order for prompt shipment is an acceptance, and a breach at the same time. The seller could have avoided that by telling the shop the green mugs were only an accommodation.","id":"ucc-formation-m1"},{"t":"UCC Formation","k":"ucc","q":"Two merchants sign a deal for 1,000 bolts and clearly intend to be bound, but say nothing about price. Under the UCC, what is the price?","o":["A reasonable price at the time for delivery.","Whatever price the seller names on the invoice.","The lowest price either party suggested in negotiations.","There is no price, so there is no contract."],"a":0,"w":"Section 2-305 says that if the parties intend a contract and leave the price open, the price is a reasonable price at the time for delivery. Three-oh-five: the zero is a blank price tag, and the court fills it in.","id":"ucc-formation-m2"},{"t":"UCC Formation","k":"both","q":"Parties intend to be bound but leave the price \"to be agreed later,\" and then cannot agree. How do the traditional common-law approach and the UCC differ?","o":["Both treat the deal as unenforceable.","Both let the court supply a reasonable price in every case.","The common law supplies a price; the UCC refuses to.","The common law tends to call it an unenforceable agreement to agree; the UCC supplies a reasonable price."],"a":3,"w":"At common law, an agreement to agree on a key term like price is often too uncertain to enforce, as in the deli lease case. For goods, sections 2-204 and 2-305 keep the deal alive and fill the gap with a reasonable price, so long as the parties intended to be bound.","id":"ucc-formation-m3"},{"t":"UCC Formation","k":"ucc","q":"A cafe has a requirements contract for coffee beans and has always needed about 50 bags a month. When the market price doubles, it demands 900 bags so it can resell them at a profit. Must the seller deliver 900?","o":["Yes, because a requirements contract has no quantity limit.","No, because the demand is not in good faith and is unreasonably disproportionate to past requirements.","Yes, because the seller took the risk of price changes.","No, because requirements contracts are unenforceable for lack of a quantity."],"a":1,"w":"Section 2-306 enforces requirements contracts, but the quantity means actual requirements in good faith, and nothing unreasonably disproportionate to an estimate or to normal past amounts. All I need, in good faith, without the greed.","id":"ucc-formation-m4"},{"t":"UCC Formation","k":"ucc","q":"A grower and a grocer never sign anything and cannot say exactly when they made their deal. For two years the grower has delivered apples weekly and the grocer has paid each invoice. Is there a contract for sale?","o":["No, because the moment of formation cannot be identified.","No, because a contract for goods always needs a signed offer and acceptance.","Yes, because conduct by both parties that recognises a contract is enough.","Yes, but only if a court can find a written purchase order."],"a":2,"w":"Section 2-204 says a contract for goods may be made in any manner sufficient to show agreement, including conduct, even if the moment of its making is undetermined. Agreement first, formalities second.","id":"ucc-formation-m5"},{"t":"Battle of the Forms","k":"both","q":"A buyer's purchase order for 500 valves is answered by a seller's acknowledgment that agrees on goods, price and quantity but adds a clause charging interest on late payments. Nothing is shipped yet. Is there a contract?","o":["Under both the common law and the UCC, yes.","Under both the common law and the UCC, no.","Under the common law mirror image rule, no; under UCC 2-207(1), yes.","Under the common law, yes; under the UCC, no."],"a":2,"w":"At common law the added clause makes the reply a rejection and counteroffer, because it does not mirror the offer. Under section 2-207, subsection one, a definite and timely expression of acceptance is an acceptance even though it states additional terms.","id":"ucc-forms-m1"},{"t":"Battle of the Forms","k":"ucc","q":"Two merchants exchange forms and a contract is formed under 2-207(1). The seller's acceptance adds a new clause. The buyer's original purchase order stated: \"Acceptance is limited to the terms of this order.\" Does the seller's new clause become part of the contract?","o":["No, because the offer expressly limited acceptance to its own terms.","Yes, because both parties are merchants.","Yes, unless the clause materially alters the contract.","No, because additional terms never become part of a contract."],"a":0,"w":"Between merchants, additional terms come in unless L M O. Here it is the L: the offer expressly limited acceptance to its own terms, so under section 2-207, subsection two, the seller's proposal stays out.","id":"ucc-forms-m2"},{"t":"Battle of the Forms","k":"ucc","q":"A seller's acknowledgment states in bold: \"Our acceptance is expressly conditional on your assent to the terms on this form.\" The buyer never assents, but the seller ships and the buyer accepts and pays for the goods. What are the terms of the contract?","o":["The seller's terms, because its form was the last one sent.","The buyer's terms, because the buyer made the offer.","There is no contract, because the forms never matched.","The terms on which both forms agree, plus UCC gap-fillers."],"a":3,"w":"The expressly conditional language means the writings did not form a contract under subsection one. But conduct by both parties did. Under section 2-207, subsection three, the terms are those the writings agree on, plus the UCC's supplementary terms. No last shot.","id":"ucc-forms-m3"},{"t":"Battle of the Forms","k":"ucc","q":"A merchant buyer's form says the seller warrants the goods. The merchant seller's acceptance says all implied warranties are disclaimed. A contract is formed under 2-207(1). Under the majority \"knockout\" approach, what happens to the warranty terms?","o":["The seller's disclaimer controls as the later term.","Both conflicting terms drop out and the UCC's implied warranties fill the gap.","The buyer's term controls because the buyer was the offeror.","The whole contract fails for lack of agreement on warranties."],"a":1,"w":"Under the knockout rule, different terms that conflict cancel each other out, and the UCC's gap-fillers take their place. The gap-filler here is the implied warranty of merchantability. When terms collide, both step aside.","id":"ucc-forms-m4"},{"t":"Battle of the Forms","k":"ucc","q":"A consumer orders a sofa from a furniture store. The store's written acceptance agrees to the order and adds a clause requiring the consumer to pay a restocking fee on any return. The consumer says nothing about the clause. Is the restocking clause part of the contract?","o":["Yes, because the consumer did not object within a reasonable time.","Yes, because the clause does not materially alter the deal.","No, because with a non-merchant an additional term is only a proposal, which stays out unless agreed to.","No, because the store's reply was a counteroffer."],"a":2,"w":"The automatic rule for additional terms in section 2-207, subsection two, applies only between merchants. When one party is not a merchant, additional terms are just proposals, and they stay out of the contract unless that party actually agrees.","id":"ucc-forms-m5"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"ucc","q":"A seller and buyer orally agree on the sale of 300 lamps for $6,000. The only writing is a note signed by the buyer: \"Confirming our lamp deal — 120 lamps.\" The buyer later refuses to take any lamps. To what extent can the seller enforce the contract?","o":["For all 300 lamps, because the oral agreement controls.","Not at all, because the writing gets the quantity wrong.","Not at all, because the note does not state the price.","For 120 lamps, the quantity stated in the writing."],"a":3,"w":"Under section 2-201, subsection one, a writing is good enough even if it leaves out or misstates a term, but the contract is not enforceable beyond the quantity shown in the writing. The price can be missing. The quantity is the ceiling.","id":"ucc-frauds-m1"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"ucc","q":"Two merchants agree by phone on the sale of $9,000 of steel. The next day the seller emails a signed confirmation stating the quantity. The buyer reads it and does nothing. Three weeks later the buyer denies the deal and raises the statute of frauds. Result?","o":["The defence fails, because the buyer did not object in writing within 10 days of receiving the confirmation.","The defence succeeds, because the buyer never signed anything.","The defence succeeds, because phone deals for goods are never enforceable.","The defence fails, because merchants are exempt from the statute of frauds."],"a":0,"w":"This is the merchant's confirmation rule in section 2-201, subsection two. Between merchants, a written confirmation that the recipient has reason to know about binds the recipient unless they object in writing within ten days. Ten days to say no way.","id":"ucc-frauds-m2"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"ucc","q":"A restaurant orally orders $3,000 of dinner plates stamped with its own crest. After the manufacturer has made half of them, the restaurant cancels and points out that nothing was signed. Can the manufacturer enforce the contract?","o":["No, because the price is over $500 and there is no signed writing.","Yes, because the goods are specially manufactured, unsuitable for sale to others, and substantially begun.","No, because only merchants can rely on oral contracts.","Yes, because restaurants are always bound by oral orders."],"a":1,"w":"Section 2-201, subsection three, makes an exception for specially manufactured goods that cannot be sold to others in the ordinary course, once the seller has made a substantial beginning. That is the S in SAP.","id":"ucc-frauds-m3"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"both","q":"Which statement best compares the writing required by UCC 2-201 with the writing traditionally required by the common-law statute of frauds?","o":["They are identical: both need every term of the deal.","The UCC is stricter: the writing must state the price and the delivery date.","The UCC is more relaxed: the writing must show a contract, be signed, and state a quantity, even if other terms are missing.","The common law requires no signature, but the UCC does."],"a":2,"w":"The common-law statute of frauds generally looks for a signed memorandum stating the essential terms. Section 2-201 asks for much less: a signed writing showing that a contract for sale was made, with a quantity. Other terms may be missing or even wrong.","id":"ucc-frauds-m4"},{"t":"Statute of Frauds","k":"ucc","q":"A buyer orally agrees to buy 10 bicycles for $2,000. The seller delivers 4 bicycles, which the buyer accepts and keeps. There is no writing. The buyer refuses the other 6. For how many bicycles is the contract enforceable?","o":["None, because there is no writing.","Only the 4 bicycles that were received and accepted.","All 10, because part performance proves the whole contract.","All 10, but only if the buyer is a merchant."],"a":1,"w":"Under section 2-201, subsection three, an oral contract is enforceable with respect to goods that have been received and accepted, or paid for with the payment accepted. The exception reaches only those goods, not the rest of the order.","id":"ucc-frauds-m5"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"A seller contracts to sell 20 desks at $300 each ($6,000). The buyer repudiates before delivery. After notifying the buyer, the seller resells the desks in a commercially reasonable sale for $250 each ($5,000), and spends $200 storing them in the meantime. What may the seller recover?","o":["$1,000.","$1,200.","$6,000.","$800."],"a":1,"w":"Section 2-706 gives the contract price minus the resale price, plus incidental damages. Six thousand minus five thousand is one thousand, and the two hundred dollars of storage is an incidental cost. Total: twelve hundred dollars.","id":"ucc-seller-m1"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"A car dealer can get as many of a popular model as it has customers for. A buyer signs for one, then backs out. The next week the dealer sells that same car to a walk-in customer at the same price. The dealer's profit on each sale is $3,000. What is the dealer's best measure of damages?","o":["Nothing, because the resale price equalled the contract price.","The full contract price of the car.","Nothing, because the market price equalled the contract price.","Its $3,000 lost profit, plus incidental costs."],"a":3,"w":"This is a lost-volume seller, as in Neri and Retail Marine. The walk-in customer would have bought a car anyway, so the breach cost the dealer one sale. Section 2-708, subsection two, awards the lost profit when the market formula is inadequate.","id":"ucc-seller-m2"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"A trophy maker engraves 200 trophies with a company's name and logo, exactly as ordered. The company then refuses to take them. Nobody else has any use for the trophies. What may the trophy maker recover?","o":["The full contract price, because the goods cannot reasonably be resold.","Only the contract price minus the scrap value of the metal, never the price.","Nothing, until it actually finds another buyer.","Only its lost profit, because the goods were never accepted."],"a":0,"w":"Section 2-709 allows an action for the price in three cases: goods accepted, goods lost after the risk passed, and goods the seller cannot resell at a reasonable price after reasonable effort. Custom goods are the classic third case. Remember A L U.","id":"ucc-seller-m3"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"both","q":"A contract for $2,000 of garden furniture says that if the buyer cancels, the buyer must pay $50,000 as \"agreed damages.\" The buyer cancels, and the seller's real loss is about $300. Is the clause enforceable?","o":["Yes, because the buyer agreed to it.","Yes, because the UCC enforces every liquidated damages clause.","No, because it is unreasonably large, and so void as a penalty, under the UCC just as at common law.","No, because the UCC forbids all liquidated damages clauses."],"a":2,"w":"Section 2-718 allows liquidated damages only at an amount that is reasonable in light of the anticipated or actual harm. A term fixing unreasonably large damages is void as a penalty. The common law draws the same line between a fair forecast and a punishment.","id":"ucc-seller-m4"},{"t":"UCC Remedies","k":"ucc","q":"Which seller's remedy is the mirror image of the buyer's right to \"cover\"?","o":["The action for the price under §2-709.","Resale under §2-706.","Liquidated damages under §2-718.","Stopping delivery under §2-703."],"a":1,"w":"A buyer covers by buying substitute goods and claiming the cover price minus the contract price. A seller does the reverse under section 2-706: resell the goods and claim the contract price minus the resale price. Seven-oh-six: resale is the fix.","id":"ucc-seller-m5"},{"t":"Perfect Tender","k":"ucc","q":"A restaurant orders 50 white tablecloths, to be delivered by June 1. They arrive on May 20, and 10 of them are cream. The restaurant rejects the cream ones. The seller immediately says it will deliver 10 white ones by May 28. May the restaurant cancel the contract now?","o":["Yes, because the perfect tender rule lets a buyer cancel for any defect.","Yes, because the seller has had its one chance to perform.","No, because the time for performance has not expired and the seller has given notice that it will cure.","No, because a colour difference can never be a non-conformity."],"a":2,"w":"Section 2-508 gives the seller a right to cure when the contract time has not yet run out, as long as it tells the buyer promptly. The rejection was fine under section 2-601, but the seller gets until June 1 to make a conforming delivery.","id":"ucc-tender-m1"},{"t":"Perfect Tender","k":"both","q":"A builder finishes a house with one trivial deviation from the plans. Separately, a seller makes a single delivery of goods with one trivial deviation from the order. Which statement is correct?","o":["The owner must pay the builder, less damages, but the buyer of goods may reject the delivery.","Both the owner and the buyer may refuse to pay anything.","Both the owner and the buyer must accept and sue for damages.","The owner may refuse to pay, but the buyer of goods must accept."],"a":0,"w":"The common law uses substantial performance, so the builder gets paid minus damages. For goods, section 2-601 sets the perfect tender rule: a failure to conform in any respect lets the buyer reject, subject to the seller's right to cure.","id":"ucc-tender-m2"},{"t":"Perfect Tender","k":"ucc","q":"A café has a contract for 12 monthly deliveries of coffee beans. The March delivery is two per cent short, and the supplier offers to send the missing beans the next day. May the café reject the March delivery?","o":["Yes, because any shortfall breaches the perfect tender rule.","Yes, but only if it also cancels the whole contract.","No, because a buyer may never reject part of an instalment contract.","No, because the shortfall does not substantially impair the instalment's value and it can be cured."],"a":3,"w":"This is an instalment contract, so section 2-612 replaces perfect tender. The buyer may reject an instalment only if the defect substantially impairs its value and cannot be cured. A small shortage that the seller will fix tomorrow does neither.","id":"ucc-tender-m3"},{"t":"Perfect Tender","k":"ucc","q":"A print shop takes delivery of a new press, inspects it, and uses it every day for three months without complaint. It then notices a small scratch on a side panel that has been there since delivery, and tries to send the press back. Can it?","o":["Yes, because the scratch means the tender was never perfect.","No, because it accepted the press, and a minor defect does not substantially impair its value.","Yes, because a buyer can reject at any time before paying in full.","No, because machines are not goods under the UCC."],"a":1,"w":"By using the press after a chance to inspect, the shop accepted it under section 2-606, and acceptance ends the right to reject. Revocation under section 2-608 needs a defect that substantially impairs the value of the goods. A scratch does not.","id":"ucc-tender-m4"},{"t":"Perfect Tender","k":"ucc","q":"After a buyer has accepted goods, who bears the burden of proving that the goods did not conform to the contract?","o":["The seller, who must prove the goods were perfect.","Neither party; the court presumes a breach.","The buyer, who must establish the breach.","The carrier that delivered the goods."],"a":2,"w":"Under section 2-607, acceptance has consequences. The buyer must pay at the contract rate, must notify the seller of any breach within a reasonable time, and carries the burden of proving the defect. Inspect and object, or you accept.","id":"ucc-tender-m5"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"ucc","q":"A court finds that a repossession clause in a contract for the sale of a tractor was unconscionable when the contract was made. Under UCC §2-302, which of these is NOT one of the court's options?","o":["Refuse to enforce the whole contract.","Enforce the rest of the contract without the clause.","Limit the clause so that it does not produce an unconscionable result.","Order the seller to pay punitive damages to the buyer."],"a":3,"w":"Section 2-302 gives the court three choices: refuse the contract, strike the clause, or limit the clause. It is a shield, not a sword. Nothing in it awards punitive damages.","id":"ucc-unconscionability-m1"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"ucc","q":"Under UCC §2-302, who decides whether a clause is unconscionable, and as of what moment?","o":["The judge, looking at the time the contract was made.","The jury, looking at the time of trial.","The judge, looking at the time of the breach.","The jury, looking at the time the contract was made."],"a":0,"w":"Section 2-302 makes unconscionability a matter of law for the court, tested at the time the contract was made. Judge, not jury. Signing day, not later fury. The parties do get to offer evidence of the commercial setting.","id":"ucc-unconscionability-m2"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"ucc","q":"A store sells a generator to a contractor for $4,000, a fair market price, after ordinary negotiation. Six months later a new model comes out and the market price of the old one falls to $1,500. The contractor, who still owes $3,000, says the price is unconscionable. Is it?","o":["Yes, because the price is now more than double the market value.","Yes, because the contractor has less bargaining power than the store.","No, because unconscionability is tested when the contract was made, and the deal was fair then.","No, because the UCC never lets a court review a price term."],"a":2,"w":"Section 2-302 asks whether the term was unconscionable at the time it was made. A market that moves afterwards does not turn a fair deal into an unconscionable one. Note that courts can review price terms, as Frostifresh shows.","id":"ucc-unconscionability-m3"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"both","q":"How does UCC §2-302 relate to the common-law rule in Restatement §208?","o":["§2-302 applies to every contract, and §208 applies only to goods.","They give courts essentially the same power; §2-302 covers sales of goods and §208 covers other contracts.","§208 lets a court refuse enforcement, but §2-302 only allows damages.","They conflict, so a court must choose whichever favours the seller."],"a":1,"w":"The two rules are twins. Section 2-302 governs sales of goods, and Restatement section 208, which was modelled on it, does the same job for other contracts. Both let a court refuse or limit an unconscionable term.","id":"ucc-unconscionability-m4"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"ucc","q":"A salesman rushes a buyer through a sofa contract, covering the page with his hand and saying, \"That's just the usual stuff.\" The hidden text is in tiny print. Which kind of unconscionability do these facts mainly show?","o":["Procedural, because the problem lies in how the bargain was made.","Substantive, because the terms are hidden.","Neither, because the buyer signed the contract.","Substantive, because sofas are consumer goods."],"a":0,"w":"Procedural unconscionability is about an unfair process: hidden terms, pressure, and no real choice. Substantive unconscionability is about the terms themselves being harsh. Remember P S, and that most courts look for some of each under section 2-302.","id":"ucc-unconscionability-m5"},{"t":"Warranties","k":"ucc","q":"A camping store's label on a tent reads \"Waterproof in heavy rain.\" The salesperson adds, \"This is the finest tent money can buy.\" The tent leaks in the first storm. Which statement created an express warranty?","o":["Both statements.","Only \"Waterproof in heavy rain.\"","Only \"the finest tent money can buy.\"","Neither, because the word \"warranty\" was never used."],"a":1,"w":"Under section 2-313, an affirmation of fact or a description that is part of the basis of the bargain is an express warranty, and no magic words are needed. Calling it the finest tent money can buy is opinion, or puffery, which is not a warranty.","id":"ucc-warranty-m1"},{"t":"Warranties","k":"ucc","q":"A painter tells a hardware store clerk, \"I need paint for a metal boat hull that sits in salt water. What should I use?\" The clerk selects an ordinary house paint, which is perfectly good on walls but peels off the hull in a week. The painter's best claim is breach of:","o":["The warranty of title.","The implied warranty of merchantability only.","An express warranty created by puffery.","The implied warranty of fitness for a particular purpose."],"a":3,"w":"Section 2-315 applies when the seller has reason to know the buyer's particular purpose and that the buyer is relying on the seller's skill to select the goods. The paint may be merchantable as house paint, yet still unfit for the hull. Three-fifteen: tell the seller what you mean.","id":"ucc-warranty-m2"},{"t":"Warranties","k":"ucc","q":"A dentist sells her used family blender to a neighbour and says nothing about its condition. It stops working after one day. Is there a breach of the implied warranty of merchantability?","o":["No, because the warranty arises only when the seller is a merchant in goods of that kind.","Yes, because all goods must be fit for their ordinary purpose.","Yes, because the dentist is a professional.","No, because blenders are not goods."],"a":0,"w":"The implied warranty of merchantability in section 2-314 arises only if the seller is a merchant with respect to goods of that kind. A dentist making a one-off sale of a blender is not a merchant in blenders.","id":"ucc-warranty-m3"},{"t":"Warranties","k":"ucc","q":"A dealer's written sales contract contains, in the same small print as everything else on the back page, the sentence: \"Seller disclaims all implied warranties.\" Is the implied warranty of merchantability effectively disclaimed?","o":["Yes, because any written disclaimer works.","Yes, because the buyer signed the contract.","No, because a written disclaimer of merchantability must mention merchantability and be conspicuous.","No, because implied warranties can never be disclaimed."],"a":2,"w":"Section 2-316 says a disclaimer of merchantability must mention the word merchantability, and if it is in writing it must be conspicuous. This one does neither, and it is not an as is clause. Three-sixteen: disclaimers must be seen.","id":"ucc-warranty-m4"},{"t":"Warranties","k":"both","q":"Which statement best contrasts the old common-law attitude to the quality of goods with the UCC's approach?","o":["The common law implied broad warranties; the UCC abolished them.","The common law followed caveat emptor; the UCC adds implied warranties to sales unless they are properly disclaimed.","Both leave quality entirely to what the seller chooses to promise.","The UCC applies caveat emptor to merchants and implied warranties to everyone else."],"a":1,"w":"Caveat emptor means let the buyer beware: no promise about quality unless the seller made one. The UCC reverses the starting point. Sections 2-314 and 2-315 add implied warranties by law, and section 2-316 sets the rules a seller must follow to disclaim them.","id":"ucc-warranty-m5"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"cl","q":"Which of these is the best example of PROCEDURAL unconscionability?","o":["A price three times the market value.","A harsh clause in tiny print, never explained to a signer who could not read the language of the contract.","A clause that lets only one side cancel.","A late fee ten times larger than any real loss."],"a":1,"w":"Procedural unconscionability is about how the deal was made: hidden terms, no real choice, no real understanding. The other three options are about what the terms say, which is substantive. Sneaky how, nasty what.","id":"unconscionability-m1"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"both","q":"A door-to-door seller sells a sound system that cost him $300 to a couple for $1,500 on credit. The sales talk was in Portuguese, the only language the couple reads. The contract was in English and was never translated. The seller sues for the full price. What is the couple's best argument?","o":["The contract is void because consumer sales must be in the buyer's language.","The seller made a mutual mistake about the price.","The price term is unconscionable, so under UCC §2-302 the court may refuse to enforce it.","The seller gave no consideration."],"a":2,"w":"This is the freezer case, Frostifresh and Reynoso, in new clothes. A sound system is goods, so the rule is UCC section 2-302, the twin of Restatement section 208. An excessive price plus a language barrier gives both substantive and procedural unfairness.","id":"unconscionability-m2"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"cl","q":"Two large companies, each advised by lawyers, negotiate a service contract for months. A year later one of them realises it agreed to pay about 15% more than the going rate. Is the contract unconscionable?","o":["No. A hard bargain between sophisticated parties with real choice is still enforceable.","Yes, because any price above the market rate is substantively unfair.","Yes, because the company would not have signed if it had known.","No, because unconscionability applies only to sales of goods."],"a":0,"w":"A bad deal is not an unconscionable deal. There was no unfair process here, and fifteen percent over market does not shock the conscience. Section 208 is a safety valve, not a refund counter.","id":"unconscionability-m3"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"cl","q":"Under Restatement §208, as of what moment does a court ask whether a contract or term is unconscionable?","o":["When the lawsuit is filed.","When the breach happens.","When the judge hears the case.","When the contract was made."],"a":3,"w":"Section 208 asks whether the term was unconscionable at the time the contract was made. The court does not use hindsight. A fair deal that later turns out badly is just a deal that turned out badly.","id":"unconscionability-m4"},{"t":"Unconscionability","k":"cl","q":"A court finds that one clause in an otherwise fair contract is unconscionable. Under Restatement §208, which of these may the court do?","o":["Nothing. A signed contract must be enforced as written.","Enforce the rest of the contract without the unconscionable clause.","Award punitive damages against the party who drafted the clause.","Rewrite the entire contract on terms the judge thinks are fair."],"a":1,"w":"Section 208 gives the court three tools. It may refuse to enforce the whole contract, enforce the rest without the bad term, or limit the term so the result is not unconscionable. Refuse, strike, or limit.","id":"unconscionability-m5"},{"t":"Undue Influence","k":"cl","q":"Under Restatement section 177, undue influence is best described as:","o":["An improper threat that leaves the victim no reasonable alternative.","Unfair persuasion of a person who is dominated by the persuader or justified in trusting them.","A false statement of fact that the victim relies on.","A contract term so one-sided that it shocks the conscience."],"a":1,"w":"Section 177 has two ingredients: unfair persuasion, and a weak spot of domination or trust. Picture one seven seven: one tired person with two sevens leaning over him. The first option is duress, which is a different defence.","id":"undue-m1"},{"t":"Undue Influence","k":"cl","q":"An elderly, fully competent widow relies on her financial adviser for every money decision. The adviser persuades her to sell him her lake cabin for a third of its value. She took no outside advice. Her family sues to undo the sale. What is the likely analysis?","o":["The sale stands, because she was mentally competent.","The sale stands, because the adviser made no threat.","The sale is void, because advisers may never buy from clients.","A presumption of undue influence arises, and the adviser must show the deal was fair."],"a":3,"w":"This is In re Estate of Burren. When a fiduciary benefits from a deal with the person who trusts him, undue influence is presumed and the fiduciary must rebut it. Competence does not save the deal, and no threat is needed.","id":"undue-m2"},{"t":"Undue Influence","k":"cl","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the warning signs of over-persuasion listed in Odorizzi v. Bloomfield School District?","o":["The agreement was put in writing.","Several persuaders worked on one person.","The deal was discussed at an unusual time.","The person was told there was no time to consult a lawyer."],"a":0,"w":"Run through mean tip. Multiple persuaders, emphasis on delay, advisers absent, no time for a lawyer, unusual time, insistence on finishing now, unusual place. Putting a deal in writing is not on the list.","id":"undue-m3"},{"t":"Undue Influence","k":"cl","q":"At 11 p.m. on the night of an accident, two insurance adjusters visit the injured driver in his hospital room. They say the offer expires tonight and there is no point calling a lawyer. Groggy, he signs a release for a small sum. No threat is made. What is his best defence?","o":["Duress, because he was in hospital.","Lack of capacity, because all patients lack capacity.","Undue influence, because of the over-persuasion of a weakened person.","None, because he signed voluntarily."],"a":2,"w":"Count the Odorizzi signs: unusual time, unusual place, insistence on finishing at once, multiple persuaders, no advisers, no time for a lawyer. There is no threat, so duress is a poor fit. Undue influence is the natural claim.","id":"undue-m4"},{"t":"Undue Influence","k":"cl","q":"A landlord tells a tenant, \"Sign this new lease at double the rent or I will smash your shop windows tonight.\" The tenant signs. Which defence fits best, and why?","o":["Undue influence, because the landlord used unfair persuasion.","Duress, because the landlord made an improper threat.","Undue influence, because landlords are fiduciaries.","Neither, because the tenant signed the lease."],"a":1,"w":"Duress twists your arm. Undue influence bends your ear. A threat to commit a crime or tort is the classic improper threat for duress. Undue influence is about over-persuasion within domination or trust, with no threat required.","id":"undue-m5"}];
